{[%evp 0,33,28,29,27,18,26,26,52,32,84,74,62,43,42,43,32,27,27,27,27,17,31,-3,0,2,55,40,43,71,77,82,78,46,59,70]} 1. d4 Nf6 (1... d5 2. Nc3 Bf5 {This is an ultra-rare continuation, yet it is the choice of several very strong Grandmasters. It is somewhat outside the scope of this course, yet we have decided to dedicate a small file to it in order to point you in the right direction. By delaying ...Nf6, Black aims to nip White's central counterplay in the bud.} 3. f3 {It is crucial to get right down to business! White prepares e2-e4, aiming to provoke Black to develop the knight to f6.} e6 (3... Nf6 4. g4 Bg6 5. g5 Nh5 (5... Ng8 6. h4 h6 7. e4 e6 8. Nh3 Nc6 9. h5 $1 Bh7 (9... Bxh5 10. Nf4 g6 11. Nxh5 gxh5 12. Rxh5 $14) 10. g6 $1 fxg6 11. Nf4 $18) (5... Nfd7 6. h4 {Refraining from the greedy 6.Nxd5, which enables Black to light the center on fire with 6...e5. Instead, we focus on central and kingside expansion.} h6 7. e4 dxe4 8. fxe4 hxg5 9. Bxg5 c5 10. d5 Qb6 11. Qd3 $2 {Markoja-Richter, corr. 2015. White overwhelmingly dominates in the center, and the b2-pawn is clearly untouchable:} Qxb2 12. Rb1 Qa3 13. Rxb7 $18) 6. e4 dxe4 7. f4 $1 {An incredible idea! White simultaneously threatens f4-f5 and Be2. In order to keep the minor piece alive and preserve the balance, Black must reproduce several impossible computer moves.} e5 $1 (7... e6 8. Be2 $16 {[%csl Rh5]}) (7... c5 8. d5 e6 9. Be2 exd5 10. Bxh5 Bxh5 11. Qxh5 $18) (7... h6 8. f5 Bxf5 9. Qxh5 Qxd4 {Law-Sen, corr. 2005. It is important for White to immediately evacuate the queen from h5. A few accurate moves douse the flames of Black's initiative and secure the extra piece:} 10. Qe2 $1 {[%cal Rc1e3]} Nc6 11. Be3 Qd7 12. Rd1 $18) 8. fxe5 h6 9. Qg4 Qd7 (9... Be7 {Amazingly, this position occurred only once, in 1965! In that game (Kaulich-Baumbach), the Austrian master grabbed the pawn on e4, but immediate development yields better practical chances:} 10. Be3 Bxg5 11. Bxg5 hxg5 (11... Qxg5 12. Qc8+ Qd8 13. Qxd8+ Kxd8 14. Nge2 Nc6 15. Bh3 $16) 12. Nh3 $1 Qxd4 (12... Nc6 13. O-O-O $36) 13. Qc8+ Qd8 14. Qxb7 Nd7 15. Bb5 $16) 10. Qxd7+ Nxd7 11. gxh6 gxh6 12. Nge2 {The endgame is miserable for Black, who suffers from a total lack of coordination and a permanently weak pawn on d4.} c5 13. Be3 cxd4 14. Nxd4 Be7 15. O-O-O Nxe5 16. Bb5+ Kf8 17. Rhg1 $14) 4. e4 dxe4 (4... Bg6 5. h4 $1 {An important inclusion, preventing the check on h4 and expanding on the kingside in typical Jobava fashion.} h6 (5... h5 {This does not fundamentally change the nature of the game.} 6. Bg5 f6 7. Be3 c6 8. Nge2 $14 {The position resembles an improved version of a Fantasy Caro-Kann. White's knight heads to f4, and White will castle queenside after moving the queen either to d2 or even to d3.}) (5... Nf6 $4 6. e5 Nh5 7. g4 Ng3 8. Rh3 Nxf1 9. h5 $1 $18 {An easy move to forget about! Both the knight on f1, as well as the bishop on g6, are trapped! Black cannot save either of them.}) 6. h5 Bh7 7. exd5 exd5 8. Bd3 Bxd3 9. Qxd3 Nf6 (9... Bd6 10. Bd2 c6 11. O-O-O Ne7 12. g4 Qc7 {Boeven-Mueller, German Senior Championship 2015. White opted for the rather odd Nce2, but simple improving moves yield an excellent position, e.g.} 13. Nh3 Nd7 14. Kb1 O-O-O 15. Ne2 {[%cal Rd2c1,Re2f4,Rf4d3] White's aim is to regroup with Bc1, Nhf4, followed by Qd2 and Nd3, controlling key squares in the center. Black is dreadfully cramped.}) 10. Bf4 $14) (4... Bg6) 5. fxe4 Bg6 6. Nf3 Bb4 7. Bd3 Nf6 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bxf6 Qxf6 10. O-O $16 {Jobava London-1...d5 2.Nc3 Bf5  2023 White's central control and prospects along the f-file guarantee us a long-lasting initiative.}) 2. Nc3 d5 3. Bf4 {In this chapter, we briefly consider a few rare alternatives to the popular responses.} c6 {This move does not have too much independent value. Most of the time, Black follows up with 4...Bf5, transposing to a line that we analyze via the 3...Bf5 move order (3...Bf5 4.Nf3 c6). In this file, we give the one legitimate independent variation, in which Black quickly develops their queen to b6 and probes for queenside weaknesses.} (3... c5 {This is the most concrete and combative response to the Jobava London. Black immediately tries to underscore the awkwardness of the knight on c3. In contrast to the (regular) London System, we do not have the possibility of supporting the pawn with c2-c3. In the dark ages, White automatically responded with 4.e3, and after 4...cxd4 5.exd4, we reach a position that resembles an inferior version of an exchange Caro-Kann. However, the presence of the knight on c3 affords us another exciting possibility that has completely revitalized this variation!} 4. e4 $5 {This scintillating counterstrike, which gained notoriety a few short years ago, is incomparably more exciting and challenging than the meek 4.e3. Our idea is straightforward: we aim to exploit our lead in development by blasting open the center. In a way, this move can be thought of as a massively improved Albin Countergambit (1.d4 d5 2.c4 e5). Black is now at a crossroads: three different captures are possible, and all of them must be considered carefully. The resulting positions are complex and full of exciting tactical possibilities.} Nxe4 {By far the most popular recapture at the highest levels. Black captures the pawn and forces a knight trade, opening up an annoying queen check on a5 and eliminating the future possibility of Nc3-b5. Incidentally, it is the least popular of the three captures online, but due to its objective potency, we have decided to promote it as the main line.} (4... dxe4 {Along with 4...cxd4, this is the most popular capture online (with approximately 5,000+ games on Lichess at the time of analysis).} 5. dxc5 {We recapture the pawn on c5, posing Black with an unenviable dilemma. Trading queens hands over control of the d-file, while refraining from the trade with 5...Qa5 gives White a dangerous middlegame initiative. It is only move 5, yet we are already asking Black a serious question!} Qa5 (5... Qxd1+ $6 {This is by far the most popular move online (with 2,000 or so games), but it gives us full control over the d-file and leads to a rather dubious endgame. It is important to realize that the trade of queens hardly diminishes White's attacking potential; our initiative does not vanish simply because the ladies are off the board! A few general pointers to help you understand this position better: 1) The pawn on e4 can be either attacked with Nge2-g3, or traded off directly with f2-f3. Both options are on the table depending on Black's setup. 2) After the recapture, White has the immediate threat of situating a knight on b5, forcing Black to contort themselves with the immensely awkward ...Na6. 3) The pawn on c5 is a strength rather than a weakness: it significantly restricts the mobility of Black's dark-squared bishop, and it can be easily supported both with pawns (b4) and with pieces (Be3).} 6. Rxd1 a6 {Black must stop the knight sortie to b5, but this move comes at the great cost of weakening the b6-square. We target that square without further delay.} (6... Nc6 $4 7. Nb5 {Black pays a steep price for ignoring the threat!} e5 8. Nc7+ Ke7 9. Bxe5 $1 {A cute desperado sacrifice, winning even more material.} Nxe5 10. Nxa8 $18 {The knight is easily evacuated through c7, when White's material advantage will prove decisive.}) (6... Bd7 7. Nb5 Na6 8. b4 {Using tactics to defend the c5-pawn.} g6 9. a3 Bg7 10. Be5 $16 {Black is completely paralyzed. Even castling kingside is not possible due to Bxf6, while castling queenside - well, we will leave the analysis of that move to the reader!}) 7. Na4 {[%cal Ra4b6] Aiming for b6!} (7. a3 $5 {This more restrained move is a sensible alternative. We prepare to construct the typical a3-b4-c5 pawn chain, which will make it rather difficult for Black to operate on the queenside in any meaningful way. Of course, the idea of Na4-b6 is still very much in the cards.} Nc6 8. Bc4 e5 9. Be3 Na5 10. Ba2 Be6 $1 11. Bxe6 fxe6 12. b3 Rc8 13. Na4 $14 {[%csl Re4,Re5,Re6]}) 7... Nbd7 8. b4 e6 (8... e5 {Although it comes with tempo, this move is almost never scary. The bishop calmly retreats, and Black is left with further weaknesess in the center.} 9. Bd2 {It may seem more natural to drop the bishop back to e3, but it is important to reinforce the b4-pawn due to the possibility of ...a6-a5, when a2-a3 fails due to the hanging knight on a4. Therefore, we have to find alternate arrangements for the b4-pawn.} a5 10. Bb5 {Perfectly exploiting the drawback of Black's previous move. Most importantly, this move secures the knight on a4, taking the sting out of the capture on b4.} axb4 11. Bxb4 Be7 12. Nb6 Rb8 (12... Rxa2 13. Nxc8 $18) 13. Ne2 $18 {Total and complete paralysis! The computer's top suggestion is 13...0-0, which tells you all you need to know about this position!}) 9. a3 {Solidifying White's queenside pawn chain, and taking the sting out of ...a5.} Nd5 10. Bd2 b5 $4 {A sad necessity. Otherwise, we send away the knight with c2-c4.} (10... Be7 11. c4 N5f6 12. Ne2 O-O 13. Nec3 $18 {Black cannot makle a single effective move: leaving d7 enables us to jump into b6. Black is simply lost!}) 11. Nc3 $14 {White's well-protected passer on c5 guarantees us a long-lasting advantage in the endgame.}) (5... Bd7 {Declining the queen trade in this way is rather awkward. We respond by quickly getting our knight to g3 and applying immediate pressure on the e4-pawn.} 6. Nge2 Nc6 (6... e5 $5 {An interesting move, trading the e-pawn for the c-pawn, although it does not fully solve Black's problems.} 7. Bxe5 Bxc5 8. Bd6 {Immediately targetting Black's strongest piece.} Na6 9. Bxc5 Nxc5 10. Qd6 {Now, it is the queen's turn to sink her teeth into the weak d6 square! In reality, this is just a fancy of way of inducing a queen trade.} Qe7 11. Qxe7+ Kxe7 12. Ng3 $14 {The computer assigns a modest advantage to White, but in reality, Black's position is very difficult. The e4-pawn is a long-term weakness, and Black is also saddled with problems related to the placement of his king. On e7, it is constantly subject to pressure down the e-file, and Black must also keep a close eye on the knight sorties to d5 and f5. Our next step is to castle queenside, after which the capture on e4 immediately becomes a threat!}) 7. Ng3 Qa5 {Pinning the knight and indirectly defending the e4-pawn.} 8. Qd2 {This is the classic response to ...Qa5, reinforcing the threat of Nxe4.} e5 9. Be3 Ng4 (9... Bxc5 10. Bxc5 Qxc5 11. Ngxe4 {Yet again, the d6-square costs Black and arm and a leg.} Nxe4 12. Nxe4 Qe7 13. Nd6+ Kf8 {Rather than greedily capturing on b7, it is better to focus on active development:} 14. O-O-O Be6 15. f4 $40 {with a powerful and long-lasting attack. Note that ...Rd8 is not scary in the least, as Black has no way to reinforce the pressure on the knight. We can follow up by developing the bishop to e2 and placing a rook on f1.}) 10. Ngxe4 f5 $5 {Black attempts to stir things up on the queenside, but we use the d6-square to gracefully get out of danger.} 11. Nd6+ Bxd6 12. Qxd6 Nxe3 13. fxe3 O-O-O 14. Bc4 $14 {Black has no effective discovery against the queen, since 14...Be8 runs into 15.Be6+. Our next step is to castle queenside.}) (5... Nbd7 {A sensible way to decline the queen trade, although it walks right into a rather nasty pin.} 6. Bb5 {Threatening c5-c6.} a6 (6... e5 {This is the only way to keep Black's position afloat, although it fails to solve all of Black's problems:} 7. Bxe5 Bxc5 8. Bd6 Qb6 9. Bxc5 Qxc5 10. Nge2 O-O 11. Qd4 $1 {The queen trade suits us just fine, since Black will be saddled with the long-term weakness of the e4-pawn.} Qxd4 12. Nxd4 $14) 7. c6 $1 {Anyway!} axb5 8. c7 $18 {A delightful final position. You will almost certainly win a few games like this!}) 6. Qd2 {Preparing to castle queenside! Of course, we could not care less about the c5-pawn: our only allegiance in this line is to rapid development.} Qxc5 $2 {This is automatic at most levels, although after this capture White's initiative becomes virtually decisive.} (6... Nc6 {This is Black's best move, but his problems are only just beginning!} 7. O-O-O e5 $6 (7... a6 {A sensible move, preventing Bb5. Of course, we have a second square that we can develop the bishop to!} 8. Bc4 {It is worth spending an hour or so mastering the ensuing complications. White is better, but the position remains very sharp and it is important to be precise on every move.} e5 {Black opens up a pathway for his dark-squared bishop, but this move comes at the serious cost of (a) weakening the d5-square, and (b) enabling our bishop to seek greener pastures on g5, where it will incessantly pressure Black's knight.} (8... Bf5 9. Nd5 {Initiating a favorable queen trade.} Qxd2+ 10. Rxd2 Nxd5 11. Bxd5 {The bishop on d5 is an absolute menace, so Black's hand is forced:} e6 12. Bxc6+ bxc6 13. Bd6 $14 {with a stable edge in the endgame. We will, as usual, follow up with Ne2-g3, putting significant pressure on the e4-pawn. The rook on h1 will be deployed to d1, lending further support to the d6-bishop.}) (8... Qxc5 9. Nd5 $1 {Of course, the threat of Nc7# renders the bishop untouchable.} Nxd5 (9... Qxc4 10. Nc7#) 10. Bxd5 {White threatens to capture on c6 and deliver mate on d8, forcing Black's hand.} Bg4 11. Be3 $1 {A cute little intermezzo, forcing Black's queen onto a more awkward square.} Qd6 12. f3 {Here, it is important to realize that we are not in the business of winning the e4-pawn: we have much bigger fish to fry! As such, we use the e4-pawn as fuel to accelerate our development.} exf3 13. Nxf3 {From a practical standpoint, White's initiative is decisive, e.g.} e6 14. Bxc6+ Qxc6 15. Ne5 $1 $18 {[%csl Rc6,Rg4] Black has to stay pegged to the d7-square, so the bishop is lost.}) 9. Bg5 Bg4 (9... Bxc5 10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. Qh6 $1 {Black's position collapses due to the twin threats of Nxe4 and Qxf6.}) 10. f3 {Yet again, we use the e4-pawn to supercharge the development of our kingside.} Rd8 11. Nd5 {And now, we stick the knight on d5 in order to force a transition into a favorable endgame, in which our initiative rages on.} Qxd2+ 12. Rxd2 exf3 (12... Nxd5 13. fxg4 $14) 13. gxf3 {The position remains complicated and unclear, but there is no question that it is more fun to handle the White pieces. Indeed, Black must continue to operate with filigree, computer-like precision to avoid losing the game on the spot.} Bf5 14. Nxf6+ gxf6 15. Rxd8+ Nxd8 16. Bxf6 Rg8 17. Bxe5 Rg2 18. Ne2 $14 {Black has insufficient compensation for the pawn, practically speaking. White has many ways to improve the position and ultimately smoke the rook out of g2. For instance, we could follow up with Kc1-d1, Be5-g3, and Bc4-b3, when we will threaten to simply trap the rook with Nf4.}) (7... Bg4 $6 8. Nb5 $1 {[%cal Rb5c7] Forcing a favorable queen trade due to the threat of Nc7.} Qxd2+ 9. Rxd2 e5 10. Nc7+ Ke7 {Here, the main thing is not to take the rook, because Black will take the bishop and we will have a very hard time retrieving our knight. Instead, we calmly move the bishop to a more active spot:} 11. Bg5 Rd8 12. Nd5+ Rxd5 (12... Ke8 13. Nxf6+ gxf6 14. Rxd8+ Nxd8 15. Bxf6 Rg8 16. Bxe5 Bxc5 17. Bg3 $16 {[%cal Rf2f3]}) 13. Rxd5 $14 {Black has clearly insufficient compensation for the exchange.}) 8. Nb5 $1 Qxd2+ (8... Qxa2 $4 9. Nc7+ Ke7 10. Qd6#) 9. Bxd2 Rb8 10. Be3 Ng4 11. Nc7+ Ke7 12. Nd5+ Ke8 13. b4 $36 f5 14. Bc4 f4 15. h3 $1 fxe3 (15... Nf6 16. Nc7+ Ke7 17. Bd2) 16. hxg4 exf2 17. Nh3 Bxg4 18. Nc7+ Ke7 19. Rdf1 e3 20. Nd5+ Kd7 21. c3 $16) 7. O-O-O {Threatening mate on d8!} Bd7 (7... Nc6 $4 {As usual, this loses on the spot:} 8. Nb5 $18) 8. Nge2 {Black is already in serious trouble, and must play like an engine to avoid losing on the spot. We will give you a few sample lines to illustrate the potency of White's attack.} Nc6 $4 {This automatic developing move loses immediately, although Black's position is already quite dire.} (8... e5 $2 {Yet again, this move creates far too many weaknesses in the center, and sends our bishop to where it wants to go anyway!} 9. Bg5 {[%csl Re4][%cal Rg5f6,Rc3e4]} Be7 (9... Qxf2 {Pawn-grabbing does not go unpunished:} 10. Nb5 $1 {[%cal Rb5c7]} Qb6 11. Nec3 {The other knight joins in the party. White now has the utterly unstoppable threat of Bxf6 followed by Nd5, when one of the horsies will infiltrate c7 or f6. For instance:} a6 12. Bxf6 axb5 (12... e3 13. Qd3 axb5 14. Bxe5 $18) 13. Bxb5 $1 {More tactics, this time highlighting our total dominance over the d-file.} Bxb5 14. Qd8+ Qxd8 15. Rxd8#) 10. Bxf6 gxf6 11. Nxe4 Qc7 12. N2c3 $18 {[%cal Rc3d5] Black is busted: the knight heads to d5, and we can follow up with Bc4. Black cannot move a muscle.}) (8... Qa5 {This difficult move is the only way to maintain practical chances. We now shuffle our knight around to b3, opening up the diagonal for our light-squared bishop and cementing our advantage.} 9. Nd4 Nc6 10. Nb3 $1 {No trade! The knight on b3 is a major asset, since it can hop into c5 at a moment's notice.} Qf5 11. Be3 {[%cal Rc3b5,Rb3c5,Rb5c7,Rc5b7,Rc5d7] Posing the twin threats of Nc5 and Nb5.} Qe5 12. h3 $1 $16 {[%cal Re3f4,Rg2g4] Cold-blooded prophylaxis! By covering the g4-square, we prevent any shenanigans related to ...Bg4 and ensure that our rook will stay attached to the d1-square. We also prepare a devious idea:} e6 13. Bf4 Qf5 14. g4 {The second point of our 12th move! The queen is banished to g6, where she will merely passively spectate the affairs in the center.} Qg6 15. Nb5 $18 {[%cal Rb5c7] The threat of Nc7 is simply unstoppable.}) 9. Nxe4 $1 {[%cal Rd2d7,Re4f6,Re4c5] The knight on e4 is untouchable due to checkmate on d7!} Qf5 10. Nxf6+ exf6 11. Ng3 Qe6 12. Bc4 {Time to dig Black a grave! The queen is tossed around like a small boat in the raging ocean.} Qe7 13. Ne4 $1 Ne5 14. Bxe5 fxe5 15. Ng5 $18 {[%csl Rf7][%cal Rc4f7,Rc4b5,Rd2d7] We could fill an entire separate course listing all of White's threats in this position.}) (4... cxd4 {This move trails the capture on e4 in popularity online, but only by a little bit. Our queen is now called to duty.} 5. Qxd4 Nc6 6. Bb5 {This is our main recommendation, bringing out the bishop and pinning the knight. It is worth spending some time on the alternative 6.Qa4, which is an incredibly dangerous (if objectively dubious) line that will bring you rapid victory if Black is not prepared.} (6. Qa4 $6 {Objectively, this is a terrible move, but in our experience, even Grandmasters don't know how to react. A single mistake can change the evaluation to White's favor, so it is more than reasonable to keep this move in your back pocket. The idea is twofold: the queen on a4 supports a rapid queenside attack with Nb5, and the d-file is cleared so that the rook will exert more influence when it reaches d1. In order to prove an advantage (and avoid losing the game), Black must generate a string of computer moves that have proven too difficult to find even for top GMs.} d4 {A very difficult move to play, as this sends the knight to b5.} (6... dxe4 $5 {This is as good as 6...d4 objectively, but from a practical perspective, the ensuing position is virtually unplayable for Black.} 7. Nb5 e5 8. Bxe5 $1 {Justifying the placement of the queen!} Nxe5 $6 {The most straightforward response, calling White's bluff.} (8... Bb4+ {This is the most popular move, but it immediately digs Black into a deep hole.} 9. c3 {Covering the check and attacking the bishop.} O-O $6 (9... Ba5 $2 10. Qa3 $1 $18 {[%cal Ra3f8,Rb5d6,Re5d6,Ra1d1] White is winning due to the unstoppable threat of Nd6+, e.g.} Nxe5 11. Nd6+ Kf8 12. Nxc8+ Kg8 13. Ne7+ Kf8 14. Nc6+ $18) 10. Bc7 $1 {An important intermezzo, moving the bishop onto a defended square with tempo.} Qe8 (10... Qd5 {This is moving more human} 11. Rd1 Qg5 12. cxb4 $18) 11. cxb4 a6 {White is up a piece, but our lack of development makes it difficult for us to untangle. It is crucial to follow up with several accurate moves:} 12. Bd6 {Exploiting the fact that b5 is untouchable due to Qxa8.} Nd4 13. Bxf8 Bg4 $1 {This cold-blooded developing move is incredibly hard to play, yet it is the only move that keeps Black in the game.} 14. Bc5 axb5 {The queen is trapped, but we are not out of tricks!} 15. Bxb5 $1 {Sacrificing the bishop in order to generate an escape square for our queen!} Nxb5 (15... Qd8 16. Rd1 $1 Rxa4 17. Rxd4 Qa8 18. Bxa4 Qxa4 19. Rd8+ Ne8 20. h3 Bh5 21. g4 Bg6 22. Ne2 $18 {White has an overwhelming amount of material for the queen, as well as massive pressure along the eighth rank.}) 16. Qb3 $16 {The queen has escaped, and Black has clearly insufficient compensation for the exchange. We will follow up by chasing the bishop away with h3, and trying to get our knight out and castle as quickly as possible.}) (8... Bc5 $1 {This is the only way to prove an advantage! Black ignores the threat of Nc7, instead focusing on setting up a sacrifice on f2. If your opponent uncorks this move, then it is time to take off your hat!} 9. Nc7+ Kf8 10. Bg3 Bxf2+ $3 {Another insane only move, deflecting the bishop from its protection of the knight on c7. This is what we are talking about when we say that Qa4 is a good move from a practical standpoint!} (10... Rb8 {This is a far more human move, and it hands over a massive initiative:} 11. Bb5 Bd7 12. O-O-O $36 {White's lead in development and the nasty possibility of a discovered attack against Black's rooks gives White a long-lasting initiative.}) 11. Bxf2 (11. Kxf2 {Unfortunately, this fails to a string of wild tactical shots:} e3+ $1 12. Kxe3 Qe7+ $1 13. Kd2 Ne4+ 14. Kd1 (14. Qxe4 Qxe4 15. Re1 Qd4+ $19 16. Kc1 Bf5 17. Nxa8 g5 $1 18. Nf3 Qa4 {[%cal Ra4c2,Ra4a2]} 19. Bd3 Bxd3 20. cxd3 Nb4 $19 {[%cal Ra4c2,Rf8g7,Rh8a8,Ra4a2]}) 14... Bg4+ 15. Nf3 Nxg3 16. hxg3 Bxf3+ 17. gxf3 Qxc7 $17) 11... Qxc7 $17 {Unfortunately, our tricks have come to an end, and White does not have sufficient compensation for the pawn. Still, the compromised position of Black's king means that White has excellent practical chances after 12.0-0-0.}) 9. Nc7+ Ke7 10. Nxa8 {We grab the rook and we WILL live to tell the tale!} Qd6 11. Qxa7 {The queen rushes to the support of the knight. We will neutralize the check on b4 with a series of checks aimed at forcing the queens off the board.} Qb4+ 12. c3 Qxb2 13. Qc5+ {Forcing the king onto a more vulnerable square.} Ke6 14. Qb6+ Qxb6 15. Nxb6 $14 {The smoke has cleared, and Black has insufficient compensation for the exchange.}) (6... Nxe4 {This leads directly to a position in which White has overwhelming practical compensation for the pawn.} 7. Nxe4 dxe4 8. Bb5 f6 {It is rather hard to believe that a human can find this move, although it is one of the only ways that Black can stay alive!} (8... Qb6 $5 9. Be3 Qc7 10. Ne2 a6 11. Nc3 Be6 12. Nxe4 $5 Rc8 13. Be2 Qa5+ 14. Qxa5 Nxa5 15. b3 $1 $14 {with a long-lasting edge in the endgame. The c2-pawn is untouchable due to an inventive tactical idea:} Rxc2 16. Bd2 Nc6 17. Bd3 {The rook is trapped!} Nd4 18. Bc3 $1 {Another blow, forcing Black to give up the exchange or lose his knight.}) 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. Qxc6+ Bd7 11. Qxe4 {Cashing in our chips!} e5 {Black has definite compensation due to the bishop pair, but I definitely prefer White. Note that Black does not threaten to capture on f4, so we can proceed with calm development.} 12. Nf3 Be6 13. O-O Qd5 14. Qxd5 Bxd5 15. Be3 Kf7 16. Nd2 $14 {Preparing c2-c4. We will follow up by advancing the pawn to c5, and supporting further queenside expansion with b2-b4.}) 7. Nb5 {[%cal Rb5c7,Rf4c7] Threatening Nc7+. Black's response is absolutely forced.} e5 8. Bxe5 {The crux of White's idea. The knight cannot leave c6 due to the discovered double check on c7, which will pick up the rook on a8.} Bb4+ (8... Nxe5 $4 9. Nc7+ Ke7 10. Nxa8 Bd7 (10... Nxe4 11. O-O-O $18) 11. Qxd4 Nc6 12. Qd2 Qxa8 13. O-O-O $18 {Black has recovered the knight, but is still dead lost. We are threaten to run Black over in the center with e4-e5.}) 9. c3 dxc3 10. Bxc3 O-O $3 {It is incredibly difficult to find this move, which leaves the bishop en prise!} (10... Bxc3+ 11. Nxc3 $14 {Black has virtually no compensation for the pawn.}) 11. Bxb4 Re8 $1 12. f3 Nxe4 $3 {Another insane tactical blow, opening up a pathway for the queen to h4. Hopefully, you are beginning to see the practical appeal of 6.Qa4!} 13. Be2 $8 Qh4+ 14. g3 Nxg3 15. hxg3 Qxh1 16. Bc5 $13 {The computer indicates that Black is somewhat better, although I would be rather terrified to handle the Black pieces here! White intends to castle kingside, when the straightforward threat of Nc7 will cause major problems.}) 6... Bd7 {This is almost automatic at all levels, although 6...a6 is a serious alternative that must be studied carefully.} (6... a6 7. Bxc6+ bxc6 8. Nf3 {The main thing to understand about this position is that the prospect of ...c6-c5 is not scary at all: our queen has plenty of spots to choose from, and ...c5 will seriously weaken Black's control over the d5-pawn. In fact, our next move will show that we actually want to induce ...c5!} e6 (8... c5 $5 {A shrewd move, setting a vicious trap: taking the pawn leads to the loss of a minor piece after ...e5. Instead, we ferry the queen over to b3.} 9. Qa4+ Bd7 10. Qb3 d4 (10... dxe4 {Grabbing the pawn hands over a massive initiative to White:} 11. Ng5 e6 12. O-O-O $1 $16 {We will recover the pawn on the next move. Black's position is in shambles.}) 11. Nd5 {Threatening the fork on c7, and forcing Black's hand.} Nxd5 12. exd5 {The pawn on d5 is a major problem for Black: it greatly restricts Black's mobility. Trading it off, however, opens up an even nastier can of worms down the e-file.} e6 13. O-O Be7 14. c3 $36 {Chipping away at Black's pawn chain. Our pieces are far more active, and Black faces a host of practical problems, e.g.} exd5 15. cxd4 c4 16. Qb7 $1 Be6 17. Qc6+ $1 {The queen is a hero! We force Black's king to lose castling rights, since blocking with the bishop loses the d5-pawn and blocking with the queen drops the rook in the corner.} Kf8 18. b3 $14) 9. Ne5 $5 {Forcing Black's pawn forward.} c5 {Black's pawns look imposing, but they are no match for White's well-developed army. With our next move, we force liquidation into a favorable endgame.} (9... Bd7 10. Nd3 $5 {[%cal Rd3c5,Rd3e5] Switching gears, from tactical to positional! By controlling c5, we are preventing the annoying prospect of ...c6-c5 and setting up a long-lasting blockade on the dark squares. Our space advantage and more active pieces give us a pleasant game.} Qa5 {[%cal Rc6c5] The most reasonable move, reinforcing the threat of ...c5. Once again, we do not fear this move, but rather encourage it!} (10... Be7 11. e5 {Things go from bad to worse, since Black's knight has nowhere good to go!} Ng8 12. Na4 $16 {Increasing the blockade on the c5-square.}) 11. O-O c5 12. Qe5 c4 {In this position, we uncork a series of absolutely stunning tactical blows, one after the other.} 13. b4 $1 {We start with a counterblow against Black's queen, drawing Black's bishop out of its initial square.} Bxb4 $4 (13... Qb6 {This is the only way for Black to stay in the game, although the situation remains extremely critical.} 14. Nc5 {Note the tertiary use of the pawn on c5: in the first place it is a decoy, in the second place...also a decoy, but in the third place, it offers the knight a temporary support!} Qxb4 $8 15. Nxd5 {Now, the second knight jumps into the game!} Nxd5 (15... Qxc5 16. Nc7+ $18) 16. Nxe6 $5 $40 {An incredible move, desperado-sacrificing the knight in order to shatter Black's center. The resulting position is virtually unplayable from a practical point of view.} (16. Nxd7 Nxf4 17. Qxf4 Qd6 18. Qxd6 Bxd6 19. Nb6 Rb8 20. Nxc4 $11) 16... fxe6 {Obviously, 16...Bxe6 17.exd5 drops the bishop, so this is a sad necessity.} 17. exd5 Be7 18. Qxg7 Rf8 19. dxe6 {White has now amassed three pawns for the piece!} Bc6 (19... Bxe6 20. Rae1 $40 {with a raging attack down the e-file. A single mistake loses the game for Black, e.g.} Rf6 21. Bg5 {Even better than 21.Rxe6.} Rg6 22. Qxh7 $1 Rxg5 23. Rxe6 Kd8 24. Rd1+ Ke8 25. h4 Rg4 26. Qh5+ $18) 20. Rad1 {Another incredible move, cutting off Black's ability to castle queenside and escape the hell that awaits him in the center! Amazingly, this is worth sacrificing another bishop for!} Rxf4 21. g3 Rf8 22. Qxh7 $40 {A rather sad product for Black, keeping in mind that he has made it this far! White threatens Qg6, and after the correct 22...Bd6 (the only move), we have a draw in our pocket with Qg6-h7. But we can certainly keep things going, e.g.} Bd6 23. Qg6+ Ke7 24. Qg7+ Ke8 25. e7 $5 Rf7 (25... Bxe7 26. Qg6+ $18 {[%cal Rg6c6,Rg6e8] White picks up both the bishop and the rook!}) 26. Qg6 Kxe7 27. a3 {Deflecting the queen onto a more vulnerable spot.} Qc5 28. Rxd6 $1 Qxd6 29. Re1+ Kd7 30. Qxf7+ $40 {White has recovered one of the minor pieces, and the attack rages on. We can follow up by capturing on c4, when we will have four pawns for the piece and long-term chances against Black's king.}) (13... cxb3 14. axb3 $18) 14. Nxb4 Qxb4 {It may appear that Black has finally doused the flames of White's initiative, but the party is only begining. White uncorks an absolutely jaw-dropping sequence, highlighting Black's total inability to keep all of the weak points in his position defended.} 15. Bh6 $3 {We start by undermining the g7-pawn in stylish fashion. You can now see the hidden role that the bishop was playing on f8: it was defending the g7-pawn!} gxh6 16. Nxd5 $3 {And now it is time for part II, highlighting the undefended rook in the corner. Black's position collapses. This move is infinitely better than the limp 16.Qxf6.} Nxd5 17. Qxh8+ Qf8 18. Qxf8+ Kxf8 19. exd5 $18 {White emerges from the complications an exchange ahead.}) 10. Qa4+ Bd7 11. Nxd7 Qxd7 {Black is forced to acquiesce to the endgame, since recapturing with the knight drops the pawn on d5.} 12. exd5 {Trading on our own terms. The immediate queen trade lets Black off the hook.} Qxa4 (12... exd5 13. O-O-O Qxa4 14. Nxa4 {Black's center collapses, e.g.} Be7 15. Nb6 Rd8 16. Bc7 $18) 13. Nxa4 {Of course, the knight on a4 is a great asset, since it threatens to jump to b6, targeting Black's center and Black's rook simultaneously. For this reason, Black has no choice but to recapture on d5 with the knight, permanently crippling his queenside and giving White a long-lasting advantage.} Nxd5 (13... exd5 14. Nb6 Ra7 15. O-O-O d4 16. c3 dxc3 17. Rhe1+ Be7 18. Nc8 $18 {winning a ton of material.}) 14. Bd2 c4 (14... Be7 15. O-O-O O-O $2 16. c4 $16) 15. O-O-O f5 $1 {[%csl Ra6,Rc4,Re6,Rf5,Rh7]} 16. Nc3 $14 {Black suffers from a bad pawn structure. White's play is very straightforward: we will bring the rook on e1 and exert tremendous pressure on Black's center.}) 7. Bxc6 Bxc6 (7... bxc6 $6 {Taking with the pawn is rather senseless, as it loses a pawn for virtually no compensation and enables White to open up the entire center.} 8. exd5 cxd5 9. Nxd5 Qa5+ 10. Nc3 {An important resource, covering the check with the knight. Note that 10...e5 is ineffective, since the square is defended twice and only attacked once.} e6 (10... Rd8 11. O-O-O $16 {Black has no effective discovery, since we can simply take on d8 wherever Black's bishop goes.}) 11. O-O-O {Getting the king to safety and increasing the pressure down the d-file. Black is forced to get his own king to safety as quickly as possible.} Bc5 12. Qe5 O-O 13. Bh6 $1 $16 {We saw a similar move in the 6...a6 line. The point of this move is to undermine the g7-pawn, creating the massive threat of Bxg7 Kxg7 Qg5+. Black's only defense is to move the bishop away from c5, leading to a queen trade and an endgame in which Black's compensation for the pawn is nonexistant.}) 8. e5 {Now that the threat against our queen has been eliminated, we focus on asserting our dominance in the center. The knight has two reasonable retreating squares: e4 and d7. White's idea is one and the same.} Nd7 (8... Ne4 9. e6 $1 {We will explain this move in greater depth in the main line (8...Ne4). Here, the idea is much the same: we permanently disfigure Black's pawn structure and open up a direct pathway to e5 for one of our pieces.} fxe6 10. Nge2 Qb6 11. Nxe4 Qxd4 (11... dxe4 12. Qc3 Rc8 13. O-O $16 {Black's pawns are tripled, and he has no easy way to develop the f8-bishop.}) 12. Nxd4 dxe4 13. Nxc6 bxc6 14. O-O-O $18 {Black's pawn structure says it all!}) 9. e6 $1 {The crux of White's entire strategy, this thematic pawn sacrifice freezes Black's kingside development and opens up the e5-square for our knight. Importantly, we will not try to win the pawn back straightaway: instead, we focus on quickly developing our kingside. We must maintain our control over the e5-square at all costs.} fxe6 10. Nf3 Qb6 {Here, there are three interesting alternatives, all of which promise extremely good practical play.} 11. Qd2 {This is the most dangerous of the three options, leaving the queens on the board and forcing Black to find a series of accurate moves in order to stay alive. We will give the other two alternatives (11.Be5 and 11.Be3) without commentary, but they are definitely worth looking into in greater depth.} (11. Be5 Qxd4 12. Bxd4 Rg8 13. h4 g5 $1 14. hxg5 e5 15. Bxe5 Nxe5 16. Nxe5 Rxg5 17. Nxc6 bxc6 18. g3 $11) (11. Be3 Qxb2 12. Nxd5 Qxd4 13. Nc7+ Kf7 14. Nxd4 Bxg2 15. Rg1 Rc8 16. Ndxe6 $13) 11... d4 $1 {Black must respond actively; otherwise he is entirely paralyzed. This is an incredibly difficulft move to play, however, since it involves giving away the pride of Black's position.} (11... g6 $6 {This is likelier to be chosen by someone unfamiliar with this line. Black rushes to complete his kingside development.} 12. O-O-O {In the ...c5 line, we almost always castle queenside! With Black's pieces as passive as they are, there is little to no threat to White's monarch on the queenside.} Bg7 13. Rhe1 O-O (13... O-O-O $2 {Ignoring the threat on e6 by going the other way does not serve Black well:} 14. Rxe6 Bf6 $2 (14... Rhe8 15. Bg5 {[%cal Rg5e7,Re6e7] Reinforcing the threat on e7.} Qb4 16. a3 {It is important to chase the queen away from b4: the tempting 16.Nxd5?? blunders mate on b2. The queen cannot keep d5 and e7 protected at the same time.} Qg4 {Hitting the rook.} (16... Qc5 17. Nxd5 {There is nothing to fear along the d-file, e.g.} Nb6 18. Nxe7+ $1 $18 {and Black loses absolutely everything.}) 17. Rxe7 {We are more than happy to oblige!} Rxe7 18. Bxe7 Re8 19. Bd6 Qxg2 {Black has managed to reestablish material equality, but strategically, White is still entirely dominating. We consolidate our advantage by establishing the knight on d4.} 20. Nd4 Bxd4 21. Qxd4 Qg5+ 22. f4 Qf6 23. Nxd5 $1 $16 {Using tactics to pick off the pawn. Of course, the bishop is untouchable due to the discovered check on b6.}) 15. Bg5 $1 $18 {Exchanging off Black's strongest piece, leaving the rest of his army unable to protect the d5-pawn and the incredibly vulnerable king at the same time.} Qc5 16. Nd4 {We are in no rush to take on f6. First, it is important to centralize the knight.} Rhf8 17. Bxf6 Nxf6 18. Nxc6 {Transforming our advantage: the powerful knight is given away in order to cripple Black's queenside pawn structure and initiate a decisive attack.} bxc6 19. Na4 {Forcing the queen to relinquish its control of the d4-square.} Qb5 20. Qd4 $18 {[%cal Rd4a7,Ra4c5,Re6e7,Rd1d3] White is winning both positionally and tactically: e7, a7, and c6 are all under fire, and White can follow up by establishing the knight on c5 and prying Black's queen away with a timely a2-a4.}) 14. Bh6 $1 Rf6 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 16. Ng5 Nc5 17. f4 $14 {White has a huge initiative. Our next move is Qd4, when we will threaten to capture on e6. In the event that Black doubles on the f-file, we can always solidify the f4-pawn with the calm g2-g3.}) 12. Qxd4 Bxf3 {The queen has lost its support, so we have no choice but to initiate the queen trade.} (12... Qxd4 $5 {The immediate capture leads to an equal ending, although White's practical chances are certainly preferable.} 13. Nxd4 Bxg2 14. Rg1 e5 $1 {Another difficult move to find. Black counterattacks our vulnerable pieces in the center, but enables us to reestablish material equality.} (14... Bd5 15. O-O-O Kf7 16. Rge1 $14 {Black has kept his extra pawn, but at great cost. We are threatening Nxd5 followed by Ne6, when Black will have no choice but to part ways with the d5-pawn.}) 15. Bxe5 Nxe5 16. Rxg2 O-O-O 17. Rg5 $11 {The position is equal, but I still prefer White - by a smidge. After the exchange of knights, Black will have a hard time deploying his bishop, and we have an easy path toward improving the rest of our pieces with Ke2 and Rd1 (or Rg1). There is no question that if Black has made it this far, he probably deserves the draw!}) 13. Qxb6 Nxb6 (13... axb6 {The computer lists this as a viable alternative to 13...Nxb6, but it creates yet another weakness on Black's queenside and leads to a pleasant endgame for White.} 14. gxf3 g6 15. Nb5 {Deploying the knight to the best square on the board and forcing Black's rook out of a8.} Ra4 {[%cal Ra4f4]} (15... Kf7 16. b3 $1 {[%cal Ra2a4] An important move, restricting the mobility of the rook and preparing to optimize White's queenside structure with a2-a4.} Bg7 17. Rd1 {a2 is untouchable since the knight hangs.} Ne5 18. a4 {It is far more important to improve our queenside structure than it is to defend the measly f3-pawn!} Nxf3+ 19. Ke2 e5 (19... Ne5 20. Be3 Rhc8 21. c4 Rc6 22. Rd4 $14 {Black is saddled with weaknesses all across the board, and we will continue to improve and prepare infiltration by doubling rooks on the d-file.}) 20. Be3 Nd4+ 21. Nxd4 exd4 22. Bxd4 Bxd4 23. Rxd4 $14 {with excellent winning chances due to Black's incredibly weak b-pawns.}) 16. Be3 Bg7 17. c3 $13 {The endgame is unclear and probably dynamically equal, but we have a clear plan of improvement: Ke2, a2-a3 (if necessary) followed by Rad1. Also worthwhile is the idea of probing for kingside weaknesses with h4-h5.}) 14. gxf3 g6 15. Be5 Rg8 16. Ne4 Bh6 17. h4 $11 {[%cal Rh4h5] Objectively, the position is equal, but we believe that White's play is quite a bit easier. We can improve our position with Ke2 followed by Rad1, and a well-timed h4-h5 threatens to shut down Black's bishop and will provoke more weaknesses on the kingside.}) (4... Nc6 {A huge blunder:} 5. Nb5 $1 e5 6. Bxe5 Nxe5 7. dxe5 Nxe4 8. Qxd5 Qxd5 9. Nc7+ Kd8 10. Nxd5 $18) 5. Nxe4 dxe4 6. dxc5 {Black is at a crossroads. The queen trade hands over control of the d-file, leading to a very pleasant endgame for White. Most players prefer to go directly for the c-pawn, an extra option afforded by the absence of the knight on c3.} Qa5+ (6... Qxd1+ 7. Rxd1 {In contrast to the endgame arising after 4...dxe5 5.dxc5 Qxd1+ 6.Rxd1, this is an improved version for Black since Nb5 is no longer a threat. However, Black still faces a host of positional problems: the c5-pawn still majorly restricts Black's development and coordination, and White's control over the d-file gives us great play in the center. Objectively, the position is equal, but we have unearthed several ways to confront Black with serious practical problems.} Nc6 (7... Bd7 8. Be3 {We will see this prophylactic move again in the main line (7...Nc6). The idea is to reinforce the c5-pawn, taking the sting out of ...e5.} e5 9. Bc4 {Turning our focus to development.} f5 10. g3 {It is, of course, very important to stop ...f5-f4, which would loosen our grip on the c5-pawn.} Be7 11. Ne2 {We now continue our development. The knight is heading not for b5 but for d5, where it will lord over the entire position.} Nc6 12. Nc3 O-O-O 13. Nd5 $14 {We can follow up by exerting our influence on the queenside with b4-b5.}) (7... e6 8. b4 {You attack, we defend!} a5 {As usual, this move is seldom to be feared in Jobava London endgames. We have plenty of resources with which to maintain the integrity of our queenside pawn structure.} 9. a3 axb4 10. axb4 Nc6 11. Bb5 {This is better than the meek 11.c3, since we simultaneously complete our development and fully immobilize Black's knight.} Bd7 12. Ne2 $14 {The b4-pawn is still untouchable. We will continue to improve our position by castling, and ferrying the knight over to c3. Once the weakening ...f5 is induced, we can rip open the center with the typical idea f2-f3, or double on the d-file and put significant pressure on the d7-bishop. Finally, our knight from c3 can seek greener pastures on b6.}) 8. Be3 $5 {A clever prophylactic move, taking the sting out of ...e5.} e5 (8... g6 9. Ne2 Bg7 10. Nd4 Bd7 (10... Bg4 11. Be2 Bxe2 12. Kxe2 O-O-O 13. Nxc6 bxc6 14. b4 f5 15. g3 Rxd1 16. Rxd1 Rd8 17. Rb1) 11. Bc4 O-O 12. Nxc6 Bxc6 13. c3 $14) 9. f3 {It may seem counterproductive to rid Black of his doubled pawns, but we must generate active prospects for our minor pieces. Once the f-file is opened, Black will also have to deal with long-term pressure against the f7-pawn.} (9. Bc4 $5 {A worthy alternative, although this move enables Black to build up a rather imposing kingside pawn mass. We will leave a deeper analysis of this move to you, but here is a sample line illustrating White's potential:} f5 10. g3 Be7 11. h4 Nd4 12. Rxd4 $5 exd4 13. Bxd4 Bf6 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. Ne2 Bd7 16. Kd2 $1 $44) 9... exf3 10. Nxf3 Bf5 (10... Be6 {This prevents Bc4, but the bishop is immediately targeted by our bloodthirsty knight.} 11. Ng5 Bg4 12. Be2 Bxe2 13. Kxe2 {The trade of bishops is in our favor, since Black is left with a lame dark-squared bishop (that darn c5 pawn again!)} h6 14. Ne4 f5 15. Nc3 Rc8 16. Nd5 $14 {Yet again, the knight has made its way to d5. As usual, we will increase the pressure dial on the queenside with b2-b4.}) 11. c3 {We now aim to develop our bishop to c4 and apply serious pressure on Black's f7-pawn.} Be6 {[%cal Re6a2]} (11... Rd8 12. Rxd8+ Kxd8 13. Ng5 Bg6 14. Bc4 Ke7 15. h4 $14 {with massive pressure on the kingside. Note that in response to 15...h6, we will simply drop our knight back to f3, putting pressure on the e5-pawn. We will then have the ability to play on all three sides: b2-b4 is the classic idea on the queenside, h4-h5 is one way to expand on the kingside, and the weak pawn on e5 will provide ample fodder for play in the center.}) 12. Nd2 {[%cal Rf1c4,Rd2c4] A clever retreating move. Of course, it is a retreating move in name only, since our intentions are extremely aggressive. We aim to root out Black's only active piece (the knight on c6 being fully restricted by the pawn on c3) with Bc4, when Black will be left with two minor pieces that will not be quite as capable of holding his position together.} f5 13. Bc4 Kf7 14. O-O {[%cal Rf1f7] Castling and threatening Rxf5+!} g6 15. b4 {Reinforcing the pawn on c5, and untying our hands for direct action on the kingside.} Be7 16. g4 $36 {The point of 9.f3 is on full display. White has full-scale pressure on the kingside, and the tempting 16...f4 creates a massive hole on e4 that we will use as an outpost for the knight.}) 7. c3 {Blocking the check and opening up a pathway for our own queen.} Qxc5 8. Qa4+ {Picking up the pawn on e4.} Nc6 (8... Bd7 {This move is exceedingly rare, but it has been the choice of several strong players in recent months.} 9. Qxe4 Bc6 10. Qc2 $1 {The queen is tucked away on an excellent square, from where it lords over both sides of the board while remaining fully impervious to attack.} e5 (10... Nd7 11. Nf3 e5 (11... e6 12. Nd4 $14) 12. Be3 Qa5 13. Bc4 Be7 14. O-O {Transposes to 10...e5 11.Be3 Qa5 12.Nf3 Nd7 13.Bc4 Be7 14.0-0.}) 11. Be3 Qa5 (11... Qd5 12. Nf3 $14) 12. Nf3 {Of course, we do not fear the capture on f3, which will hand us full control of all the light squares in the center.} Nd7 13. Bc4 {Deploying the bishop to an extremely active post.} Be7 (13... Bxf3 $2 14. gxf3 Qc7 15. Bb3 Bc5 16. Qf5 O-O 17. Bg5 g6 18. Rg1 $1 gxf5 19. Bf6#) (13... Bc5 $4 14. b4 $18) 14. O-O {Transposes to 10...e5 11.Be3 Qa5 12.Nf3 Nd7 13.Bc4 Be7 14.0-0.} O-O 15. Rad1 Rad8 16. Ng5 $36 {In order to parry the mate threat, Black must either swing his knight to f6, leaving its vis-a-vis fully entrenched on g5, or hand over the bishop pair, giving White a clear advantage in both cases.}) (8... Qc6 $4 {This move has occurred quite a few times online!} 9. Bb5 $18) 9. Qxe4 {An important position has been reached. Black has two main ways of continuing: there is 9...Bf5, forcing White's queen to decide straightaway, and the more methodical 9...g6, preparing to fianchetto the bishop (which will pose a rather evil threat indeed). Both moves must be examined through the looking-glass, although 9...g6 has been the almost automatic choice of most top players in recent months.} g6 (9... Bf5 10. Qa4 {On a4, the queen is mostly impervious to attack and discourages Black from castling queenside.} e5 (10... O-O-O {This brazen move creates a lot more problems than it solves!} 11. Nf3 e5 (11... Qa5 12. Qxa5 Nxa5 13. Be2 e6 (13... Nc6 14. Nd4 $1 {A lovely pawn sac to open up the c-file and engineer massive pressure on Black's king.} Nxd4 15. cxd4 Rxd4 16. Rc1+ Kd7 17. Be3 $36) 14. Nd4 Bg6 15. O-O $14 {White has a clear edge in the endgame due to our more active pieces and more mobile pawn mass (see common ideas #3).}) 12. Be3 Qd5 13. Bc4 {Black's queen gets tossed around, and the worst is yet to come.} Qe4 14. O-O Bd6 15. Ng5 $18 {Several of our blitz games reached this position. The queen has to move, and we will capture f7, with a decisive fork.}) 11. Be3 Qa5 12. Qxa5 Nxa5 13. O-O-O {White holds a small but significant edge in the endgame for several reasons: 1) We have already castled, and staked our claim on the d-file. Black will not only have a hard time castling, but will experience difficulties in deploying the a8-rook, since the a7-pawn will hang. 2) Our subsequent development is clear. The f1-bishop belongs on b5, while the knight will be deployed to f3. 3) As the subsequent lines reveal, the e5-pawn is also a target that we can chip away at with our f-pawn.} f6 $6 $14 {Reinforcing the e5-pawn appears sensible, but merely throws more oil into the fire.} (13... Be6 $5 14. f4 exf4 15. Bxf4 Be7 16. Bb5+ Nc6 17. Nf3 O-O 18. Rhe1 $14) 14. f4 $5 exf4 15. Bxf4 {The e-file has been blasted open, and our pieces come out with furious vigor.} Be6 $6 16. Bb5+ Kf7 17. Nf3 $16 {Black is in deep trouble, both strategically and tactically. Our rook will come to f1, creating long-term tactical threats along the f-file (such as Ng5+, followed by a discovered check with the bishop). Furthermore, our control over the d-file is particularly problematic, since Black's bishop cannot leave e6 (due to Rd7+). Finally, we can centralize our knight with Nd4, and if the bishop, the knight will move on to f5.}) 10. Nf3 {For now, we proceed with our development.} Bg7 {This way of developing has given quite a few Jobava London players grief, since Black generates the hidden threat of ...Bxc3+. Hans Niemann himself blundered in this position at the most recent World Blitz!} 11. Rd1 $5 {This is much more exciting (and far more rare) than the more standard 11.Qe3. White keeps the queens on the board and prepares a rapid kingside attack by means of Bf4-e3, Qh4, and ultimately Bh6. Black faces very serious practical problems in the center and on the kingside.} O-O (11... Qb6 $5 {This rare move is a very reasonable way to sidestep the complications arising after 11...0-0. Black's queen retreats to a somewhat safer square, and threatens the b2-pawn.} 12. Rd2 O-O (12... Bf5 13. Qa4 O-O 14. Bb5 $5 (14. Be3 Qc7 15. Be2 Rfd8 16. O-O Rxd2 17. Nxd2 $11) 14... a6 15. Bxc6 Qxc6 16. Qxc6 bxc6 17. O-O $11) 13. Be3 Qc7 14. Be2 {This is more modest than 14.Bc4, but it keeps the fourth rank open for our queen.} Bf5 15. Qa4 {With the Black queen on a5, we go the other way (to h4), but here, it makes more sense to exert queenside pressure with our queen, since we are given the opportunity to do so.} (15. Qh4 Rad8 16. O-O Rxd2 17. Nxd2 Rd8 18. Re1 $11) 15... Rfd8 16. Rxd8+ Rxd8 17. O-O a6 18. Rd1 $11 {The position is equal according to the engine, but it is definitely easier to play White. We can proceed by creating a luft square with h2-h3, and then gradually roll our queenside pawns up the board. It is worth starting with a2-a4, since that is the least committal of the queenside pawn moves. Later on, we will follow up with b2-b4-b5. Black, on the other hand, has quite a hard time making something concrete of his pawn majority.}) 12. Be3 {Forcing the queen onto an inferior square, and paving the way for the development of our other bishop.} Qa5 (12... Qf5 13. Qa4 {[%cal Rf1e2]} Be6 14. Be2 Rad8 15. O-O $14) 13. Bc4 {Developing while defending the a2-pawn.} Bf5 14. Qh4 Bf6 (14... Rad8 15. O-O Rxd1 16. Rxd1 Rd8 17. Re1 $1 {Keeping a pair of rooks on the board is crucial if we want to maintain a long-lasting initiative. With every trade, the power of White's kingside pressure diminishes considerably. Note that control over the d-file is hardly important, since all of the potential infiltration squares (d1, d2, d3, etc.) are fully covered by our rook and minor pieces.} h5 {Taking the sting out of the threat of Ng5.} (17... Ne5 {We have faced this type of move countless times in blitz.} 18. Nxe5 Qxe5 19. Bd2 (19. h3 $5 {An interesting alternative that sidesteps ideas related to the weak back rank.} h5 20. Bd2) 19... Rxd2 {A spectacular move, sacrificing the queen in order to attain tactical chances on the first rank. The objective evaluation is 0.00, but we can force Black to uncork countless insane computer moves to maintain this evaluation.} 20. Rxe5 Rd1+ 21. Bf1 Bd3 (21... Bxe5 $2 22. Qxe7 {and 22...Bd3 no longer works due to a crucial tactical detail:} Bd3 23. Qe8+ Kg7 24. Qxe5+ f6 25. Qe7+ {Checks galore!} Kg8 26. h3 {Simple and decisive - this disarms all of Black's play on the first rank.} Rxf1+ 27. Kh2 $18) 22. g4 {It is important to refrain from h2-h3, which would enable Black to capture the rook on e5 with check (after ...Rxf1+ Kh2). Instead, g2 is the ideal escape square for the king, since it will also maintain contact with the rook on f1, straining the bishop on d3.} Bxe5 23. Qxe7 Bg7 $8 {Another difficult only move, preparing to parry a check on the eighth rank with ...Bf8.} (23... Rxf1+ 24. Kg2 {It turns out that block drops a piece: there is no way to stop the simultaneous threats of Qxe5 and Qd8+-xd3. A lovely display of the queen's power in an open board!} Re1 25. Qd8+ Kg7 26. Qxd3 $18) (23... Bxf1 24. Qe8+ Kg7 25. Qxe5+ Kg8 26. h4 $18) 24. Qd8+ Bf8 25. Qxd3 Rxd3 26. Bxd3 $14 {This is obviously a draw with best play, but White retains serious practical chances. We will bring our king up to the center and create a passed pawn on the queenside.}) 18. h3 Ne5 19. Nxe5 Qxe5 20. Bd2 {Opening up a direct attack against the e7-pawn.} Bf6 21. Rxe5 Bxh4 22. Rd5 Rxd5 23. Bxd5 b6 24. Bf4 $16 {Black has succeeded in getting the heavy pieces off the board, but he has gone from the frying pan into the fire. White threatens to massacre Black's vulnerable queenside pawns with Bb8.}) (14... Ne5 15. Nxe5 Qxe5 (15... Bxe5 16. O-O Bf6 17. Qg3 Be5) 16. O-O Bf6 (16... Rad8 17. Bb3) 17. Qh6 $5 Bg7 18. Qg5 Bf6 (18... h6 19. Qh4 g5 20. Qh5 Bg6 $2 21. Qxg6 $18) 19. Qg3 Qxg3 20. hxg3 $14 {White holds a small but very stable edge in the endgame. We will have a much easier job creating a passed pawn on the queenside, and we have many easy ways to improve our position: queenside expansion with a4-a5, as well as straightforward improving moves such as Rfe1 and Bd5 come to mind.}) 15. Ng5 {Defending while attacking! This move provokes a serious weakness on the kingside, but more importantly, it buys us a tempo that we can use to light the fire of our initiative!} h5 (15... Bxg5 16. Qxg5 {Objectively position is even, but our bishop pair and more active light-squared bishop gives us a practical plus. Indeed, Black has to find the somewhat inhuman 16...Qa4 to keep the balance; a few inaccurate moves will hand White a stable plus, e.g.} Rad8 17. O-O Bc2 18. Qxa5 Nxa5 19. Rxd8 Rxd8 20. Be2 Nc6 21. f4 Bd3 22. Kf2 Bxe2 23. Kxe2 $14 {We are unquestionably better in the endgame. As usual, our plan is to gradually advance the queenside pawns, with the dual aims of creating a passed pawn and restricting the mobility of Black's knight, which is already greatly restricted by the c3-pawn.}) 16. Rd5 {Another scintillating blow, hitting the queen and the bishop at the same time!} Bxc3+ $1 {This only move is virtually impossible to find! It leads to a wild tactical battle that ultimately peters out into a draw, but 10 more computer moves stand between Black and the peaceful result!} (16... Qc7 $2 {This is the human move, and it loses on the spot:} 17. Rxf5 $1 {All of White's previous moves come together beautifully. Black must recapture the rook, opening up the pathway for White's queen. This is why we induced ...h7-h5!} gxf5 18. Qxh5 Bxg5 {The only way to stave off mate, though Black's days are obviously numbered.} 19. Qxg5+ Kh7 20. Qh5+ Kg8 21. g4 {Prying open either the g-file or the b1-h7 diagonal. In both cases, White's attack becomes decisive.} f4 22. Qg6+ Kh8 23. Qh6+ Kg8 24. Bd3 $1 f5 25. Bc4+ Rf7 26. Rg1 $1 $18 {[%cal Rg4f5]}) 17. bxc3 Qxc3+ 18. Bd2 {Covering the check, and sending the queen on a journey to h1.} Qa1+ 19. Ke2 Qxh1 {White has exactly one tempo before the checks resume, and we put this tempo to very good use:} 20. Rxf5 $1 {[%cal Rc4f7,Rf5f7,Rg5f7] Black has made it quite far, but his most difficult challenge is still ahead!} Rad8 $8 {[%csl Gd4][%cal Rd8d2,Rc6d4,Rd4f5,Rd4e2] Another impossible only move! Black sets up the threat of ...Nd4+, which will grant him just enough counterplay against our king to eke out a draw.} (20... gxf5 $4 21. Qxh5 Nd4+ {It is time for the king to go on an incredible journey:} 22. Ke3 f4+ (22... Nc2+ 23. Kf4 e5+ 24. Kxf5 Nd4+ 25. Kf6 $1 $18 {A gorgeous way to sidestep the checks!}) 23. Kxd4 Qa1+ (23... Rad8+ 24. Ke5 $3 {The center is often the best place to run, since the king has so many avenues of escape.} Qa1+ 25. Kxf4 Qf6+ 26. Kg4 Qg7 27. Bc3 Rd4+ 28. f4 $18) (23... Rfd8+ 24. Ke4 Qxg2+ 25. Ke5 $18) 24. Ke4 $8 {Black has run out of meaningful checks, and has no choice but to return dishonorably back to g7.} Qg7 {Now, it is our turn to start attacking!} 25. Bc3 e5 26. Bxe5 Rae8 {It appears that there is plenty of fight left, but we use our optimally-placed minor pieces to quash Black's resistance with a lovely mating pattern:} 27. Bxf7+ Rxf7 28. Nxf7 Qxf7 29. Qh8# {Remember, pinned pieces protect (PPP), so ...Kxh8 is illegal!}) (20... e6 21. Rxf7 $3 Rxf7 22. Nxf7 Kxf7 23. Qf4+ Ke7 24. Qc7+ Ke8 25. Qd6 $1 Qxg2 26. Qxe6+ Kd8 27. Qf6+ Kc7 28. Bf4+ Kb6 29. Qb2+ Kc5 30. Qb5+ Kd4 31. Be3+ Kc3 32. Qb3#) 21. Bxf7+ Rxf7 22. Nxf7 Nd4+ {Unfortunately, our king has nowhere left to run, since 23.Ke3 will lose the queen. The least we can do is sacrifice the queen on our own terms!} (22... gxf5 23. Nxd8 Nxd8 24. Qxe7 $18) 23. Qxd4 Rxd4 24. Nh6+ Kh7 25. Rf7+ Kh8 26. Rf8+ Kh7 27. Rf7+ $11 {Jobava London-3...c5  2023 If Black was able to find all 27 moves of these moves, then it is time to tip your hat and offer a draw, because you are most likely facing Magnus Carlsen, Stockfish 800, or a combination of the two!}) (3... a6 {A solid continuation, covering the b5-square once and for all.} 4. e3 e6 {This is, without question, one of Black's best lines from an objective standpoint. There is no doubt that Black equalizes, but we stress that the position remains complex and difficult to handle from a practical standpoint. From this position, Black has two main setups: the first involves striking at the center right away with ...c7-c5. In response, we will trade on c5 and quickly prepare the typical central breakthrough e3-e4, granting us a nice space advantage and easy piece play. The second plan, more seldom seen at the club player level, involves grabbing as much queenside space as possible with ...b7-b5, and following up with ...Bb7 to prevent e4. We are quite proud of our recommendation against this second plan, which involves quickly closing up the queenside and transitioning our focus to a lightning kingside attack.} (4... Nc6 {This is extremely popular online, with over a hundred thousand games! Although developing the knight to c6 is natural, it blocks the c-pawn, handing White immediate supremacy in the center. Of course, we have also committed the same transgression, but we have the benefit of the extra tempo! We harness our development advantage by quickly shepharding the knight to e5, and following up with g2-g4 in the event that Black develops the bishop to f5.} 5. Nf3 Bg4 (5... Bf5 6. Ne5 Nxe5 (6... e6 7. g4 $1 {The typical shot works to perfection here.} Nxe5 {This is necessary in order to maintain an objectively acceptable position, but from a practical standpoint, White has a major initiative.} (7... Bg6 {This is far more common than 7...Nxe5, but sends Black even further into deep trouble. The only way to extricate the bishop is to introduce near-fatal weaknesses on the kingside.} 8. h4 {As usual, the threat of h5 induces a decisive kingside weakness.} Nxe5 (8... h6 9. Nxg6 fxg6 10. Bd3 Kf7 11. Qf3 $16 {Adding insult to injury! After we castle, the threat of g4-g5 will spell immediate doom to the Black king.}) 9. dxe5 Ne4 10. Nxe4 Bxe4 11. f3 Bg6 12. h5 $18 {A staggering number of games concluded in this fashion. Black loses the bishop.}) 8. gxf5 Nc6 (8... Nc4 9. Bxc4 dxc4 10. fxe6 fxe6 11. Qe2 b5 12. Rg1 $16 {This move prevents the easy development of the bishop. Black's king has nowhere safe to go, and his structure is in shambles.}) (8... Ned7 9. fxe6 fxe6 10. Bh3 {Hitting where it hurts! Note that 10...Qe7 drops the c7-pawn, so Black has no choice but to part ways with e6.} Bb4 (10... Qe7 11. Bxc7 $16) 11. Bxe6 c6 12. Rg1 $16 {Black has zero compensation for the pawn, and ...Ne4 always runs into the devastating Qh5+.}) 9. fxe6 fxe6 10. Bh3 Bd6 {Here, it is important to preserve the bishop pair rather than to greedily capture on e6, which leads to the complete dissolution of White's center after the trade on f4.} (10... Qd7 11. a3 Bd6 12. Bg3 O-O-O 13. Qd3 $14 {White has the bishop pair (for now) and a pleasant bishop. Next, we will castle queenside and can play either in the center (e3-e4) or expand on the kingside with f2-f4.}) 11. Bg5 Qe7 12. e4 {This is the best practical try. The computer gives equality (as usual), but Black's position is remarkably hard to play and traps exist by the basinful.} dxe4 13. Nxe4 O-O-O 14. c3 {Only 14...Kb8 preserves a double-edged position; the natural moves all fail to the same trap, which wins immediately:} Kb8 (14... Qf7 $2 15. Bxf6 gxf6 16. d5 $16 {[%cal Rh3e6,Rd5c6]}) 15. Qe2 h6 16. Bxf6 gxf6 17. O-O-O f5 18. Nxd6 Rxd6 19. Rhg1 $11 {Black struggles with the weak pawn on e6, and the bishop has great potential once it returns to g2. Black can maintain the balance with computer-like play (starting with 19...Qh4), but there is no question that any normal human would prefer White.}) (6... Nb4 {In contrast to Nb5, this move is nothing more than an empty threat. We easily protect the pawn and send the knight back where it belongs.} 7. Rc1 e6 8. a3 Nc6 9. g4 {The rook on c1 and pawn on a3 change nothing: this shot is as strong as ever.} Nxe5 10. gxf5 Nc6 11. fxe6 fxe6 12. Ne2 $14 {[%cal Rc2c4]}) 7. dxe5 Ne4 {Here, it is important not to capture on d5, as that enables Black to develop quickly and dangerously with 8...e6.} 8. Nxe4 dxe4 (8... Bxe4 9. e6 $1 {Shattering Black's center and carving out a dominant outpost on e5. We see the exact same idea in the 3...c5 line.} fxe6 10. Be5 Qd7 11. f3 Bg6 12. h4 {Grabbing space and inducing even more kingside weaknesses.} h5 13. Qd2 O-O-O 14. a4 $40 {Total domination in the center and an unstoppable attack on the queenside. Our next move is to b2-b4, and after the pawn appears on b5, Black will probably be best advised to resign in order to avoid a total massacre. Also do not forget about the possibility of a rook lift to c3, particularly if Black's queen appears on c6.}) 9. Qd4 $1 {Trading on our own terms!} Qxd4 10. exd4 e6 11. c3 {Solidifying the center.} c5 12. Be3 {An astute positional move, preparing to recapture on d4 with the bishop.} cxd4 13. Bxd4 {It may seem more intuitive to recapture with the pawn, but that would grant Black active prospects for his dark-squared bishop, and subject us to long-term pressure down the d-file. On d4, the bishop lords over the entire position, preventing the active development of Black's DSB and keeping the d-file closed for business. Our next step is to complete our development and get our king to e3, when Black experiences long-term problems with the e4-pawn.} Rc8 14. Be2 Bc5 15. Kd2 Ke7 16. Rhc1 Rhd8 17. Ke3 $14 {White will combine kingside and queenside expansion. Next up is g4 followed by h4, and we can expand on the queenside with a4-a5 as well - that is why we left the rook on a1! Finally, after g4 is played, remember that you can transfer your light-squared bishop via f1 to g2, when Black will likely lose the e4-pawn.}) 6. h3 {Asking the bishop a polite question!} Bxf3 (6... Bh5 7. g4 Bg6 8. Ne5 {This series of moves should be automatic by now! With Black's pawn not yet on e6, the power of the knight on e5 is increased even further.} e6 (8... Nxe5 9. dxe5 Ne4 10. Nxe4 Bxe4 11. f3 Bg6 12. e6 $1 {This move should be automatic as well!} fxe6 13. Be5 $16) 9. h4 {As usual, the threat of h5 induces a decisive kingside weakness.} h6 (9... Nxe5 10. dxe5 Ne4 (10... d4 {This is the only way to muddy the waters, although White still emerges from the complications with a nearly winning position.} 11. exd4 Nd5 {Now, the intuitive 12.Nxd5 gives Black a nagging initiative, so the silicon fiend unearths a delightful idea that defies belief:} 12. Bg2 $1 {Simply leaving the f4-bishop en prise! Upon closer investigation, the idea of this move should become clear: after Black takes the bishop, we will capture the pawn on f7, when the threat of h4-h5, coupled with the prospect of a nasty check on c6, spells immediate doom for a totally uncoordinated and underdeveloped Black army. Ideas like these are why we love the Jobava.} Nxf4 13. Bxb7 Be7 (13... Bb4 14. Bc6+ Kf8 15. Qf3 (15. h5 {As tempting as it is, this move meets with an incredible refutation that gives Black attacking chances.} Bd3 $3 16. cxd3 Rb8 $13 {[%csl Rd4] The weakness of the d4-pawn grants Black sufficient counterplay.}) 15... Qxd4 16. Bxa8 Qxe5+ 17. Qe3 $18 {Black's attack fizzles out, and White's material advantage will be decisive. Note that the g2-square is controlled by our bishop!}) (13... Rb8 14. Bc6+ Ke7 15. Qd2 Rb6 16. Ba4 Qa8 17. O-O-O Nd5 18. h5 Nxc3 19. Qxc3 $18 {[%cal Rc3c7]}) 14. Bc6+ {An important intermediate move, to prevent castling.} Kf8 15. h5 {Black loses a decisive amount of material. There is one more detail to keep in mind, though:} Bd3 {As in the 13...Bb4 line, taking the bishop leads to unnecessary complications. Instead, we sidestep the bishop and act with surgical precision.} 16. Qf3 $1 {Who cares about the bishop? Black is unable to save the rook and the knight at the same time.} Qxd4 17. Bxa8 Qxe5+ 18. Qe3 $18 {The queen trade is inevitable, after which Black loses even more material.}) 11. Nxe4 Bxe4 12. f3 Bg6 13. h5 $18 {A staggering number of games concluded in this fashion. Black loses the bishop.}) 10. Nxg6 fxg6 11. Qd3 Kf7 12. O-O-O $18 {Black's position is in ruins. Note that ...Nb4 is not scary, as our queen simply drops down to e2.}) 7. Qxf3 e6 {Although we have the bishop pair, Black has the twin ideas of ...Bb4 and ...Bd6, both of which threaten to neutralize our initiative. In order to maintain the edge, it is crucial for us to act decisively.} 8. O-O-O Bb4 (8... Bd6 9. g4 {Initiating kingside pressure. It is rather painful to part ways with the bishop pair, but there was nothing we could have done: 9.Bg5 would be futile on account of 9...h6 followed by ...g5.} Bxf4 10. Qxf4 Qd6 (10... Qe7 11. g5 Nh5 (11... Nd7 $2 12. Qxc7 $16) 12. Qg4 g6 {With the knight out of commission, we switch the center of action from the kingside to the queenside.} 13. Na4 b6 (13... O-O 14. Be2 Ng7 15. h4 $40 {Black has no good answer to the straightforward idea of dropping the queen to h3 or g2 and following up with h5, blasting open the h-file.}) 14. c4 $1 {Having induced the dreadfully weakening ...b6, we initiate queenside operations without further delay. It may seem rather brazen to push the c-pawn once we have castled queenside, but Black's pieces are too passive and uncoordinated to present a serious dangerous to our king. Worst comes to worse, the king can always find shelter on b1 or a1. Black's king, on the other hand, has nowhere safe to run.} dxc4 {This natural move loses quickly:} (14... Qb4 15. Nc3 {Black has no obvious continuation: the pawn on d5 is hanging, and his position is on the verge of collapse.} Ne7 16. cxd5 exd5 17. Nxd5 $1 Nxd5 18. Qe4+ $18 {Picking up the knight with interest.}) 15. Bg2 Qd7 16. Qe4 Qd5 17. Qxd5 exd5 18. Bxd5 Kd7 19. Bxc4 $18) 11. Qxd6 cxd6 {Even with the queens off the board, we are able to generate a massive initiative in the center with a series of accurate moves:} 12. g5 Nd7 13. e4 {Breaking through in the center.} dxe4 {Now, the meek 14.Nxe4 would enable Black to repair his center with 14...d5. Instead, our intentions are far more violent.} 14. d5 exd5 15. Rxd5 $16 {Black is in trouble: he will lose either d6 or e4, or both. Whichever pawn we capture first, we will bring out the rest of our pieces and surround the other one, likely with Bg2 and Re1 or Rhd1.}) 9. Ne2 {Simple and powerful! Of course, we cannot allow ...Bxc3. Now, the bishop looks dumb. Later down the line, we also possess the idea of pushing c2-c4, Queen's Gambit-style.} Ne4 (9... Qe7 10. Bg5 {An annoying little move, aiming to fully destroy Black's kingside pawn structure with Bxf6. The bishop pair is meant to be given away!} h6 11. Bxf6 gxf6 (11... Qxf6 12. Qxf6 gxf6 13. Nf4 Ke7 14. g4 $14 {White has a stable plus in the endgame. We will plant the knight on h5, tying Black down to f6, and follow by preparing c2-c4 or even (after Bg2 and Rhf1) f4-f5, accentuating the weakness of the f6-pawn.}) 12. Nf4 {Bringing the knight into position. We now threaten to freeze the f6 pawn with g2-g4, when Black will have no good answer to the threat of Nh5.} f5 13. g4 {This move is just as powerful here as with the pawn on f6. By opening the h-file, we shift our focus from the f-pawn to the h-pawn.} fxg4 14. hxg4 O-O-O 15. Rh5 $16 {Anatoly Karpov would kill for such a position with White! Our next step is to develop the bishop and stack the rooks. Black's ability to defend h6 is limited by the poor coordination of his pieces and by the fact that the knight on c6 has no access to any central squares.}) (9... O-O 10. g4 {Commencing the attack without delay.} Ne4 11. Ng3 (11. h4 {This is also good, and perhaps even more straightforward than 11.Ng3.} Qe7 12. Ng3 Nc3 $1 {A scary-looking tactic, but it is easy to parry.} 13. Rd3 $1 Nxa2+ 14. Kb1 Bc3 15. e4 $1 Nxd4 16. Rxd4 Bxd4 17. Kxa2 $16) 11... g5 {It appears that we have blundered a piece, but this is all part of the plan. In return for the minor piece, we will receive two pawns and, most importantly, fantastic chances to checkmate Black on the kingside.} 12. Bxc7 $1 Qxc7 13. Nxe4 dxe4 14. Qxe4 Rae8 15. h4 $16 {From a practical perspective, Black's position is untenable. The h-file is about to open up, and Black will have no answer to the massive amount of material that we will pile up on the h7-pawn, which will include the bishop and the other rook.}) (9... Bd6 10. g4 {See 8...Bd6 9.g4. Here, the only difference is in the placement of the knight; we believe that it is even better on e2, since ...Bxf4 will always be met with Nxf4.} Ne4 11. Ng3 Nxg3 12. fxg3 $5 $14 {An ingenious positional move, opening up the f-file. Our long-term pressure along the f-file will guarantee White a stable advantage. Also, do not forget about c2-c4, which would come at the least convenient moment for Black.}) 10. Ng3 g5 11. Be5 (11. Nxe4 $5 {This sacrifice deserves serious merit, although it leads to a rather chaotic tactical battle that requires intense study.} dxe4 (11... gxf4 12. Nd2 fxe3 13. fxe3 $14) 12. Qxe4 gxf4 13. d5 f5 $8 14. Qxe6+ Ne7 15. d6 Bxd6 16. Bc4 Rf8 17. e4 fxe4 18. Qxe4 $40 {[%cal Re4h7,Re4b7]}) 11... Nxe5 (11... f6 12. Nxe4 fxe5 13. Qh5+ $18 {Black's king is driven out and massacred.}) 12. dxe5 Nxg3 13. Qxg3 {Visually, Black's position appears more than tenable, but the incredibly weak pawn on g5 tells a different story. We will use that pawn in order to develop enduring pressure down the h-file. Black, on the other hand, has very little going on the other side of the board: our king is perfectly safe on c1.} Qe7 14. h4 {Using the g-pawn as a "hook" to open the h-file, a typical motif worth remembering!} gxh4 15. Rxh4 O-O-O 16. Bd3 Rdg8 17. Qf4 Bc5 18. g3 $14 {Black has a permanent weakness on h7. We will accumulate the pressure with Rdh1, when the pawn's days are likely numbered.}) (4... c5 5. dxc5 {Typically, Black does not have any difficulty winning back the pawn, but this position is a notable exception. If Black plays ...e7-e5 (after 5...Nc6), the d5-pawn becomes very soft, and we maintain our material advantage. If, on the other hand, Black goes after the c-pawn immediately with 5...e6, we cling to it with 6.Na4, forcing Black to give up the valuable dark-squared bishop in order to recover the pawn. In either case, White has a long-lasting advantage, but the lines in the 5...Nc6 line are very sharp and it is worth spending an hour or two to understand the zen of the position.} Nc6 (5... e6 6. Na4 {We ain't gonna give that pawn up easy! It turns out that Black has no way to win back the pawn without yielding the bishop pair, which turns out to be incredibly valuable in this particular structure.} (6. b4 $2 {It is essential to avoid this move, after which our queenside will collapse like a house of cards.} b6 $1 7. cxb6 (7. Rb1 bxc5 8. bxc5 Qa5 $15) 7... Bxb4 8. Nge2 Qxb6 $19) 6... Bxc5 (6... Nbd7 7. b4 {In human terms, Black is already in massive trouble.} b6 (7... a5 8. a3 $16) 8. c6 {This move signals the key difference between 7.b4 and 6.b4: the knight in d7 is now under fire, and after 8...Bxb4+ 9.c3 we attack two minor pieces at the same time. Amazingly, Black can make this line work using an insane sequence of computer moves, but White still maintains an objective advantage at the end. Most people who stumble into this line will have done so unintentionally!} Bxb4+ 9. c3 Ba5 10. cxd7+ Bxd7 {It transpires that Black has serious compensation for the pawn, in the form of pressure on the c3-pawn and the constant possibility of ...Bxa4. We have to proceed with the utmost precision.} 11. Be5 Ne4 12. f3 $1 {Not fearing the check on h4, which is cooly rebuffed by g2-g3. Practically speaking, Black is already in very serious trouble. The engine finds ways to keep Black's initiative going, but it is safe to say that a human will not be able to demonstrate anything resembling sufficient compensation for the piece.} f6 13. Bd4 Nd6 14. Rb1 {Poking and prodding at the b6-pawn. Now, ...b5 yields the c5-square, so Black must defend obediently.} Rb8 15. f4 {Preventing ...e5, and preparing to develop our knight.} O-O 16. Nf3 $14 {We aim to complete our development with Bd3 and 0-0, when Black's compensation will simply evaporate. The engine finds a way to keep the spark of Black's initiative going, but we will bet a silver dollar that this position will never occur in your tournament or online practice :)}) 7. Nxc5 Qa5+ 8. c3 Qxc5 9. Nf3 Nc6 10. Bd3 O-O (10... Qe7 {A rather devious move, threatening to win a piece with ...e5 followed by ...e4.} 11. Ne5 {As in most Jobava positions, this knight sally is incredibly effective. In particular, it underscores the extent to which Black misses his dark-squared bishop in these sorts of positions; it is remarkably difficult to kick our minor pieces away from e5; Black can only do so at the cost of seriously compromising the activity of his own minor pieces.} Nd7 (11... O-O 12. O-O Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Nd7 14. Bg3 e5 15. Bc2 Nf6 16. Bh4 $14 {Turning up the pressure dial on d5. We have a litany of improving moves that we can follow up with, such as a4-a5 (to grab space on the queenside) and Qd3 (to threaten the h7 pawn and coordinate the rooks). If we can induce ...e5-e4, then we will use the d4-square as an outpost for the queen.}) 12. Nxc6 {Taking this knight permanently damages Black's queenside pawn structure.} bxc6 13. Bg3 $1 {Taking the sting out of ...e5.} O-O 14. Qc2 h5 15. Qe2 {[%cal Re2h5]} g6 (15... h4 $2 16. Qh5 $16) 16. h3 Nc5 17. O-O $14 {We are more than happy to enter an opposite-colored bishop middlegame, where our vise-like grip on the dark squares, coupled with the prospect of opening the center at our leisure with e3-e4, guarantees White a pleasant edge. Black, on the other hand, will have a very hard time deploying his light-squared bishop to a reasonably active square.}) 11. O-O b5 12. b4 {Using tactics to halt the progress of Black's pawn. Of course, c3 is untouchable due to Rc1.} Qe7 13. a4 $16 {Prying open the queenside, and creating a serious long-term weakness on a6. Note that in response to 13...bxa4, we first have to withdraw the dark-squared bishop to g3 in order to take the sting out of ...e5. Then, we will recapture on a4 and turn up the queenside pressure.}) (5... Qa5 {A sensible way to recapture the c5-pawn, but it comes at the steep cost of Black's development.} 6. Qd2 Qxc5 (6... e6 7. Nf3 Bxc5 8. Bd3 O-O 9. O-O Nc6 10. a3 Be7 11. b4 Qd8 12. h3 $14 {[%cal Re3e4]}) 7. O-O-O $1 {Capitalizing on our development advantage and applying immediate pressure on the d5-pawn.} e6 (7... Bf5 {Indirectly defending the d5-pawn.} 8. Nf3 Nc6 (8... e6 9. Nh4 {Securing the bishop pair and reopening the possibility of e3-e4.} Bg6 10. Nxg6 hxg6 11. e4 $16) 9. Ne5 {We now transition to a plan of kingside expansion, preparing to underscore the vulnerability of the f5-bishop with f2-f3 followed by g2-g4. Even without the pawn on d4, our knight is perfectly safe on e5, and the bishop that will take its place in the event of ...Nxe5 will pose the serious threat of Bxf6, crippling Black's kingside pawn structure.} e6 10. f3 Bd6 (10... Be7 11. g4 Bg6 12. h4 Nxe5 13. Bxe5 h6 14. Kb1 $16 {Black's king has nowhere to run!}) 11. Nxc6 Bxf4 12. exf4 bxc6 13. g4 {Transitioning to a furious kingside attack! Even without a knight on e5 to guide them forward, the kingside pawns constitute a formidable force. As a result of Black's compromised queenside pawn structure, the Black king has no safe place to hide.} Bg6 14. h4 h6 (14... h5 {This move halts the progress of White's pawns on the kingside...or so it seems. We take the opportunity before Black has castled to smash open the e-file, giving us a dangerous and enduring initiative.} 15. f5 exf5 16. Na4 {Forcing the queen away from c5 and preparing to entrench the knight on b6.} Qd6 17. Nb6 {It may seem that the knight is anything but stable here, but we will use some fascinating tactics to justify its placement!} Rb8 18. g5 {The point is revealed: the knight is untouchable due to the fork on e3!} Nd7 19. Re1+ Kf8 {Just when it seems that our attack has fizzled out, a lovely tactical shot comes to the rescue.} 20. Qh2 $1 {Gorgeous! The queen is taboo due to Nxd7+, and Black is forced to part ways with his extra pawn.} f4 21. Nxd7+ Qxd7 22. Qxf4 $14 {We have recovered our material investment, and stand clearly better. Our pawn structure is superior, and we can continue stoking the coals of attack by bringing our bishop out to h3. Of course, ...Qb7 is not dangerous on account of b3, and Black's "attack" comes to an end.}) 15. Re1 {Threatening f5 and forcing Black to seek shelter on the kingside...where a far scarier enemy awaits him!} O-O 16. h5 Bh7 17. g5 $40 {The attack is already quite devastating.}) 8. e4 {Straightforward and extremely powerful. The pawn is obviously untouchable due to checkmate on d8.} Nc6 9. exd5 exd5 10. Na4 {We have seen this typical move many times in these variations (such as in the 7...Bf5 line, 14...h5 sub-variation). The queen is chased away to a far less desirable square, making it easier for us to exploit the countless weaknesses in Black's position. In particular, we put our knight in position to infiltrate the dreadfully weak b6-square, which spells immediate doom for Black.} Qa7 11. Nf3 Be7 12. Be3 {The beginning of the end.} Qb8 13. Nb6 $18 {Black loses a ton of material.}) 6. a3 {Preparing to reinforce the pawn with b4, and essentially forcing Black's hand.} e5 (6... g6 {I have faced this move several times in blitz (DN), and it makes a good deal of sense: 7.b4 is now quite a bit less desirable, since the bishop on g7 will exert rather nasty pressure on our knight. Instead, we defend the pawn in an altogether different way:} 7. Na4 $1 {This removes the knight from the line of fire of Black's bishop, and threatens Nb6. Note that ...Qa5+ is never dangerous in such positions due to c3 (followed by b4, chasing the queen right back to its initial square).} Nd7 8. b4 {Refraining from the greedy Qxd5, which causes unnecessary complications. With the knight on a safe square, there is nothing to fear from the deployment of Black's bishop to the long diagonal.} Bg7 9. Rc1 {An astute positional move. The rook will support the c2-c4 breakthrough.} e5 (9... b5 10. cxb6 Nxb6 11. c4 $1 $16 {This is why we put the rook on c1!}) 10. Bg3 d4 11. Nf3 O-O 12. c3 {Forcing Black to get rid of the d4-pawn.} dxe3 13. fxe3 e4 14. Nd4 $16 {Black has no compensation for the pawn. Moreover, he can barely move, since ...Nde5 will always run into Nb6. We will follow up by completing our kingside development, when our extra pawn and superior piece placement gives us a near-decisive advantage.}) 7. Bg5 {Here, and only here! The lackluster 7.Bg3 enables Black to recover the pawn without consequences.} Be6 (7... d4 {This combative move does not solve Black's problems, but it leads to a good deal of tactical complexity, so it is worth looking over the following moves very carefully.} 8. Na4 {We refrain from capturing on d4, which enables Black to entrench his knight on a powerful central square.} Be6 (8... Qa5+ $2 $18 {As always, this move is innocuous and merely hastens Black's demise.} 9. c3 Ne4 (9... dxc3 10. b4 {A crucial detail that justifies the entire line! We shut down Black's queenside counterplay, and prepare to recapture the pawn on c3 with a dominant position. The piece sac on b4 leads nowhere:} Nxb4 11. axb4 Qxb4 12. Qc2 {Simple and cold-blooded, preventing Black's only idea.} Bxc5 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Bd3 $18 {The c3-pawn is not going anywhere! First, we will complete our kingside development, and then capture the pawn at our leisure. Black's position is resignable.}) 10. exd4 $1 {Fearless and incredibly powerful. The piece sacrifice is temporary; we will use our control over the b6-square and the dreadful placement of Black's queen to gain the material back with interest.} Nxg5 11. Nb6 {First, the knight is placed on b6 to slice off the queen's only evacuation route. We now threaten b2-b4, which will trap Black's queen.} Ne4 (11... Rb8 12. b4 $18) 12. b4 {Anyway! This is a lot better than the greedy 12.Nxa8, which allows Black to seriously complicate affairs with a nasty capture on c3. In order to save the queen, Black must part ways with his extra piece.} Nxc3 13. Qb3 Nxb4 14. Qxb4 Qxb4 15. axb4 Rb8 16. d5 $18 {The pawn phalanx is totally overwhelming. We can continue by smoking out Black's knight with Ne2, and we can respond to ...Be7 with d5-d6.}) (8... Bf5 {This nondescript developing move accomplishes nothing, and enables us to attain an ideal setup on the queenside.} 9. Nb6 Rb8 10. b4 h6 11. Bxf6 Qxf6 12. Nf3 Rd8 13. e4 $1 {Shutting down Black's counterplay on the d-file.} Bxe4 14. Bd3 Bxf3 15. Qxf3 Qxf3 16. gxf3 {Black is borderline lost due to our immense control over key light squares, including e4 and c4. Black also struggles with a total lack of coordination and an inability to find a suitable square for the dark-squared bishop.} Be7 17. Nc4 O-O 18. Be4 f6 19. O-O-O $18 {[%cal Rf3f4] f3-f4 is coming sooner rather than later.}) 9. exd4 Nxd4 (9... Qxd4 10. Qxd4 Nxd4 11. Bd3 Bd5 12. f3 Nd7 13. b4 a5 14. Nc3 Be6 15. Rb1 b6 16. cxb6 Nxb6 17. Nge2 $14) (9... exd4 $2 10. Nb6 Ra7 11. b4 h6 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. Bd3 $18) 10. Nb6 Bxc5 11. Nxa8 Qxa8 12. Bxf6 gxf6 13. Nf3 Qc8 14. Nd2 {[%cal Rd2e4]} Bf5 15. Bd3 Rg8 16. Ne4 Bxe4 (16... Qc6 17. Nxc5 Qxg2 18. Kd2 b6 19. Bxf5 Qg5+ 20. Kc3 Qxf5 21. Qd3 $1 Qc8 22. Rag1 $18) (16... Rxg2 17. c3 $1 Bxe4 (17... Nb5 18. Nxc5 Qxc5 19. Qf3 {[%cal Rf3f5,Rf3g2]}) 18. Bxe4 Rg4 19. f3 Rg2 20. cxd4 Bxd4 21. Rc1 $18) 17. Bxe4 f5 18. Bd5 $14) (7... Bxc5 8. Bxf6 {Of course, this is the point of deploying the bishop to g5. Black loses the d5-pawn without compensation.} Qxf6 9. Qxd5 Bb6 10. Qf3 $16 {Withdrawing the queen from the center and offering a queen trade. Black has absolutely nothing in return for the pawn.}) 8. b4 Be7 (8... h6 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Na4 {Standard fare, deploying the knight to the beautiful b6-square.} Qc7 11. Nb6 Rd8 12. c3 {Halting the progress of Black's d-pawn. Despite the somewhat chaotic apperance of the position, Black has no active plan and no compensation for the pawn.} f5 (12... d4 {is futile on account of} 13. Qc2 $16) 13. Bd3 f4 14. Bc2 {Taking the sting out of ...e4, which will no longer come with tempo.} fxe3 15. fxe3 Rg8 16. g3 {Calmly defending g2.} Rg4 17. Ne2 d4 18. e4 $1 {Keeping things tight in the center!} dxc3 19. Nd5 $18 {A devastating final resource, underscoring the potency of the knight. Trading on e5 hands over a decisive pawn phalanx in the center, while 19...Qc8 enables us to recapture the c3-pawn with the other knight, when its colleague on d5 will be unassailable. Black is objectively lost.}) (8... d4 9. exd4 Nxd4 (9... exd4 10. Na4 a5 11. Bb5 axb4 12. axb4 Be7 13. Nf3 O-O 14. O-O Nxb4 15. Nxd4 $16) 10. Nf3 $16) 9. Nf3 O-O 10. Be2 {White is now up a pawn for no compensation, but there is one more detail worth mentioning.} Qe8 $5 {[%cal Rf6e4] A devious resource that was employed against DN in a recent speedrun game. Now, 11.0-0 (as played in the game) runs into 11...Ne4, and Black wins material. As usual, our top priority is to lead our c3-knight to the promised land.} 11. Na4 Rd8 12. O-O d4 13. Qc1 $14 {Cold-blooded and powerful. Black is out of active ideas, and we can improve our position in a litany of ways, ranging from Nb6 to c2-c3 to Rf1-d1.}) (4... b5 {This has been the choice of many strong GMs; in particular, Belarussian GM Sergei Zhigalko has played this line regularly against the authors of this course. Black aims to rapidly deploy the bishop to b7, making it virtually impossible for White to break in the center with e3-e4. We spent a long time on this move, and are proud to recommend a rare idea that confronts Black with serious practical problems.} 5. a3 {Preventing the further progress of Black's b-pawn, and setting up the main idea of the line.} Bb7 6. Nf3 e6 7. b4 {This is a rare, but extremely dangerous idea. We freeze Black's queenside play entirely, preventing ...c7-c5. Without this idea, Black is essentially stalemated. We, on the other, have clear ways to improve our position: the knight will be centralized on e5, the bishop developed to d3, and once Black castles kingside, our queen is immediately transported to h3, creating long-lasting and potentially fatal threats on the kingside. Do not be fooled by the engine's assessment: our practice and analysis reveals that Black's position is very hard to play due to the conspicuous absence of a clear active plan. See 4...b5 5.a3 Bb7 6.b4 e6 7.Nf3.} Nbd7 (7... Bd6 8. Ne5 O-O 9. Bd3 Nbd7 {See 7...Nbd7 8.Ne5 0-0 9.Bd3 Bd6.}) 8. Bd3 Bd6 (8... Ne4 {This rare move presents a serious challenge to White's setup. There is no question that Black equalizes, but we can definitely pose some serious problems with accurate play.} 9. Bxe4 dxe4 10. Nd2 Nf6 (10... f5 11. Nb3 Bd6 12. Na5 Bd5 13. Qh5+ g6 14. Qh6 Bf8 15. Qh3 Bd6 16. Qg3 Nb6) 11. O-O Be7 12. Bg5 $16) 9. Ne5 {The knight on e5 is completely unassailable: to get rid of it, Black must either part ways with a minor piece or must hand over the bishop pair to White. We see this exact type of setup in many lines. Note the power of the pawn on b4: without the possibility of ...c7-c5, Black has no good way to assail our dominance in the center.} O-O {See 7...Nbd7 8.Ne5 0-0 9.Bd3 Bd6.} (9... Ne4 {An inventive way to prevent Qf3, but this enables us to change the pawn structure in our favor.} 10. Bxe4 dxe4 11. Qg4 {Asking Black a rather nasty question. Castling fails tactically, while ...g6 creates long-term weaknesses on the kingside. Black can maintain objective equality with a host of computer moves, but no one in their right mind would prefer Black's position here!} Qf6 {Trying to force pieces off the board fails tactically.} (11... O-O $2 12. Bh6 $16 {Black loses the exchange, since 12...Qf6 drops the knight (and then the rook).}) (11... Kf8 12. h4 $36 {A great example of a position where the computer evaluation is utterly useless. The computer gives 0.00, but we strongly believe that White's position is incomparably preferable. We will expand on the kingside with h4-h5, and can follow up by dropping the queen to h3 and advancing the g-pawn. Black has no active plan.}) (11... g6 $5 12. h4 Nf6 13. Qe2 {[%cal Rc3b5]} Nd7 (13... h6 14. Nxb5 $1 $16) 14. Bg5 Be7 (14... f6 15. Nxd7 fxg5 (15... Kxd7 16. Bf4 Bxf4 17. exf4 f5 18. a4 $36) 16. Nc5 Bxc5 17. bxc5 gxh4 18. Qg4 $16) 15. Nxd7 Qxd7 16. Bxe7 Qxe7 17. Qg4 f5 18. Qg5 $14) 12. Nxd7 Kxd7 13. Bxd6 cxd6 14. Nxe4 Qg6 15. Qxg6 hxg6 16. f3 {A crucial resource, supporting the knight and keeping our extra pawn.} Bxe4 17. fxe4 Rh4 {Just when it appears that Black is out of the woods, we open a second front.} 18. a4 $1 {Perfect timing; this move works to perfection with the rook on a8 now undefended.} Rxe4 19. axb5 Rxe3+ 20. Kd2 Re4 21. Kd3 $18 {Defending the d4-pawn with tempo. We will capture on a6 on the next move, when the connected passers should swiftly decide the outcome of the game.}) 10. Qf3 {A classic maneuver: the queen is ferried over to h3, where it exerts long-term pressure against the h7-pawn. Without the possibility of ...c7-c5, Black is consigned to total passivity and must defend very precisely merely to avoid getting checkmated in the next few moves.} Nb6 11. Qh3 {The ideal setup is now complete. We are exerting massive pressure on h7, and intend to follow up with the prosaic g4-g5. Amazingly, we have transposed to a position that arises in the 4...b5 line! White's attack is virtually unstoppable.} Ne4 (11... g6 {This creates massive holes on the kingside, which we can exploit by repositioning our bishop to a more active square.} 12. Bg5 Be7 13. Qh4 $16) (11... h6 {Of course, this merely throws more oil into the fire.} 12. g4 Ne4 13. g5 $1 {Ignoring the hanging knight on c3, which is non-essential to our attack. Unsurprisingly, given the superiority of our pieces, the tactics work out perfectly for White.} Nxg5 (13... Nxc3 14. Rg1 {It is important to appreciate the value of these types of moves. Even though White is down a piece, we are in no big hurry, and it is worth bringing in the big boys to ensure that the attack is decisive.} Ne4 15. gxh6 g6 16. Bxe4 {Eliminating Black's only active defender.} dxe4 17. h7+ Kh8 18. Rxg6 $1 {Smashing through Black's pitiful pawn shell.} fxg6 19. Nxg6+ Kg7 20. Qh6+ Kf7 21. Qh5 $1 {Another important quiet move, setting up the threat of a discovered check. The simplest threat is Nxf8+ followed by h8=Q. Black's king is forced back into the ring of fire.} Kg7 22. Nxf8 Qxf8 23. Bh6+ {And now the coup d'grace, skewering the queen!} Kh8 24. Bxf8 Rxf8 25. O-O-O $18 {[%cal Rd1g1,Rg1g8] Nominally, Black has a lot of material for the queen, but the minor pieces can only spectate as we get our rook to g1 and follow up with Qg4/g5/g6, setting up unstoppable threats along the g-file.}) 14. Bxg5 Qxg5 15. f4 {Forcing the queen off of the g-file and lending further support to the knight, taking the sting out of ...Bxe5.} Qe7 16. Rg1 Kh8 17. O-O-O {The point of this move is not to shuttle the king to safety; it was perfectly safe in the center. Rather, by connecting the rooks, we are setting up the unstoppable threat of a sacrifice on g7!} a5 (17... Rg8 18. Rg6 $1 Qf8 {Now, a beautiful sequence decides the game:} 19. Nxf7+ $1 Qxf7 20. Rxh6+ $1 gxh6 21. Qxh6+ Qh7 22. Qxh7#) 18. Rxg7 $3 Kxg7 19. Rg1+ Kh8 20. Qxh6# {This line demonstrates the incredibly potency of White's construction.}) 12. f3 $1 {Get out!} Nf6 (12... Nxc3 13. Qxh7#) 13. Bg5 g6 14. Qh6 Re8 15. O-O Be7 16. f4 $18 {[%cal Rf1f3,Rf3h3] The rook lift to h3 will lead to immediate checkmate. The extent of Black's queenside paralysis is rather picturesque.}) 5. a3 {An important inclusion. By preventing ...Bb4, we are making it difficult for Black to find active prospects for the dark-squared bishop. More importantly, we are preparing to take control of the queenside with a well-timed b2-b4, which will make it exceedingly difficult for Black to attain much-needed activity on the queenside.} c5 (5... b5 6. b4 {This very likely transposes to 4...b5 5.a3 Bb7 6.b4 e6. Of course, Black can also play this line without fianchettoing the light-squared bishop, but that hardly changes the general tenor of the game. White's plan will remain the same: to entrench a knight on e5, develop the light-squared bishop to d3, and light the kingside on fire by maneuvering the queen to h3.} Bd6 (6... Bb7 7. Nf3 {See 4...b5 5.a3 Bb7 6.b4 e6 7.Nf3.}) 7. Nf3 O-O 8. Bd3 Nbd7 9. Ne5 Nb6 10. Qf3 Bb7 (10... Nfd7 11. Qg3 Nxe5 12. dxe5 Be7 13. Bh6 g6 14. h4 $40 {In these types of positions, you almost never want to capture the rook on f8 because the bishop is such a valuable attacker. White is dominating on the kingside, but we do not even have to rush with h4-h5: an even more powerful continuation in response to 14...Kh8 would be to shuttle the knight around to d4 via e2.}) 11. Qh3 {Amazingly, we have transposed to a position that arises in the 4...b5 line! White's attack is virtually unstoppable.}) 6. Nf3 {For now, we keep the tension, awaiting a more opportune moment to trade on c5.} b5 {Black continues to expand on the queenside.} (6... Nc6 7. h3 {It is important to throw this move in to discourage ...Nh5.} Bd6 (7... b5 8. dxc5 {We cannot hold off on this move any longer, since Black was ready to play ...c4 or ...b4. We now proceed to quickly prepare the e4 pawn break.} Bxc5 9. Bd3 {Importantly, the knight on c6 hinders the development of Black's bishop, and makes it impossible for him to stop e4.} Bb7 10. O-O {Before pushing the e-pawn, we complete our deverlopment.} O-O 11. e4 {The time has come! Our pieces are perfectly placed to deal with the consequences of this breakthrough.} dxe4 {The most common response, resolving the central tension straightaway.} (11... d4 12. Nb1 {A very important idea; this is almost always the desirable response to ...d4. The knight is re-routed to d2, and it can later find a home on b3.} Rc8 13. Nbd2 h6 14. Nb3 Bb6 15. e5 {Staking a claim in the center and popping open the b1-h7 diagonal.} Nd5 16. Bh2 $14 {White is better for several reasons: 1) The pawn on d4 is a long-term weakness that cannot be easily eradicated; 2) We will proceed with Qe2-e4, a nasty idea that will induce a serious kingside weakness; 3) We have many auxiliary ways to improve our position, such as Rfd1 (after Qe2), Nb3-d2-e4-d6, and a timely a3-a4 can also be employed to underscore the hidden vulnerability of Black's queenside structure. In contrast, Black has no clear way to improve his position or cause problems on either side of the board.}) (11... h6 12. exd5 {This is quite a bit better than the tempting 12.e5, when Black drops the knight back to d7 and reopens the kingside with the nasty ...f7-f6.} Nxd5 13. Nxd5 Qxd5 14. Re1 {Moving the rook to a better spot and setting up the positional threat of Be4, which will dislodge Black's queen from its active post.} Rad8 (14... Nd4 $4 {This move looks scary, but it simply loses a piece:} 15. Be4 Nxf3+ 16. Qxf3 $18) 15. Be4 Qh5 (15... Qxd1 16. Raxd1 Rxd1 17. Rxd1 Rd8 18. Rxd8+ Nxd8 19. Bd3 {White has definite chances for an advantage: the idea is to expand on the queenside with b2-b4 and c2-c4, ultimately creating an outside passer. Of course, we do not fear ...Bxf3, which hands over the bishop pair and creates a long-term weakness on the light squares.}) 16. Qe2 Rfe8 {This position occurred in Bortnyk-Baryshpolets, Nabokov Memorial 2015.} 17. c3 {This is best, leading to a small edge. White controls the crucial d4-square.} e5 18. Be3 Bxe3 19. Qxe3 $14 {Our dark-square control, coupled with the nasty threat of Qb6, secures a very pleasant game for White. Black's queen on h5 is also somewhat out of commission.})) (7... cxd4 $6 8. exd4 {The position resembles a favorable version of an exchange Caro, with Black's light-squared bishop locked behind his pawns.}) 8. dxc5 {This forces Black to make another move with his dark-squared bishop, thereby losing a tempo.} Bxc5 (8... Bxf4 $6 {This is a very common response, but Black will be unable to easily recover the pawn.} 9. exf4 Qc7 (9... Qe7 10. b4 e5 11. Nxd5 Nxd5 12. Qxd5 exf4+ 13. Be2 O-O 14. Qd6 $1 Qe4 15. Qd3 Qe7 16. O-O Rd8 17. Qc4 $16) 10. Qd2 $14 {There is no way to win the c5-pawn, which can be supported with b2-b4 as necessary. Our queen can also park itself on e3.}) 9. Bd3 O-O (9... Bd6 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. O-O e5 12. e4 d4 13. Ne2 (13. Nd5 $5 {An innovative alternative, although this is quite unnecessary, since 13.Ne2 transposes into a line that leads to a very dangerous position for Black.} Nxd5 14. exd5 Qxd5 15. Re1 O-O (15... f6 16. c3 O-O 17. Be4 Qd6 18. Qd3 $14) 16. Be4 Qb5 17. Bxc6 bxc6 18. Rxe5 Qxb2 19. Qxd4 Qxd4 20. Nxd4 $40) 13... O-O 14. Ng3 {Transposes to 10...Bd6 11.Bxd6 Qxd6 12.e4 etc.}) 10. O-O {We see a virtually identical position in the 7...b5 line; the placement of the pawn on b7 does not change the nature of the position. We are ready to push e3-e4, posing Black with serious positional problems.} h6 (10... Bd6 11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. e4 d4 (12... dxe4 13. Nxe4 Nxe4 14. Bxe4 Qc7 (14... Rd8 $2 15. Qxd6 Rxd6 16. Rfd1 Rxd1+ 17. Rxd1 Kf8 18. Bxc6 bxc6 19. Ne5 c5 20. Rd8+ Ke7 21. Rg8 g6 22. Rh8 h5 23. f4 $18) 15. Qe2 $14) 13. Ne2 e5 14. Ng3 Be6 15. Qd2 {This position epitomizes the power of the Jobava London, especially with respect to its ability to puzzle even the strongest engines. Stockfish 15, upon reflection, gives equality with a very small preference to Black. But if you look at White's online results, they are staggering (I myself have won almost all my games, including a crushing win over Nepomniatchtchi - O.B.). This is because White has a very clear plan: to maneuver the f3-knight to h4 (and later to f5) and to blast open the f-file with f2-f4. Black, on the other hand, does not have any clear plan of action on the queenside, since the b4-square is guarded and the c2-pawn is well-defended. If this explanation doesn't convince you, perhaps the subsequent analysis will!} h6 16. Nh4 Ne7 17. f4 $14 exf4 18. Rxf4 Nd7 19. Raf1 g5 (19... Ne5 20. Nh5 Rac8 21. Rf6 N5g6 22. Nf5 $18 {Bortnyk-Nepomniatchtchi, Titled Tuesday (blitz) 2022.}) 20. Nhf5 $16 Nxf5 21. Nxf5 Bxf5 22. Rxf5 $18) 11. e4 d4 (11... Re8 12. e5 Nd7 13. Qd2 Bf8 14. Rfe1 $14) (11... dxe4 12. Nxe4 Nxe4 13. Bxe4 Qf6 (13... Qxd1 14. Rfxd1 $16 {[%cal Rf4d6]}) 14. Qc1 e5 15. Bg3 Re8 16. Re1 $14 {[%csl Re5][%cal Re4c6,Rf3e5]}) 12. Ne2 Qe7 13. e5 Nd5 14. Re1 $16 {Black is in a vise-like grip. The bishop can be maneuvered to h4, and White can follow up by amputating the d5-knight with Qd2 and Nf4.}) (6... cxd4 7. exd4 Bd6 8. Bxd6 Qxd6 9. Bd3 O-O 10. O-O Nc6 11. Re1 {White has a small but stable advantage, much akin to the Exchange Caro where Black has closed in his bishop with a premature ...e6. We will inevitably ensconce on our knight on e5 and follow up by building up the kingside pressure.}) (6... Nh5 $6 {This is somewhat premature, since the bishop has a suitable escape square on g5.} 7. Bg5 Be7 8. Bxe7 Qxe7 {With the dark-squared bishop gone, it is a perfect opportunity to resolve the tension in the center and launch a furious kingside offensive.} 9. dxc5 Qxc5 10. g4 $1 Nf6 11. g5 $1 Nfd7 12. e4 $16 {Blasting open the center, after which our knight will sink its teeth into the juicy d6-square!}) 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. Bd3 {We follow up with the typical developing move to d3, preparing e3-e4.} Bb7 {Without question, Black's setup constitues one of the more problematic lines of play against the Jobava London. Black makes it very difficult for us to open up the center with e3-e4 (in contrast to lines with an early ...Nc6, when it is quite a bit easier to carry out this break) while developing his minor pieces to very active posts. In order to avoid getting squeezed, we must insist on pushing e4 as quickly as possible.} 9. Qe2 Nbd7 (9... Nc6 {This move somewhat contradicts the purpose of Black's overall setup, which is to discourage e3-e4. Still, it is a natural developing move and must be taken seriously.} 10. O-O O-O 11. e4 d4 12. Nb1 (12. e5 $2 {This is tempting, and it works on certain occasions (usually with the queen on d1), but here it fails due to the unfortunate placement of White's queen:} dxc3 13. exf6 Qxf6 14. Bg5 Nd4 $1 $17 {White's queen trap falls apart.}) 12... Nh5 13. Bg5 f6 14. Bc1 {It appears that our pieces are all moving backwards, but we have in fact provoked serious, potentially decisive, weaknesses on both sides of the board. We currently threaten to trap the knight with g2-g4, forcing Black to overextend himself even further.} e5 15. a4 {Poking at the weak b5-pawn, and opening up the c4-square for our bishop.} b4 16. Ng5 {Let the party begin!} fxg5 (16... Nf4 17. Bxf4 exf4 18. Bc4+ Kh8 19. Nxh7 $18) 17. Qxh5 h6 {It turns out that this ultra-natural move is a mistake, but find us a human player who will refrain from this move!} 18. h4 $1 gxh4 19. Bxh6 $1 gxh6 20. Qg6+ Kh8 21. Qxh6+ Kg8 22. Bc4+ Rf7 23. Qg6+ $18) (9... O-O 10. e4 {Now, the top computer move is 10...e5, which we refuse to take seriously because no one in their right mind would ever chance upon such a move. Barring that, the position is equal objectively, but definitely easier to play for White.} dxe4 (10... d4 11. Nb1 Nc6 {Now, 12.0-0 transposes into a position that we reach via 9...Nc6 10.0-0 0-0 11.e4 d4 12.Nb1, but we have a superior move:} 12. e5 Nd5 13. Qe4 g6 14. Bh6 Re8 15. Nbd2 $14 {with excellent kingside prospects connected to the idea of opening the h-file with h2-h4.}) (10... e5 11. Bxe5 Nbd7 12. Bxf6 Nxf6 13. e5 Re8 14. O-O {Black doesn't recover the pawn easily, and must continue to make computer moves.} Ng4 $2 15. h3 $1 Nxe5 16. Nxe5 Qc7 17. Qh5 g6 18. Nxd5 $3 Qxe5 (18... Bxd5 19. Nxg6 hxg6 20. Qxd5 $16) 19. Qxe5 Rxe5 20. Nf6+ Kg7 21. Nd7 Rd5 22. Nxc5 Rxc5 23. Rfd1 $16) 11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Bxe4 Bxe4 13. Qxe4 Nd7 14. O-O {Transposes to 9...Nbd7 10.e4, etc.}) 10. e4 {Despite Black's best efforts, we have succeeded in pushing e4, leading virtually by force to a series of piece exchanges in the center. The resulting position may appear rather lifeless, but upon closer inspection, it turns out that White certainly has the easier practical game.} dxe4 11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Bxe4 Bxe4 13. Qxe4 {The position has simplified considerably, and the symmetrical material may lead you to believe that the position is devoid of serious winning chances. That could not be further from the truth! Black's queenside is rather weak, and the pawn on b5 is more of a liability than an asset. White's play is ultra straightforward: we will castle and quickly position our rooks on d1 and e1, when White's superior pieces grant us easy practical play.} O-O 14. O-O {Transposes to 9...Nbd7 10.e4, etc.} Nf6 15. Qe2 Qd5 16. Rfe1 Rad8 17. h3 h6 18. Be5 Nd7 19. Rad1 {Jobava London-3...a6  2023 White has an indisputable initiative, with control over the center and a better-placed bishop. Black must continue to make precise moves to keep equality.}) (3... e6 {This is the most popular move on Lichess, and it is the one you are likely to face at least half the time. Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and establishes a firmer presence in the center.} 4. Nb5 {This is a very important move that forces Black to make a positional concession. Black must either give away the bishop pair and damage his pawn structure with 4...Bd6, or permanently misplace his knight with 4...Na6. In the latter case, Black will push away the knight with ...c6, but the knight from a6 will still have a very hard time rejoining the game.} Bd6 {There is no question that this is the worst of Black's three main lines, but it is the most popular by a country mile.} (4... Na6 {This is the most straightforward response. Black defends the c7-pawn without giving away the bishop pair. In this line, the strategic potency of Nb5 is on full display: the knight on a6 will have a very difficult time rejoining society: the shortest path involves ferrying the knight around to d6 via c7 and e8, which already takes a staggering number of moves. Our general approach is to quickly complete our kingside development. After we do that...okay, how about we cross that bridge when we get there!} 5. e3 {Black is at a further crossroads. He can chase the knight away immediately with 5...c6, focus on kingside development with 5...Be7, or strike at the center straightaway with 5...c5. All of these eventualities must be carefully considered - each leads to a fundamentally different type of battle.} Be7 {The main move at a high level. Black retains maximum flexibility, developing his kingside and reserving the possibility to chase away White's knight at a more convenient moment.} (5... c6 6. Nc3 {It is quite clear that our journey to b5 has been anything but a waste of time. Not only is Black's knight awkwardly placed, but it is outright vulnerable: Black must now reckon with the positional threat of Bxa6, permanently ruining Black's queenside structure and creating a massive hole on c5 that we can easily access with our knight.} Bd6 (6... Bb4 7. Bxa6 {We certainly don't miss the opportunity to create massive, long-term weaknesses on the queenside.} bxa6 8. Nf3 {Calmly continuing our development. It is important to remember that the combination of ...Bb4 and ...Ne4 is almost never scary in the Jobava: we almost always respond by castling, giving up the c3-pawn but attaining a massive initiative in return. In this case, we will also attain total positional domination on the queenside.} O-O (8... Qa5 9. O-O {As with 8...Ne4, we are more than happy to give up a pawn in exchange for full control over the dark squares.} Bxc3 10. bxc3 Qxc3 11. Rb1 $1 {Seizing the b-file and preventing Black from deploying the bishop to b7.} O-O 12. Bd6 {Striking while the iron is hot!} Re8 13. Bb4 Qc4 {The queen has been forced onto a more vulnerable square, enabling our knight to jump into the action.} 14. Ne5 Qb5 15. c4 $1 {Now, another pawn is temporarily sacrificed in order to tear open the queenside and increase the power of our initiative.} dxc4 16. Bc5 Qa5 17. Qf3 $18 {Black is absolutely screwed: he can barely move, his queenside is collapsing, and our pieces are on optimal squares. This line really showcases the multi-faceted nature of the Jobava London: it is an opening that promises its adherents not just tactical victories, but strategic masterpieces as well!}) (8... Ne4 9. O-O Nxc3 10. bxc3 Bxc3 11. Rb1 {In contrast to the 8...Qa5 line, here Black keeps the dark-squared bishop, but it is unable to move back to the a3-f8 diagonal. As such, White still holds complete domination over the dark squares, and the imminent prospect of Ne5 will push Black over the edge.} O-O (11... f6 {Preventing Ne5 does more harm than good:} 12. Qd3 Ba5 13. Qa3 {Preventing Black from castling, and reinforcing our control over key dark squares along the a3-f8 diagonal.} Bc7 (13... Bb6 14. c4 $1 {We saw a similar move in the 8...Qa5 line (see White's 15th move). If the pawn is captured, Black's entire queenside will be in tatters; if it isn't, the pawn will deliver a further blow to Black's defensive capacity once it reaches c5.} Kf7 15. c5 Bc7 16. Bd6 $1 {And now, we entrench the bishop on the sexiest outpost on the board!} Re8 17. e4 $1 {A lovely positional sacrifice, giving up the center pawn in order to carve out a devastating path for our knight.} dxe4 18. Nd2 f5 19. Nc4 Kg8 20. Ne5 {It took us a while, but we finally got our knight on a perfect square! c6 is hanging, and Black has no choice but to allow the infiltration of our rook.} Bd7 21. Rb7 $18 {This line is worth reviewing several times. Note that White sacrifices three pawns, and all three sacrifices are entirely positional in nature. First, the c3-pawn is sacrificed to spark the flames of the initiative. Then, after the queen is ferried to a3, we open up the queenside with c2-c4. Once Black declines the pawn, it is driven to c5, and we finish with a sacrifice that enables us to carve out a devastating pathway for our knight. A certified Jobava Classic!}) 14. Bxc7 Qxc7 15. c4 Qe7 16. Qa4 $16) 12. Ne5 Bd7 (12... Qe8 {Defending the pawn with the queen prevents Rb7, but opens Black up to a devastating attack on the kingside.} 13. Rb3 {It is worth flicking in this move, which forces Black's bishop on an even more awkward square.} Ba5 14. Qg4 {Suddenly, we transition from the queenside to the kingside! It turns out that our previous move had a hidden aim: White now intends the gorgeous e3-e4, opening up a pathway from the queenside to the kingside for our rook! In addition, we threaten the banal Bh6, forcing Black to burn a crucial tempo.} Kh8 (14... f6 15. Bh6 $18) 15. e4 $1 {There it is! We saw this same move in the 11...f6 sub-variation, although it was played for an entirely different reason (to get the knight to e5). Here, the knight is already on e5, and this move is played in order to lift the rook over to the kingside.} dxe4 (15... f5 16. Qh4 Bd8 17. Bg5 Bxg5 18. Qxg5 {Note that total dominance of our knight over Black's entire army!} fxe4 19. Rg3 Rg8 {The king is now boxed in, and a beautiful mating pattern seals the deal:} (19... g6 20. h4 Rf5 21. Nxg6+ Qxg6 22. Qd8+ $18) 20. Ng6+ Qxg6 21. Qxg6 hxg6 22. Rh3#) 16. Rg3 g6 17. Qh4 $18 {with endless threats against Black's king, all of them unstoppable.}) 13. Rb7 Be8 14. Qg4 $18 {We could keep going, but we think you'll be able to take it from here! White dominates on both sides of the board.}) (8... a5 {A sensible move, preparing to deploy the light-squared bishop on a6. Still, it does not address the gross structural problems on the queenside, and our response is rather straightforward.} 9. O-O {Obviously, it is important to castle before Black brings the bishop to a6.} Ba6 (9... Bxc3 10. bxc3 Ba6 11. Re1 Ne4 {Naroditsky-Makhnyov, Chess.com (blitz) 2023. Now, the natural response is 12.Ne5, but it is beat out by a scintillating positional sacrifice:} 12. c4 $1 Bxc4 (12... O-O 13. c5 f6 14. Nd2 Nc3 15. Qg4 Re8 16. a4) 13. Ne5 O-O (13... Bb5 14. c4 dxc4 (14... Bxc4 15. Rc1 O-O 16. Nxc4 dxc4 17. Rxc4 Qd5 18. Qc2 Rfc8 19. Rc1 $16) 15. a4 Ba6 16. Qf3 Qd5 17. Bh6 $3 gxh6 (17... O-O 18. Qg4 g6 19. f3 Nd6 20. Nd7 $18 {[%cal Rd7f6,Rd7f8]}) 18. Qxf7+ Kd8 19. f3 Ng5 20. Qf6+ Kc7 21. Qg7+ Kb6 22. Nd7+ Kc7 23. Nf6+ $18) 14. f3 Nd6 (14... Nf6 15. e4 $14) 15. Nxc6 Qc7 16. Bxd6 Qxd6 17. Nxa5 Rfc8 18. Nb7 Qb4 19. Nc5 Qc3 20. Qc1 Rab8 21. Rd1 $14 {[%cal Rc1d2]}) 10. Re1 O-O (10... Ne4 {As usual, this move is futile, but this time for a more tactical reason:} 11. Nxe4 {Exploiting the uncastled nature of Black's king!} Bxe1 (11... dxe4 12. Nd2 O-O 13. c3 Be7 14. Nxe4 $18) 12. Nd6+ $18 {We pick up the bishop on the next move, with a decisive material advantage.}) 11. Ne5 Rc8 12. Bg5 $1 {An important idea, that re-occurs several times throughout this course. While this pin may appear empty, it in fact causes Black very serious headaches. We threaten to capture the knight on f6 and follow up with a fork on d7, an idea that is often overlooked even by extremely strong grandmasters. If Black drops the bishop back to e7, then the c3-knight is liberated, and we can establish our control over the c5-square with Na4.} h6 13. Bxf6 gxf6 {A sad necessity, but this creates long-term weaknesses on the kingside.} (13... Qxf6 14. Nd7 $18) 14. Nd3 Bd6 15. e4 $14) 9. O-O a5 (9... Bxc3 $6 10. bxc3 Ne4 11. c4 dxc4 12. Ne5 Qd5 13. f3 Nf6 14. e4 $18) (9... c5 10. Na4 cxd4 (10... c4 11. c3 Be7 12. b3 cxb3 (12... Nh5 13. Bg3 Nxg3 14. hxg3 Qc7 15. Qc2 Bd7 16. Nb2 cxb3 (16... Bb5 17. bxc4 Bxc4 18. Nxc4 Qxc4 19. Nd2 Qc7 20. c4 dxc4 21. Qxc4 Qxc4 22. Nxc4 $14) 17. axb3 Rab8 18. Ne5 Be8 19. Ned3 a5 20. c4 $14) 13. axb3 Nh5 14. Bg3 Nxg3 15. hxg3 Qc7 16. Qc2 $16 {[%cal Rc3c4]}) 11. exd4 Bd7 12. c3 Be7 13. Nc5 $14 Bxc5 14. dxc5 Ne4 15. b4 Nxc3 16. Qd3 Ne4 17. Qxa6 Qc8 18. Qa5 $14) 10. Ne5 c5 11. Re1 Ba6 12. Bg5 $1 {We saw a very similar idea in the 8...a5 variation, with the minor difference that Black's rook was on a8 and the pawn was still on c6. These cosmetic differences change very little: White still threatens Bxf6 followed by Nd7, and White is still very much incapable of dealing with the problems that await him on both sides of the board.} cxd4 (12... h6 13. Nc6 {A pretty in-betweener, driving the queen away from d8 and setting up Bxf6.} Qd7 14. Bxf6 gxf6 (14... Qxc6 15. Qg4 g6 16. Qh4 Kh7 17. a3 Bxc3 18. bxc3 $40) 15. Nxb4 cxb4 16. Nb1 Kh7 17. c3 $14 {White is better on both sides of the board!}) 13. exd4 Be7 {Instead of the regular 14.Na4, here we have a tactic that leads to immediate victory:} 14. Nc6 Qd7 15. Nxe7+ Qxe7 16. Nxd5 $18) (6... Nc7 {With this move, Black takes away the possibility of Bxa6, but the knight is hardly in a better place on c7. We follow up in typical fashion, developing our knight to f3 and bishop to d3.} 7. Nf3 Bb4 (7... Bd6 8. Ne5 {See typical idea #1: the knight is once again placed between the two bishops!} O-O (8... Qe7 9. Qf3 {Yet another textbook move, preparing to lift the queen up to h3 in the event that Black castles. If Black tries to root out the knight on e5 straightaway, a secondary point of this move is revealed:} Nd7 10. Nxf7 $1 {Boom shaka-laka! The queen on f3 renders ...Kxf7 impossible, and Black has no choice but to part ways with a very important pawn.} Bxf4 11. Qxf4 {An important detail: the myopic 11.Nxh8 led to a far more double-edged position, since we would run the risk of losing the knight.} Qxf7 12. Qxc7 O-O 13. f4 {Defending the pawn and preventing ...e5!} Qe8 14. O-O-O Rf7 15. Qd6 $18) 9. Bd3 {We have completed our minor piece development, and are fully ready to initiate aggressive action on the kingside. We will do so by flying our queen to h3, and, if necessary, supplementing with g4-g5. Black's main issue is that he has nothing going on the queenside, giving us a free hand on the other side of the board. The power of the knight on e5 can hardly be overstated: it lords over the entire position, making it virtually impossible for Black to get anything going in the way of counterplay.} c5 {The most logical move: Black initiates counterplay in the center and aims to undermine our knight. As it turns out, we are more than happy to go along!} (9... Qe7 {A sensible attempt to prepare ...Nd7 by protecting the bishop. However, it is much to slow, and the knight on e5 is so well-defended that Black will not possibly be able to get it to move!} 10. Qf3 Nd7 {Here, Black puts more immediate pressure on the knight, so it is worth amending the queen's path to lend the knight further support:} 11. Qg3 Nxe5 (11... Bxe5 12. dxe5 $16) (11... Ne8 {Now, we engineer a curious queen shuffle to provoke even more weaknesses in Black's kingside.} 12. Qh3 g6 13. Qg3 {Black is virtually paralyzed, and any attempt to free himself leads to immediate calamity:} f6 14. Bxg6 $1 {This was the reason for inducing ...g6!} hxg6 15. Nxg6 Qg7 16. Bxd6 Nxd6 17. Ne7+ $1 Kf7 18. Qxd6 $18 {Picking off the knight and defending our own! Of course, the knight on e7 has a very convenient escape hatch through c8! The capture on g2 is utterly innocuous and can be met with 0-0-0.}) 12. dxe5 Bb4 13. Bg5 Qe8 (13... Qd7 14. Qh4 Bxc3+ 15. bxc3 h6 16. g4 $1 Ne8 17. Bxh6 gxh6 18. g5 $18) 14. Bf6 $18) (9... Nd7 $2 {This loses immediately to a familiar tactic:} 10. Nxf7 Rxf7 11. Bxd6 Ne8 12. Bg3 $18) 10. dxc5 {The knight on e5 no longer requires the support of the d4-pawn, since the bishop does the job perfectly well.} Bxc5 11. Qf3 {Now, it is time for typical idea #5: the queen is ferried over to h3 before initiating a furious kingside storm.} Bd6 (11... d4 12. exd4 Bxd4 13. O-O-O $1 Ncd5 14. Bxh7+ Kxh7 15. Rxd4 $18) 12. Qh3 g6 13. g4 $40 {Having induced serious weaknesses on the kingside, we move on to the pawn storm phase. Our next step is to shift the queen to g3 in order to leave open the path for the h-pawn.} Nce8 (13... Nd7 14. Nxg6 $1 fxg6 15. Bxd6 $18) 14. Qg3 Qc7 15. O-O-O {It is always important to be patient in the Jobava London, and this line is no exception. Before rolling up our sleeves on the kingside, we ensure the safety of our king (and threaten Nb5).} a6 16. h4 Nd7 17. Nxd7 Bxd7 18. Bxd6 Nxd6 19. h5 $40 {While the computer will likely hold off the attack, we believe that the position is totally untenable from a human perspective. We will either open the h-file with hxg6 or, in the event of ...g5, smash open the kingside with f2-f4.}) 8. Bd3 Nb5 9. O-O {As always, we are more than willing to part ways with a pawn in order to achieve a harmonious setup and gain control of key queenside squares (typical idea #8).} Nxc3 10. bxc3 Bxc3 11. Rb1 O-O 12. Bg5 {A familiar idea, establishing a very nasty pin against Black's knight.} h6 13. Bh4 $44 {White's compensation is absolutely massive: Black has no good way to end the pin, since ...g5 creates fatal weaknesses on the kingside and ...Qc7 allows us to capture on f6. We can follow up by intensifying the pressure with Ne5 and f2-f4, followed by a rook or queen lift to the kingside. On the queenside, Black has no active prospects and no good way to activate the light-squared bishop.}) (6... Nb8 {This knight transfer is incredibly inefficient. Black will eventually re-route the knight to a relatively active square, but the tempi that he burns in the process will grant us an excellent game.} 7. Nf3 Bb4 8. Bd3 c5 9. dxc5 Nbd7 10. O-O Nxc5 (10... Bxc3 11. bxc3 Nxc5 12. Ne5 O-O 13. Bg5 $14) 11. Nb5 {Black has spent endless tempi to get the knight to an active square, only to be sent back to a6!} Na6 (11... O-O 12. Nc7 Rb8 13. Nxd5 $1 $18 {Winning material.})) 7. Bxa6 {Typical idea #4: we do not miss an opportunity to cripple Black's queenside pawn structure and create a massive hole on c5.} bxa6 (7... Bxf4 8. Bxb7 {A nice desperado move to pick up a pawn.} Bxb7 9. exf4 $14) 8. Nf3 c5 {Pushing the pawn may eradicate the weakness on c6, but it does not rid us of the outpost on c5.} (8... Bxf4 9. exf4 {We are almost always happy to achieve the d4-f4 pawn bind, which resembles a Maroczy Bind shifted one file to the right.} O-O 10. Na4 Ne4 {The knight on e4 appears imposing, but it can be kicked away at our convenience with f2-f3.} (10... Qa5+ 11. c3 $16) (10... Nd7 11. O-O Qc7 12. Ne5 f6 13. Nxd7 Bxd7 14. Nc5 Rae8 15. Qg4 $16) 11. O-O Qd6 12. Ne5 f6 13. Nd3 $16 {The knight has been ferried over to an ideal square. Now, both knights control c5, and we threaten to win the knight with f2-f3. The position plays itself: Nac5 can be followed by Re1, when Black is sinking under the weight of his endless weaknesses.}) (8... Rb8 {Attacking b2 does not change our plans in the slightest, since we were planning to play Na4 anyway.} 9. Na4 Bxf4 10. exf4 c5 11. Nxc5 Rxb2 12. O-O $16) 9. Bxd6 Qxd6 10. Na4 cxd4 11. exd4 Ne4 12. Ne5 O-O (12... f6 $2 13. Qh5+ g6 14. Nxg6 $18) 13. f3 Bd7 14. c3 Nf6 15. Nc5 $16) (5... c5 $6 {As usual, this move makes very little sense, since it enables the knight to remain on b5 forever.} 6. c3 Be7 7. a4 {It is always a good idea to reinforce the knight, taking the sting out of ...Bd7.} Bd7 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Bd3 Nh5 10. Be5 f6 {Now, rather than the meek 11.g3, we can exploit the vulnerability of Black's knight with a lovely tactic:} 11. Ng5 $1 g6 12. Nxh7 $1 {Now, 12...Kxh7 leads to rapid checkmate after 13.Qxh5, so Black must part ways with a lot of material and give up the final pawn which provides shelter to the king.} fxe5 13. Nxf8 Qxf8 14. Bxg6 Ng7 15. Qg4 Be8 16. dxe5 $18 {with three pawns and a rook for two pieces, as well as domination on tthe queenside and the straightforward idea of pushing the h-pawn all the way to h6.}) (5... Bb4+ {An interesting move, inducing c2-c3 in order to take away our knight's typical retreating square. However, we are able to find alternate arrangements for our knight just in time.} 6. c3 Be7 (6... Ba5 7. Qa4 $18) 7. a4 {This is not played to reinforce the knight, so much as to establish contact between the rook and the a3-square, rendering ...Bxa3 ineffective once the knight retreats.} O-O 8. Bd3 c6 9. Na3 c5 10. Nb5 {Right back to home base!} Bd7 11. Nf3 c4 12. Bb1 {We have achieved our ideal minor piece setup, and can improve our position in a litany of ways.} Qb6 (12... Ne4 13. Bxe4 dxe4 14. Nd2 $16) (12... Bxb5 13. axb5 Nc7 14. Qc2 Nxb5 15. Ne5 Nd6 (15... g6 16. h4 Nh5 17. Bh6 Re8 18. g4 Nf6 19. f3 $40) 16. f3) 13. Qc2 {Needless to say, we could not care less about giving up a measly pawn on the queenside. With this move, we set up a queen-bishop battery, which will likely lead to a swift and decisive attack against Black's king.} Bxb5 14. axb5 Qxb5 15. Ng5 g6 16. h4 {Typical idea #3: we rush the h-pawn up the board, and Black has too many weaknesses and not enough defenders on the kingside to do anything about it.} Qb3 17. Qe2 $1 {Obviously, we do not even think about trading queens!} h6 18. Nf3 Kg7 19. h5 g5 20. Nxg5 {A thematic piece sacrifice, smashing open the kingside. With the queen entirely out of commission on b3, Black cannot lend nearly enough reinforcement to the kingside and the attack will end the game in a matter of a few moves.} hxg5 21. h6+ Kh8 22. Be5 Nb8 23. Ra3 Qb6 24. Bxf6+ Bxf6 25. Qc2 $18 {Rebuilding the battery! This time, Black has no pawn to put on g6, and the bishop blocks the f-pawn. Checkmate is coming on the next move.}) 6. Nf3 O-O 7. h4 $5 {A very interesting move favored by GM Arjun Erigaisi, one of the leading specialists of this line. White's idea could not possibly be more straightforward: we want to checkmate Black on the kingside! As a bonus, this move opens up an escape square for the bishop on h2, which takes the sting out of a knight jump to h5. The engine is slow to approve of this move, but careful analysis shows that Black's position is incredibly difficult to handle.} c6 (7... c5 8. c3 Bd7 9. a4 {As always, this kind of position favors White, since the knight on b5 cannot be easily driven away.} c4 (9... Ne4 10. Bd3 c4 11. Bxe4 dxe4 12. Nd2 $16 {Picking up a pawn.}) 10. b3 {Chipping away at Black's pawn chain. Now, 10...cxb3 enables us to develop our bishop to an ideal square, while ...Rc8 drops the pawn on a7. Black has only one other way to try to maintain the pawn on c4, but it fails as well.} Qa5 {Hoping to meet bxc4 with ...Bxb5 followed by ...Qxc3+. But we have a lovely positional move up our sleeve:} (10... cxb3 11. Bd3 b2 12. Rb1 Ne4 13. Rxb2 $16) 11. Nd2 $146 {Gorgeous. By covering the e1-a5 diagonal, we ensure that ...Qxc3 does not come with check, so 11...Bxb5 no longer works. After the trade on b3 (which is forced, since we threaten to take on c4), the queen will be driven back to b6 and we will finally be able to deploy our light-squared bishop to d3.} cxb3 12. Nxb3 Qb6 (12... Qd8 13. Bd3 $14) 13. Nd2 {It is a good idea to shuttle our knight back to d2 in order to reinforce the e4-square and open up the b-file.} Rfc8 14. Bd3 Bxb5 15. axb5 Rxc3 {It appears that Black has gotten the better of us tactically, but yet another scintillating retreating knight move proves otherwise.} 16. Nb1 $3 {Simply divine! The knight moves back to its initial square, establishing contact between the queen and bishop, and facing Black with two threats at the same time. Black has nothing better than to give up the exchange, when his compensation will be woefully insufficient.} Rxd3 17. Qxd3 Nb4 18. Qe2 Rc8 19. O-O {Our king has been ferried to safety, and Black has nothing going on on the queenside.} Rc2 20. Qd1 $16) (7... Ne4 {This move looks like a sensible way to take away the c3-square, but in fact it takes away nothing, since Nc3 is still very much possible; the subsequent damage that we suffer on the queenside is nothing more than cosmetic. Furthermore, the knight on f6 was integral to the long-term safety of the king, and with the knight gone, we have every chance to develop a quick and decisive kingside attack.} 8. Bd3 c6 9. Nc3 Bb4 10. O-O Nxc3 11. bxc3 Bxc3 {Should we meekly shift the rook to b1? Absolutely not! With the knight no longer on f6, the correct idea should be second nature to a seasoned tactician.} 12. Bxh7+ {Greek gift time! Yes, seemingly every idea has its moment in the sun in the Jobava London. This move decides the game instantly.} Kxh7 13. Ng5+ Kg8 14. Qh5 Re8 15. Qxf7+ {A classic concept, removing the pawn from f7 before returning to h5 and delivering check on h7. Black is mated by force.} Kh8 16. Qh5+ Kg8 17. Qh7+ Kf8 18. Qh8+ Ke7 19. Qxg7#) 8. Nc3 c5 (8... Qa5 9. Be2 Ne4 10. O-O Nxc3 11. bxc3 Qxc3 {Yet again, we are happy to part ways with a pawn (typical idea #8).} 12. h5 h6 (12... c5 13. h6 g6 14. Rb1 c4 15. e4 dxe4 16. Ne5 $16) 13. Bxa6 bxa6 14. Rb1 $16 {[%csl Rc8] We dominate on both sides of the board, and can follow up with Ne5.}) (8... Nc7 9. Bd3 Nh5 $4 (9... h6 10. Ne5 $18 {g4-g5 will be utterly crushing.}) (9... c5 10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. g4 $1 Nxg4 (11... Bb4 12. g5 Ne4 13. Bxe4 dxe4 14. Qxd8 Rxd8 15. Bxc7 Rd7 16. Ne5 $1 Rxc7 17. O-O-O Be7 18. Nxe4 $16) 12. Bxh7+ {This isn't a greek gift sacrifice, but rather an ingenious way to weaken the king permanently and win back the pawn.} Kxh7 13. Ng5+ Kg8 14. Qxg4 e5 15. Qh5 Bf5 16. Bxe5 f6 17. Bxc7 Qxc7 18. Nf3 g6 19. Rg1 Qg7 {It appears that our queen is trapped, but we have a nice way to trade it off for its counterpart.} 20. Qxf5 gxf5 21. Rxg7+ Kxg7 22. Nxd5 $14 {We have two pawns for the exchange, and Black's incredibly weak kingside pawns will give us plenty of targets. Furthermore, the h4-pawn - once an attacker - has now become a fearsome passed pawn which will keep Black's rooks at bay.}) 10. Bxh7+ Kxh7 11. Ng5+ Kg6 12. Be5 {Black's king is caught in the crossfire of a lovely attack that quickly results in mate.} f6 13. Qd3+ f5 14. g4 Nf6 15. h5+ Kxg5 16. Bf4+ Kxg4 17. Qe2#) 9. h5 {Driving the pawn down to h5 and creating the rather nasty threat of h5-h6, which will provoke long-term weaknesses on the kingside.} h6 (9... Bd7 10. h6 g6 11. Bxa6 bxa6 12. dxc5 Bxc5 13. Bg5 Be7 14. Qd4 $18 {Dark-square domination! Black is utterly paralyzed.}) 10. g4 {Time to rip open the g-file! White's attack proceeds at a furious pace, while Black has very little going on the other side of the board.} Nxg4 (10... cxd4 11. exd4 Ne4 (11... Nxg4 12. Rg1 f5 13. Qd2 Bd7 14. O-O-O $40) 12. Bd3 Nxc3 13. bxc3 f6 (13... Bd7 14. Rb1 Qc8 15. g5 $1 hxg5 16. Bxg5 Bxg5 17. Nxg5 Qxc3+ 18. Kf1 $18) 14. Qd2 e5 15. dxe5 Bxg4 16. Rg1 $1 f5 (16... Bxf3 17. Bxh6 g5 $8 (17... Rf7 18. e6 $18) 18. Qe3 Bxh5 19. Qh3 Qe8 20. Bxf8 Kxf8 (20... Bxf8 21. Qf5 Qf7 22. exf6 $18) 21. Rh1 $18) 17. Nd4 $18) 11. Rg1 f5 12. Ne5 {The g4-knight is the last obstacle standing between White and Black's monarch. Note that we are not just playing on the kingside: the long-term threat of a well-timed capture on a6 is yet another headache that Black must manage.} Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Bf6 14. Bxf6 Qxf6 15. Bxa6 {It is the perfect opportunity to capture on a6, since we will follow up by capturing on c5 and opening up the d4-square for our queen.} bxa6 (15... cxd4 16. Rg6 $1 Qe7 (16... Qh4 17. Qxd4 Qxd4 18. exd4 bxa6 19. Kd2 $16) 17. Qxd4 bxa6 18. O-O-O $16) 16. dxc5 Bd7 17. Qd4 Be8 18. Ne2 Bxh5 19. Nf4 Bg4 20. Nd3 $16 {[%csl Re5] Despite the loss of a pawn, White maintains complete dominance of the key e5-square, which will be used as an outpost for the knight. This, coupled with the passed pawn on c5, gives White overwhelming compensation for the pawn.}) (4... Bb4+ {An ingenious way to defend the c7-pawn: the bishop travels back to a5. This is the least popular of Black's three alternatives, yet it is the choice of many top-level players and, in our opinion, constitutes Black's best objective challenge to 4.Nb5. We spent countless hours on this move, and came up with several fascinating ideas that confront Black with serious challenges. The resulting positions are convoluted, so we will try to carefully explain White's main ideas with text and illustrative variations.} 5. c3 {The first step is obvious: we block the check with our pawn, sending the bishop back to a5.} Ba5 6. a4 $1 {A clever, dastardly resource! Rather than putting our cards on the table with the immediate 6.b4, we set up the threat of b2-b4 and a4-a5, which will trap the bishop on b6. Black must respond by attempting to chase away our knight.} a6 {A critical position. It may appear that White's queenside play has ground to a complete halt, but the party is only beginning.} (6... c6 {This is a more desirable way to chase away the knight from a strategic point of view, but it contains a fatal tactical flaw: Black grants us access to the d6-square!}) 7. b4 $1 {Counterattacking the bishop! This is the first of many fantastic tactical moves that pose serious problems for Black on the queenside.} axb5 {The most popular move.} (7... Bb6 8. a5 axb5 (8... Bxa5 9. Rxa5 $18) 9. axb6 Rxa1 (9... Na6 10. e3 c6 11. Bd3 Qxb6 12. Nf3 $44 {White has overwhelming compensation for the pawn.}) 10. Qxa1 Na6 (10... cxb6 $4 {Believe it or not, over a thousand people on Lichess made this terrible blunder!} 11. Bxb8 $18) 11. bxc7 Nxc7 12. Qa5 $16) (7... Bxb4 {A tricky desperado sacrifice: it is crucial to refrain from 8.Nxc7+ due to 8...Qxc7, and Black regains the queen with an extra piece.} 8. cxb4 axb5 9. axb5 Rxa1 10. Qxa1 {White has three main advantages: control over the a-file, the bishop pair, and, most importantly, the doubled pawns, which in this position are a major asset. The pawn on b5 fully restricts Black's knight, and threatens to advance to b6. From a practical standpoint, there is no question that White's game is much easier.} O-O (10... Qe7 11. Qa3 {We defend the pawn while maintaining control over the a-file. In addition, 11.Qc3 would run into 11...Ne4.} Nh5 12. Bg3 Nxg3 13. hxg3 O-O 14. e3 {White's position is preferrable:we have quite a bit more space, long-term pressure down the h-file, as well as total domination over key queenside squares and the eventual possibility of pressure down the c-file.}) (10... Nh5 11. Be5 Nd7 (11... O-O 12. Qc1 $1 $14 {[%csl Rc7][%cal Re5c7,Rc1c7]}) (11... f6 12. Bg3 Nd7 13. e3) 12. h4 $1 {An incredible move, expanding on the kingside and threatening the devastating g4. In addition, White creates an escape square for the dark-squared bishop!} f6 (12... Nxe5 13. dxe5 O-O (13... d4 14. Nf3 $14) 14. e3 g6 (14... d4 15. Qxd4 Qxd4 16. exd4 Rd8 17. Ne2 $16) 15. Nf3 $16) (12... O-O 13. e3 Nxe5 14. dxe5 g6 15. Nf3 Bd7 16. Qc3 $16 {[%cal Rg3g4,Rg2g4,Rh4h5]}) 13. Bh2 {[%cal Rg2g4]} f5 14. e3 O-O (14... Qxh4 15. Be2 O-O 16. Nf3 Qe7 17. O-O Qxb4 18. Rc1 $16)) 11. e3 Nbd7 12. Bd3 Nb6 13. Nf3 Bd7 14. O-O Nc4 (14... Qa8 $2 15. Bxc7 $18) (14... Ne4 15. Rc1 Nd6 16. Bxd6 cxd6 17. Qa7 Bc8 18. Bf1 h6 19. Nd2 $14 {[%cal Rd2b3,Rb3a5]}) 15. Qa7 Bxb5 (15... Qa8 16. Ra1 Qxa7 17. Rxa7 Bxb5 18. Rxb7 c6 19. Ra7 $16) 16. Qxb7 c6 (16... Nd6 17. Bxd6 Bxd3 18. Bxf8 Bxf1 19. Bxg7 Bxg2 20. Bxf6 Qxf6 21. Kxg2 $18) 17. Ra1 $14) (7... Nc6 {This ingenious (though rare) move must be taken seriously. Black aims to recapture on a5 with the knight after the smoke clears on the queenside.} 8. bxa5 axb5 9. axb5 Nxa5 10. e3 {The first step is always to open the diagonal for our bishop and buttress the b-pawn.} Bd7 11. Bg5 {An inventive alternative to the more conventional 11.Nf3, pinning the knight on f6 and inducing a major kingside weakening with ...h6-g5. If Black does not chase the bishop away, White will maintain lasting pressure on the kingside.} h6 (11... O-O 12. Nf3 h6 13. Bh4 $14 {Now that Black has castled kingside, ...g7-g5 comes with a far steeper price tag.} g5 14. Nxg5 $1 {In the finest traditions of the Jobava London! With the dark squared bishop gone, Black will have a very hard time insulating the kingside against the litany of threats that our bloodthirsty queen-bishop tandem will pose.} hxg5 15. Bxg5 Kg7 16. Qf3 {The queen is terribly overextended, paralyzing the knight on a5.} Qe7 17. Qg3 {Setting up the vicious threat of Bxf6+ and Qh4+, picking off Black's queen.} Rh8 18. Bd3 {Quiet and powerful. By completing our development, we ensure that our kingside attack will never fizzle out.} Rh5 19. h4 $18 {Black has nothing better than to give back the material with 19...Ne4.}) (11... Nc4 12. Bxc4 dxc4 13. Ne2 $1 {We readily sacrifice the pawn on b5 in order to complete our development. The idea is to properly time the trade on a8 and to follow up with Bxf6, permanently ruining Black's kingside pawn structure and offering White a lasting initiative.} Bxb5 14. e4 {Putting the bishop on g5 to work!} h6 (14... Rxa1 15. Qxa1 h6 16. Bxf6 {Black instantly pays the price for the sloppy capture on a1. The queen must babysit the a8-square, forcing Black to recapture with the pawn.} gxf6 (16... Qxf6 17. Qa8+ Qd8 18. Qxb7 $16) 17. O-O {White has fantastic compensation for the pawn, as defined by several factors: 1) The bishop on b5 has no active prospects, while the knight has countless ways to join the game, ranging from Nf4-Nh5 to the more creative d5 followed by Nd4. 2) Black's pawn structure on both sides of the board, but particularly the kingside, is horrific. 3) We control the center and the only open file on the board; 4) There is no shelter anywhere on the board for Black's king. The subsequent lines are obviously not forced, but they nicely illustrate the way in which these factors translate to a monster initiative.} Bc6 (17... O-O 18. Qc1 $1 Kh7 19. Qb1 $3 {Incredible geometry. After provoking ...Kh7 (which may seem counterproductive), the queen returns to b1, attacking the bishop and creating an x-ray against the King. But White does not follow up with e5+, which would be futile. The correct continuation is far more explosive!} Bc6 20. d5 $1 {There is the rub! The bishop is forced away from c6, leading to the destruction of Black's entire queenside.} Bd7 21. Qxb7 $1 $16 {What a variation! The zigzag pattern adopted by our queen leaves quite the lasting impression.}) 18. d5 {Smashing open the d-file, and carving out the d4-square for the knight.} exd5 19. Rd1 O-O 20. Qc1 Kh7 21. Ng3 $44 {[%csl Rb1,Rf5][%cal Rg3f5,Rc1b1] From a practical perspective, White's compensation for the two pawns is absolutely overwhelming. Black is facing a litany of threats in the center and on the kingside, the most immediate being Nf5.} Rg8 22. Qb1 {The queen shuttles back to b1, forcing Black's king to relinquish its control over the h6-pawn.} Kh8 23. Nf5 Qf8 24. Qc1 {Back to c1 we go! The pressure has reached a zenith, and in order to emerge unscathed, Black must uncork a series of jaw-dropping computer moves.} dxe4 25. Rd8 $1 {A lovely deflection sacrifice that would be decisive if not for an even crazier counter-combination.} Rxg2+ $3 {Luring the king to g2, upon which Black can intercept the c1-h6 diagonal with ...e3+ and pick up the rook on d8. Of course, we refuse to cooperate!} 26. Kf1 (26. Kxg2 e3+ $19 {[%cal Rc1h6,Rf8d8]}) 26... Qxd8 27. Qxh6+ Kg8 28. Kxg2 e3+ 29. Kg3 $40 {Even after finding all of these moves, Black still remains under serious pressure. 29...Qf8 will be met with 30.Qxf6, when Black must still demonstrate accuracy to hold the draw.}) 15. Rxa8 Qxa8 16. Bxf6 gxf6 17. O-O Qa5 18. d5 O-O 19. Qd2 Kh7 20. Qf4 $18) 12. Bh4 g5 {It is far from easy to make these moves, which permanently weaken Black's kingside. The resulting position - while objectively balanced - remains very difficult for Black to accurately navigate.} 13. Bg3 Ne4 (13... O-O 14. f3 {A typical prophylactic move, dealing a blow to the knight's prospects.} Nh5 15. Kf2 {Wonderfully inventive chess! The king embraces its new role as an integral part of the kingside mosaic. Its role on f2 will be revealed on the next move.} Kg7 (15... f5 {This weakens the e5 square and enables us to maintain the bishop pair.} 16. Be5 $16) 16. h4 $1 {And this, ladies and gentlemen, is the point of White's previous move! The h-pawn rushes forward to blast open the kingside, while the king - yes, the king - is assigned the role of keeping the bishop defended! Note that doing the same thing with the knight (15.Kf2) would have allowed the rather nasty possibility of ...Nc4. In almost all of these lines, the development of the knight is delayed until we have deployed our bishop to d3 in order to keep the a5-knight at bay.} Nxg3 17. Kxg3 b6 18. Bd3 {The king feels perfectly at home on g3, and the time has finally come to complete our development.} e5 19. dxe5 gxh4+ 20. Kf2 {Having done its job on g3, the indefatigable monarch ducks back to safety on f2.} Qg5 21. Ne2 $14 {While the position remains level, White's practical play is quite a bit easier. Our knight will find a lovely home on d4, while the rook on h1 keeps Black's queen tied down to the h4-pawn.}) 14. f3 $1 {Despite our outward lack of piece coordination, we are able to hold Black's knight at bay on the strenghth of some badass tactics.} Nxc3 $4 (14... Nd6 15. b6 $36) 15. Qd2 $18 {[%csl Ra5,Rc3][%cal Rd2a5] The knights are pinned, and Black loses a minor piece:} Nb3 16. Rxa8 Nxd2 17. Rxd8+ Kxd8 18. Kxd2 $18) (7... Nh5 {An ultra-rare and not particularly threatening move. We are more than happy to withdraw our bishop from f4.} 8. Bd2 axb5 (8... Bb6 9. e4 $1 {Using the compromised position of Black's knight to stake a claim in the center.} Nf6 (9... axb5 10. Qxh5 bxa4 11. e5 Nd7 12. Bg5 $1 $18) 10. e5 Ne4 11. a5 $1 {It turns out that Black's bishop is most certainly not out of the woods!} axb5 12. axb6 Rxa1 13. Qxa1 Nxd2 14. bxc7 Qxc7 15. Kxd2 O-O 16. Bd3 $14 {The smoke has finally cleared, and it is obvious that we are the one calling the shots. We can follow up by solidifying our central control with f2-f4. Black suffers from a lack of space and the permanent weakness of the b5-pawn.}) 9. axb5 c6 10. e3 {Underscoring the counterproductive nature of Black's seventh move. This move now comes with tempo, enabling us to recapture Black's bishop while keeping our queenside pawn structure fully intact.} Nf6 11. c4 $1 dxc4 12. Bxc4 $14 {Black's knight sally to h5 has been an abject failure: we are ready to recapture the bishop on a5, and Black has no way to recover the pawn.}) 8. axb5 $1 {It is crucial not to rush with taking the bishop, as it is pinned to the rook on a8.} Bd7 (8... Nh5 9. Bc1 $1 {An incredibly deep move. While 9.Bd2 is far more natural, the bishop is better placed in the following variation:} c6 10. e4 Nf6 11. e5 Ne4 12. Ne2 $16 {Had the bishop been on d2, Black could have captured it with their knight! As it stands, White is simply winning, since we will recapture the bishop and chase away or even trap the knight with f2-f3.}) (8... Bxb4 {This exchange sacrifice leads nowhere, since White can block the check on c3 by returning to d2.} 9. Rxa8 Bxc3+ 10. Bd2 Bxd2+ 11. Qxd2 {Clearly, Black has insufficient compensation for the exchange. Our next step is to calmly develop our kingside and ferry the king to safety.} Nbd7 12. e3 Nb6 (12... Ne4 13. Qb4 Nb6 14. Ra1 {Transposes to 12..Nb6 13.Ra1 Ne4 14.Qb4.}) 13. Ra1 Ne4 14. Qb4 {Transposes to 12..Nb6 13.Ra1 Ne4 14.Qb4.} Qe7 {An important moment. It is very tempting to agree to the queen trade, but the resulting position offers Black satisfactory compensation for the exchange due to his more active pieces and the long-term weakness of the b5-pawn.} 15. Qb1 {This is the best square for White's queen. On b1, it is far away from the predations of Black's knights, and will link up with bishop (after Bd3) to exert nagging pressure on the e4-knight. Note that ...Nc3 is nothing but an empty threat, in response to which we can simply bring our queen up to b3.} O-O 16. Nf3 Bd7 17. Bd3 $16 {We have successfully completed our kingsid development and kept Black's queenside counterplay at bay. We will follow up by castling and deploying the rook to c1. White's total control over all of the key queenside squares renders Black's compensation for the exchange virtually nonexistant.}) (8... c6 {A tricky move, linking up the queen and bishop and asking the b5-pawn a rather impolite question. It is crucial to avoid making concessions with 9.bxa5 or 9.bxc6, both of which play right into Black's hands by allowing him to stir the pot on the queenside. Instead, we keep the tension by reinforcing the b5-pawn.} 9. e3 Bd7 $1 {The only way to maintain a feasible position, although this move is not at all easy to find. Transposes to 8..c6 9.e3 Bd7.} (9... cxb5 10. Bxb5+ Bd7 11. Bd3 {It is vital to keep the bishop pair.} Nc6 12. bxa5 Nxa5 13. Nf3 $14 {We have successfully navigated the queenside chaos, and it is clear that White will have all the fun in the middlegame. After castling, we can intensify the pressure with Ne5 and Bg5.}) (9... Ne4 {This move looks quite intimidating, but we are more than happy to part ways with the pawn on c3 in order to achieve rapid development and optimal piece coordination.} 10. bxa5 {This is the perfect opportunity to capture on a5, since Black cannot recapture due to the hanging knight on b8.} Nxc3 11. Qd2 Nxb5 12. Bd3 O-O 13. Nf3 $40 {The extra pawn is meaningless: our excellent piece coordination, bishop pair, and firm control over all key dark squares in the center grant us excellent prospects in the middlegame.}) (9... O-O $6 {This loses a pawn without a fight:} 10. Bxb8 Rxb8 11. Rxa5 $14) 10. b6 {We refuse to hand over control of the queenside! The lackluster 10.bxc6 would have played right into Black's hands by enabling the smooth development of the knight. This move, on the other hand, keeps Black's rook and knight frozen in place, and buys us a crucial tempo that we use to assert queenside dominance.} Qxb6 11. bxa5 {Obviously, a5 is untouchable since the knight hangs.} Qb2 (11... Rxa5 12. Bxb8 $18) 12. Ne2 {Cool, calm, and collected! All alone on our territory, the Black queen is fighting a lost cause.} O-O 13. Bd6 {This move underscores the importance of dark-square control in this variation. The bishop has a few questions to ask of the Black queen!} Re8 14. Ba3 Qb5 15. Nf4 Qxa5 16. Bb2 $18 {A lovely sequence indeed. Black loses an exchange for no compensation. Note the path taken by White's bishop, which started on f4 and ends all the way on the other side of the board!}) (8... b6 {With this move, Black reinforces the bishop on a5. He will have a passed pawn in the resulting position, but it will be far more of a weakness than a strength.} 9. bxa5 bxa5 (9... Rxa5 10. Rxa5 bxa5 11. Qa4 {With the rook gone, Black instantly loses the pawn.} Nbd7 12. e3 {Note that we do not even have to rush to take the pawn - it is going nowhere!} O-O 13. Bd3 Nb6 14. Qxa5 $16) 10. e3 O-O (10... Nbd7 11. Bd3 O-O 12. Nf3 Bb7 {see 10...0-0 11.Nf3 Nbd7 12.Bd3 Bb7.}) 11. Nf3 Nbd7 (11... c6 {This move almost never works tactically, since our bishop is trained at the knight on b8.} 12. Bxb8 Rxb8 13. bxc6 Rb6 14. Qa4 Qc7 15. Ne5 $16 {Black has no way to win back the pawn.}) 12. Bd3 Bb7 {see 10...0-0 11.Nf3 Nbd7 12.Bd3 Bb7.} 13. Ne5 {Oftentimes, this move is not as advisable with Black's knight on d7 and no bishop on d6, since Black can now simply capture our knight. In this specific position, however, we can effectively recapture on e5 with the pawn, driving away Black's knight and leading to an incredibly strong kingside attack.} Nxe5 14. dxe5 Nd7 (14... Ne4 15. Qh5 {The threat of f2-f3 renders Black's knight an utterly ineffective defender.} f5 16. exf6 Nxf6 (16... Qxf6 17. O-O Qxc3 18. Bxe4 dxe4 19. Rac1 {Picking up the pawn with interest.} Qf6 20. Rxc7 Rf7 21. b6 Rxc7 22. bxc7 $18) (16... gxf6 17. f3 $18 {[%csl Re4][%cal Rd3h7,Rh5h7]}) 17. Qe5 $16 {Black is dominated on the dark squares, and cannot defend e6 and c7 simultaneously.}) 15. Qh5 g6 16. Qh6 Qe7 (16... f6 17. Bxg6 $1) 17. h4 $16 {The march of the h-pawns spells big trouble for Black. Note that we can push h4-h5 against virtually any move, including ...f6. The attack is decisive.}) 9. e3 {[%cal Yf1b5] We still do not take the bishop!} Bxb4 {Now that White's bishop cannot return to d2 to block the check on c3, this is a far more serious idea. However, White's king is able to dodge the checks by journeying to f3!} (9... Ne4 {Hunting for the c-pawn is almost never effective, as Black loses a ton of tempi in the process and we are able to attain an ideal setup.} 10. bxa5 Nxc3 11. Qb3 Ne4 12. f3 Nd6 13. Bd3 $16) (9... b6 $6 10. bxa5 bxa5 11. Bd3 O-O 12. Qb1 $16 {Forming a queen-bishop battery, and preparing to meet 12...c6 with the devastating 13.b6.}) (9... c6 {Transposes to 8..c6 9.e3 Bd7.}) 10. Rxa8 $1 Bxc3+ 11. Ke2 Bxb5+ 12. Kf3 {Intrepid play is a prerequisite if you want to consider yourself a real Jobava player! As scary as this appears, Black simply does not have the firepower to exploit the king's compromised position. He has no choice but to spend time on castling and on activating his knight with ...Ne4; we will use this time to carve out shelter for our king on g2.} Bxf1 13. Qxf1 O-O (13... Ne4 14. Qb5+ $18) {Before building shelter for our king, we induce a queenside weakness and make it difficult for Black to untangle on the queenside.} 14. Qb5 {The best defense is attack! Despite appearances, White's king on f3 is perfectly safe, and we can hide it on g2 at our convenience.} b6 15. g3 {[%cal Rf3g2]} Ne4 16. h4 {...g5 must be stopped.} f6 17. Kg2 e5 {It appears that Black has trapped our bishop, since opening the f-file is unthinkable. But we have one more trick up our sleeve.} 18. f3 $1 {Counterattacking Black's knight, and forcing Black to relinquish his control over the c3-bishop. The tactics work out in White's favor.} Nd2 19. Ne2 {Another counterblow, this time hitting the bishop! If the bishop retreats to a5, we can finally save our bishop by taking twice on e5.} exf4 (19... Ba5 20. dxe5 fxe5 21. Bxe5 Nxf3 22. Bxg7 $1 Kxg7 23. Nf4 {Gorgeous tactics. The threat of Ne6+ forces Black to leave the knight on f3. Our king will journey back to the promised land to pick it up! This time, under the able protection of the knight, it will be quite a bit safer on that square. Note that 23...Qe7 should be met with 24.Qxd5, covering the e4-square.}) 20. Nxc3 fxe3 21. Re1 $18 {The smoke has cleared, and Black falls apart completely.}) 5. Nxd6+ {Thank you for the bishop pair!} cxd6 {This is one of the only lines where Black attains the pawn structure that we are used to having after Bd3 Bxd3 cxd3 (see typical idea #2). However, Black lacks a dark-squared bishop, which renders this pawn structure quite a bit less effective. The other bishop does not have active prospects, and preparing the ...e6-e5 pawn push - while opening up a diagonal for the bishop - will create even more targets in the center.} 6. e3 {White's advantage is defined by two factors: (1) the bishop pair and (2) the long-term pressure that we will exert against the d6-pawn.} Nc6 (6... Qb6 {This is best according to the computer, but it is virtually never played and does not really change the tenor of the position.} 7. Rb1 Qa5+ (7... O-O 8. c3 h6 9. h3 {It is quite important to throw this move in in response to ...h6, since Nf3 allows Black to pick up the bishop with ...Nh5.} Re8 10. Nf3 Ne4 11. Bh2 Bd7 12. Qb3 $14 {(Niemann) After the trade, Black will have a very hard time keeping d6 protected.}) 8. c3 Qxa2 9. Bxd6 $16 {Recovering the pawn and preventing Black from castling. Things are already quite bad for Black:} Ne4 10. Bc7 {A strange-looking move, but it prevents the queen from escaping via a5 and - much like Nb5 (see typical idea #7) - lures Black's knight to an incredible passive square.} Na6 11. Bf4 g5 12. Bg3 h5 13. Bd3 $1 $16 {Preparing to meet ...h4 with Be5 f6 Bxe4, when Black can only take one bishop at a time! White's advantage is not up for debate.}) (6... O-O {Black often starts with this move, but it will almost always transpose to the main line (6...Nc6) after Black develops the knight.} 7. c3 Nc6 (7... Re8 8. Nf3 Nc6 9. h3 {Transposes to 6...Nc6 7.Nf3 0-0 8.c3 Re8 9.h3.}) 8. Nf3 {Transposes to 6...Nc6 7.Nf3 0-0 8.c3.}) (6... e5 $2 {This is surprisingly common, but it simply drops a pawn for no compensation.} 7. dxe5 dxe5 8. Bxe5 Nc6 9. Bxf6 Qxf6 10. c3 O-O 11. Bd3 $16) 7. Nf3 O-O 8. c3 {It is always a good idea to buttress the d4-pawn, rendering ...e6-e5 even less effective. Transposes to 6...Nc6 7.Nf3 0-0 8.c3.} Ne4 {Activating the knight and reinforcing the d6-pawn.} (8... Re8 9. h3 {Making luft for the bishop. Transposes to 6...Nc6 7.Nf3 0-0 8.c3 Re8 9.h3.} e5 {This move is the opposite of scary. The x-ray against White's king my appear concerning, but with the d4-pawn as secured as it is, Black has absolutely no way to convert his temporary development advantage into anything tangible. After we complete our kingside development, Black will experience long-lasting problems with his center.} 10. Bh2 Bf5 (10... exd4 11. Nxd4 Qb6 12. Qb3 Nxd4 13. Qxb6 Nc2+ 14. Kd2 axb6 15. Kxc2 $16 {Recovering the knight and leading to a miserable endgame for Black. His pawn structure says it all!}) (10... e4 11. Nd2 Bf5 {Under different circumstances, this type of pawn structure can favor Black, but with no dark-squared bishop, it makes a lot less sense. We have active ideas on both sides of the board.} 12. Be2 a6 13. Qb3 Qd7 14. g4 $1 {The pawn advances to g5, where it will either trap the knight or force the horsey to lose contact with the crucial d5-pawn.} Bg6 (14... Be6 15. g5 $18 {[%csl Rf6][%cal Re2h5] The knight is trapped!}) 15. g5 Nh5 16. Qxd5 $18) 11. Be2 {Since ...e4 is never scary, we simply continue our development.} Rc8 12. O-O h6 13. Qb3 {This is a great square for the queen, from where it pressures two pawns and stays out of the way of the rest of our pieces.} Qd7 14. Rfe1 {Continuing to improve our position by reinforcing the bishop, which will prove useful in the event that Black takes on d4.} exd4 (14... a6 15. a4 Bg6 16. Bf1 $14 {We can continue to improve with Rad1 and Qa2, paving the way for a potential pawn storm on the kingside. Black is saddled with tons of weaknesses in the center, and has no easy way to improve.}) (14... Bxh3 {This sacrifice is the opposite of scary.} 15. gxh3 Qxh3 16. Nd2 Ng4 17. Bxg4 Qxg4+ 18. Kh1 $16) 15. Nxd4 Nxd4 16. cxd4 $16 {It is also possible to take with the e-pawn, but we prefer this recapture, which opens up the queenside. This may seem to favor Black, but his control of the c-file is temporary - Black must burn a tempo to prevent Bb5, which we will use to contest the c-file with Rac1.}) (8... Qe7 9. Be2 e5 10. Bg3 Ne4 11. dxe5 $16 {Black drops the d-pawn.}) (8... h6 9. Be2 Re8 (9... Nh5 {This is not particularly scary. It is rather painful to part with our dark-squared bishop, but almost all of Black's problems will remain in the resulting position.} 10. O-O Nxf4 11. exf4 Qf6 12. Qd2 Bd7 13. Rfe1 Rfe8 14. Bf1 $14 {We have a nice space advantage and excellent prospects on the queenside.}) 10. h3 e5 11. Bh2 Bf5 12. O-O Ne4 13. Qb3 $14 {Hitting where it hurts! The only (tactical) way to defend both pawns is ...Qb6, but the queen trade further damages Black's pawn structure and makes it harder for them to defend d5.}) 9. Bd3 f5 10. h3 {This move recurs constantly in the 4...Bd6 line. In this case, we take the sting out of ...g7-g5.} Qf6 11. Bh2 {Tucking the bishop away and ensuring its permanent safety.} Bd7 12. O-O Be8 (12... e5 $2 {This move succumbs to a lovely tactical refutation that underscores how nicely our pieces are coordinated.} 13. dxe5 dxe5 14. Nxe5 $3 Nxe5 15. Bxe4 dxe4 16. Bxe5 $16 {Picking up the bishop on d7 and emerging a pawn ahead.}) 13. Ne1 $5 {[%cal Rf2f3] An inventive idea, preparing to kick away the knight with f2-f3. Black has no choice but to add another defender to d6.} Qe7 14. f3 Nf6 15. Bc2 {More excellent maneuvering. The bishop politely steps away to make room for the knight.} Bg6 {Preparing ...e5.} 16. Nd3 e5 17. Ba4 {The bishop has its moment in the sun! On a4, it hits the knight on c6, threatening to win a pawn and forcing Black to create serious dark-square weaknesses by advancing the pawn to e4.} e4 18. fxe4 dxe4 (18... fxe4 19. Nf4 Bf7 20. Qe1 $14 {The queen is ferried over to g3. We have the bishop pair, the superior pawn structure, and excellent prospects on the f-file.}) (18... Nxe4 19. Nf4 Bf7 20. Bb3 Nf6 21. Qf3 $16) 19. Nc1 $5 {An incredibly deep move, keeping contact with the f5-pawn so that Black's bishop cannot retreat to f7. White's control over the dark squares ensures total domination.} d5 20. Bxc6 bxc6 {It may seem that Black has fully rectified his pawn structure and has solved his positional problems, but they are in fact only beginning! Note that we have complete and permanent dominance over the dark-squares: the bishop on h2 is the hidden engine of our position. We can assert our advantage by activating our queen and knight, creating a situation in which Black is devoid of pawn breaks and of any active opportunities on either side of the board.} 21. Qa4 Qe6 22. Nb3 Nd7 23. Nc5 Nxc5 24. dxc5 $16 {Jobava London-3...e6  2023 The knight trade opens up the d4-square, which we can use as an outpost for our queen. Our bishop controls b8 and prevents Black from engineering the ...f4 pawn break. After we reposition our queen to b4, we can accumulate the queenside pressure with b2-b4 and a2-a4 (preparing b5), or blast open the center by preparing c3-c4. Even after an endless series of accurate moves, there is no end in sight to Black's suffering.}) (3... g6 {Most Grunfeld and King's Indian players choose this system. The resulting positions are quite complex, and markedly different from those that arise after 3...Bf5, 3...e6, and 3...a6. We begin by solidifying our center and initiating a furious kingside assault.} 4. e3 {Black is at an early crossroads: the most popular move by a country mile is 4...Bg7, but Black has a few serious alternatives that must be studied carefully.} Bg7 {By far the most common response, completing the fianchetto.} (4... c6 {Although much less popular than 4...Bg7, this alternative must be taken seriously. Black solidifies his center and prepares an eventual queen sally to b6.} 5. h4 {As usual, we initiate kingside operations and pose Black with an early dilemma. Analogously to the position after 4...Bg7 5.h4, Black has two main ways to meet Harry's march: Black can continue developing with 5...Bg7 (transposing to a position that we analyze via the 4...Bg7 5.h4 c6 move order), or meet White at the top with 5...h5.} Bg7 (5... h5 6. Nf3 {Proceeding with development.} Bg7 (6... Bg4 {The early bishop sortie to g4 is very common, but the bishop will be tossed around by our pawns. We respond by unpinning our queen and reinforcing the idea of Ne5.} 7. Qd2 Nbd7 (7... Bxf3 {This exchange is squarely in White's favor: the resulting pawn structure offers White fantastic play in the center and on the kingside, as well as the bishop pair.} 8. gxf3 Bg7 9. e4 {Striking at the center without delay.} e6 10. O-O-O Nbd7 11. Kb1 {It is almost always worth investing a tempo to improve the safety of our king.} a6 12. Rg1 {Deploying the rook, and applying pressure on g6.} Ng8 {An ingenious move, opening up a line of fire to the h4-pawn. Since our lead in development is quite vast, we can afford to leave the h-pawn en prise!} 13. Bh3 Qxh4 (13... Ne7 14. Bg5 {This pin is quite nasty, since Black cannot afford to play ...f6 for obvious reasons.} O-O 15. Ne2 Nb6 16. Nf4 {Black is paying a steep price for advancing his h-pawn and opening the g-file: we threaten the devastating Nxh5 gxh5 Bf6, while Black's "attack" hasn't even gotten off the ground.} Nc4 17. Qb4 $18 {[%csl Re7] The knight is hanging, and Nxh5-Bf6 is imminent.}) 14. Rh1 {A cold-blooded response, sending the queen back where it belongs!} Qd8 15. exd5 {It is now time to smash open the center at all costs!} cxd5 16. Nxd5 $1 {At this point in your Jobava journey, moves like these should not elicit any surprise.} exd5 17. Rde1+ Ne7 (17... Kf8 18. Bd6+ $18) 18. Rxe7+ $1 {Another blow, removing the final defender from the e-file. Obviously, taking with the queen loses to Re1, so Black's king has no choice but to go on a walk.} Kxe7 19. Qb4+ {A surgical follow-up check, preventing ...Kf8.} Ke8 20. Re1+ $18) 8. Ng5 {This creative night sally underscores the awkwardness of the bishop on g4, which will provide fuel for a furious pawn storm in the center. f2-f3 and e3-e4 is coming, and Black can do absolutely nothing about it.} Bg7 9. f3 Bf5 10. e4 {In order to generate a suitable escape square for the bishop, Black must cede total control of the center.} dxe4 11. fxe4 Bg4 12. e5 Nh7 13. Nxh7 Rxh7 14. Ne4 $16) 7. Ne5 Nbd7 8. Bd3 Nxe5 (8... Qb6 $1 {This is the strongest move, but it is very infrequently played. Objectively, the position is dynamically equal, but there is no question that it is quite a bit easier to play White.} 9. a3 {An important resource, indirectly defending the b2-pawn.} O-O (9... Qxb2 $4 10. Na4 $18) 10. Na4 Qa5+ 11. Qd2 $1 {Lovely tactics! Black is posed with an ultimatum: take the knight and run the risk of losing their queen, or transition into a rather unpleasant endgame in which White's space advantage and better-placed pieces offers the better practical chances.} Qxa4 (11... Qxd2+ 12. Kxd2 Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Ng4 14. Bxg7 Kxg7 15. Rhf1 $14 {[%cal Rf2f3,Rb2b4]}) 12. b3 {In order to extricate the queen, Black must come up with a series of computer moves.} Ne4 $8 13. Bxe4 Qa6 14. Bd3 Qb6 15. O-O {Do not be afraid to castle kingside even with the pawn on h4, since Black lacks the machinery and activity to exploit this cosmetic damage.} Nxe5 16. Bxe5 Bxe5 17. dxe5 {The pawn on e5 is incredibly nasty, since it controls important squares and severs the coordination between Black's pieces.} Qc7 18. Qc3 c5 19. e4 $1 {Sacrificing the e5-pawn for a furious initiative on both sides of the board.} d4 20. Qd2 Qxe5 21. f4 Qg7 22. b4 cxb4 (22... b6 23. f5 Bd7 24. e5 $1 gxf5 25. Bxf5 e6 26. Be4 Rac8 27. Rae1 $40 {[%cal Rf1f3,Rd2f4,Rf3g3]}) 23. Qxb4 $36) 9. Bxe5 O-O 10. Qf3 {Building up the kingside pressure.} Ng4 (10... Bg4 $6 11. Qg3 {[%cal Rf2f3] and White will send away the bishop on the next move.}) 11. Bxg7 Kxg7 12. Qg3 {Setting up the threat of f2-f3, and virtually forcing Black to initiate a queen trade.} Qd6 (12... b5 13. f3 $1 {The e3-pawn is untouchable due to Qe5+, forcing Black's knight out of commission.} Nf6 14. Ne2 $14 {Our knight is ferried to f4, when the pressure against g6 will become seriously alarming. Black has nothing going on the other side of the board.}) 13. Qxd6 exd6 {The tactical battle has subsided, and it is time to switch gears to methodical positional play. The first step is to optimize our queenside pawn structure.} 14. a4 a5 15. b3 Nh6 16. Ne2 $14 {Step one has been achieved, and now it is time for step two: maneuvering the knight to f4, and opening up the queenside with c2-c4. White has a small but stable edge in the endgame. Following that, we will place our rook on b1 and exert nasty pressure on Black's b7-pawn. There is no question that White stands better, and we are playing essentially for two results.}) 6. Be2 h5 7. Nf3 {See 4...Bg7 5.h4 c6 6.Be2 h5 7.Nf3.}) (4... a6 {Combining ...g6 and ...a6 is rather awkward. Our response does not change.} 5. h4 Bg7 (5... h5 6. Nf3 Bg7 7. Ne5 O-O 8. Be2 {Straightforward and powerful: we threaten to smash open the kingside with g2-g4, which will likely lead to the summary execution of Black's monarch.} Nbd7 {Now, 9.g4 is hasty due to ...Nxe5 and ...Nxg4, so one more patient preparatory move is required.} 9. f3 Nxe5 10. Bxe5 c6 11. Qd2 {11.g4 was possible, but there is no hurry: we are in charge in the center, and Black is too passive and poorly coordinated to generate serious prospects on the queenside.} b5 12. O-O-O $40 {[%cal Rd1g1,Rg2g4] Our next move will Rdg1, when everything will finally be ready for g2-g4! Of course, 12...b4 merely exacerbates the issue due to 13.Na4, when Black will be saddled with additional queenside weaknesses.}) 6. h5 {Here, the exchange sacrifice is fully justified, since the d5-pawn will be undefended in the resulting position, costing Black a crucial tempo.} Nxh5 7. Rxh5 gxh5 8. Qxh5 {Highlighting the ineffectiveness of Black's fourth move. Black must now waste another tempo to defend the d5-pawn, giving us the window of opportunity that we need to develop long-lasting kingside pressure.} c6 (8... e6 9. Qh2 $1 {A crucial resource, aiming our queen-bishop battery at the c7 pawn. Black has no way to defend it without decisively compromising his king safety.} Nc6 (9... Kd7 10. Na4 $1 {[%cal Ra4c5]} b6 11. c4 {An old friend comes knocking! We are more familiar with the c2-c4 idea in a positional context, but it is just as powerful here. Opening up the c-file enables us to deploy our rook, when the pressure against c7 quickly becomes decisive.} dxc4 12. Rc1 {Now, ...b5 runs into the deadly Nc5+, so Black must part ways with the c4-pawn.} Bb7 13. Bxc4 Nc6 14. d5 $18 {It should be clear even to an untrained eye that White's attack is utterly crushing.}) 10. Bxc7 Qf6 {Having snagged the c7-pawn, we can now chill out and focus on bringing out the rest of our pieces. In particular, the knight is itching to occupy a certain deadly outpost on the kingside. We must listen to the horsey!} 11. Nh3 {[%cal Rh3f4,Rf4h5]} Bd7 12. Nf4 Qh6 13. Nh5 $18 {The knight on h5 is unassailable, and it controls the key f6 and g7-squares, restricting Black's already ultra-cramped position even further. We can continue by castling queenside, completing our development with Be2, and even finding appropriate digs for the other knight with Na4-b6.}) 9. Nf3 Nd7 {Had Black saved a tempo on ...c6, he would have been able to ferry his knight over to f6, forcing our queen out of h5 and essentially consolidating his material advantage. In this line, we beat him to the punch - with totally devastating effect.} 10. Ng5 {This and the next moves truly emphasize how deadly the queen + knight tandem can be in the right circumstances.} Rf8 11. Ne6 {[%cal Re6d8]} Qa5 12. Bc7 $1 $18 {[%cal Re6g7] Game, set, match!}) (4... c5 5. Nb5 Na6 6. c3 Bg7 7. h4 {Transposes to 4...Bg7 5.h4 c5 6.Nb5 Na6 7.c3.}) 5. h4 {This is not just a "bullet move," but a very dangerous system that confronts Black with serious problems. White's idea is to shove the pawn down to h5, when the resulting exchange sacrifice (i.e. ...Nxh5 Rxh5 gxh5 Qxh5) offers White a massive initiative, which can turn decisive if Black fails to reinforce the d5-pawn or unwisely decides to castle kingside. At a high level, Black almost always responds by pushing their own h-pawn one or two squares, but both of these options create long-term weaknesses on the kingside that we will use to spark the flames of an eventual kingside attack.} h5 {This is the most common response online, by quite a wide margin. Our next steps are to develop the knight and ready ourselves for queenside castling.} (5... O-O {Ignoring White's plan leads to a quick and decisive kingside attack. Amazingly, 5...0-0 is the second most common move online, with around 37,000 games at the time of analysis. A welcome statistic indeed!} 6. h5 $1 Nxh5 {Black takes the plunge.} (6... c5 {This is second in popularity behind 6...Nxh5, but objectively it is clearly Black's best try. Still, the resulting positions - with a wide open h-file granting us exciting attacking opportunities at every turn - are virtually impossible for a human to handle accurately.} 7. hxg6 hxg6 (7... fxg6 {This keeps the h-file closed, but creates a host of weaknesses in the center and on the kingside.} 8. dxc5 Nc6 9. Nf3 Bg4 (9... Qa5 10. Nd2 $1 {A nifty little resource, shuttling the knight to b3 and prying the queen away from the d5-pawn.} Qxc5 (10... e5 11. Nb3 Qd8 12. Bg5 Be6 13. Qd2 $16 {We will follow up by castling queenside, landing the d5 pawn in serious trouble. Obviously, Black cannot even dream of any compensation for the pawn!}) 11. Nb3 Qb6 (11... Qb4 12. a3 {This leads to the same position as after 11...Qb6, with the pawn on a3 clearly favoring White.}) 12. Nxd5 Nxd5 13. Qxd5+ e6 14. Qe4 Bxb2 {Black has recovered the pawn, and the ensuing check on c3 may appear rather disconcerting, but Black's position remains virtually untenable due to the semi-open h-file, the lack of active prospects for the LSB, and the permanent weakness of the e6-pawn.} 15. Rd1 Bc3+ 16. Nd2 Rd8 17. Bd3 Qb4 18. Ke2 $1 {Unpinning the knight and preparing to trade on our own terms.} Bxd2 19. Rxd2 Qxe4 20. Bxe4 Rxd2+ 21. Kxd2 Bd7 22. Rb1 b6 23. Rb3 $14 {The bishop pair and our incredibly active rook guarantee us excellent winning chances in the endgame.})) 8. Qd2 {Rome was not built in a day! Before launching our kingside attack, we must do the bare minimum to complete our queenside development and shuttle our king to relative safety. With this move, we also take the sting out of ...cxd4, which will greatly increase the potency of the bishop sortie to h6.} Nc6 9. O-O-O Qa5 (9... Bf5 10. f3 {The standard response to Black's last move, underscoring the awkwardness of Black's bishop and preparing g2-g4, which will pave the way for our queen to land on the h-file.} Rc8 11. g4 {Beating Black to the punch!} Be6 12. Qh2 {Once the queen lands on h2, Black is totally doomed.} Re8 13. Nge2 {This patient move highlights the extent of our dominance. There is no need at all to hurry with Bh6; Black has absolutely nothing going on the queenside. Now, we are ready to thrust the bishop into h6 and follow up with Nf4, with utterly decisive consequences.} Qb6 14. Bh6 Bh8 15. Bf8 $1 {Beautiful!!} Kxf8 16. Qxh8+ Ng8 17. Rh7 Red8 {This position occurred in "Boknoy2019" - "Mustang6000", Lichess (blitz) 2021. White committed a small inaccuracy with Rg7, enabling Black to prolong the game with ...Ke8. Believe it or not, Black actually went on to win the game! Instead, immediate victory is yielded by a move on the other side of the board:} 18. Na4 $1 Qa5 19. Nxc5 Qxa2 20. Qg7+ Ke8 21. Qxg8# {The point of getting the knight to c5 was not to win a pawn, but to close off the king's only escape hatch!}) 10. a3 {One more bit of prophylaxis, taking the defensive burden off of our knight. Depending on Black's response, we can finally initiate kingside operations with Bh6 or casually finish our development with Nf3. Simply put, Black has very little to brag about on the queenside, and we have a lot more time on our hands than it may seem!} c4 11. Nf3 {An old friend comes knocking: we bring the knight over to e5, which will likely tip Black's already poorly-coordinated army over the edge.} b5 12. Ne5 Bb7 13. Qe1 $1 {Gorgeous maneuvering. We prepare f2-f3 and Qh4, when the attack will finally begin in earnest!} b4 14. axb4 {Black's queen is but a paper tiger, since 14...Qb1+ is cooly met with 15.Kd2 (followed by f2-f3, to eliminate the possibility of ...Ne4+).} Nxb4 15. f3 Qa1+ 16. Kd2 Qxb2 17. Rc1 {Cold-blooded and ridiculously powerful. Black's queenside activity has come to a shuddering halt, and now it is our time to shine!} Nh5 18. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 19. Qg3 {[%cal Gf4h6]} Kh8 20. Qh4 Na2 21. Nxa2 Qxa2 22. Qxh5+ Kg8 23. Bh6 {Removing the final obstacle. Without its last faithful defender, the Black king will be left entirely to its own devices. To use a phrase that both of us promised not to use in this course, the position plays itself!} Bxh6 24. Qxh6 Qa6 25. Qg5+ Kh8 26. Bxc4 $1 $18 {A simple but lovely tactic to top it all off. Rh1 is unstoppable, and mate follows in a few moves.}) (6... Bg4 $2 {This horrific move is incredibly common.} 7. f3 {It transpires that the pawn on h5 is untouchable due to g2-g4, trapping the bishop! Black has no choice but to retreat in shame.} Bf5 8. g4 Be6 9. Qd2 $18 {[%cal Rd2h2] Black's position is already resignable!}) 7. Rxh5 {The typical exchange sac works to perfection here, since the d5-pawn is undefended and the newly-castled king will get absolutely terrorized.} gxh5 8. Qxh5 {This is the best possible version of the exchange sac, since Black's king has found itself on the worst possible square. Our next step is to complete our development while leveling threat after threat against Black's hapless king.} c6 (8... e6 9. Bd3 f5 10. Qh2 $1 {Surgically precise. We saw this exact idea in the 4...a6 5.h4 Bg7 variation. The pawn on c7 represents Black's last hope to generate counterplay; without it, Black will be left without even an iota of play.} Na6 11. Bxa6 bxa6 12. O-O-O {Having mangled Black's queenside, it turns out that we do not even need to waste any time capturing the pawn on c7! Instead, we can turn directly to the most important task at hand: getting the rook to h1 as quickly as possible.} c5 13. Nf3 $18 {Rh1 is coming, and Black's king is doomed.}) (8... Nd7 9. Bd3 Nf6 {Bringing the knight to f6 does not change the nature of the position in any way; the knight is a good defender, but it will be horribly outmatched.} 10. Qh2 c5 11. dxc5 {The point of this move is not so much to snag a pawn (we will give it right back) but to prevent ...c4, ensuring that our bishop remains singularly trained on the h7-pawn.} Qa5 12. O-O-O Qxc5 13. Nge2 a6 14. Rh1 h5 15. Ng3 $18 {Black will lose the h-pawn, and with it, any hopes of not getting checkmated before move 20!}) (8... f5 9. Nf3 Qe8 {This defensive approach is quite common online. Now, 10.Qh2 obviously maintains a decisive advantage, but we have a much simpler approach, cashing in our chips and attaining a decisive material advantage in the endgame.} 10. Qxe8 Rxe8 11. Nxd5 {The pawn on c7 cannot be defended!} Na6 12. Bxa6 bxa6 13. Nxc7 $18) 9. Bd3 f5 10. Nf3 Qe8 11. Qh2 Nd7 12. O-O-O Nf6 13. Rh1 h5 14. Ne5 Ng4 15. Qg3 Rf6 16. Qh4 Be6 17. Ne2 Nxe5 18. dxe5 Rg6 19. Qxh5 Rxg2 20. Qh7+ Kf8 21. Bg3 Qf7 22. Nf4 Rxg3 23. fxg3 $18) (5... h6 {A typical antidote to h4-h5: Black prepares ...g6-g5 in response. However, the h6-pawn constitutes a long-term weakness, and a timely h4-h5 - even if it fails to directly open up the kingside - will induce further weaknesses along the b1-h7 diagonal.} 6. Nf3 c6 7. Qd2 {Our approach is identical to the one we adopt in the main line (5...h5), quickly entrenching our knight on e5 and preparing to ferry our king to safety at a moment's notice.} O-O 8. Ne5 Nbd7 9. Bd3 Nxe5 10. Bxe5 {Black's position is cramped and unpleasant, and is powerless against the ensuing series of kingside pawn breaks.} Nd7 11. Bxg7 Kxg7 12. h5 {Softening up Black's position.} g5 13. f4 $1 {Continuing to batter away at Black's kingside!} g4 14. f5 {Screwing in the pawn, leaving Black's bishop with no active prospects and the g4-pawn without a key defender.} Nf6 15. O-O-O b5 16. e4 b4 17. e5 bxc3 18. exf6+ exf6 19. Qxc3 $16 {[%cal Rc3c6,Rd1f1,Rh1h4] Black's position is riddled with holes. We can follow up with Rdf1 and Rh4, picking up the g4-pawn and proceeding to destroy Black on the kingside.}) (5... c6 {This move, although played orders of magnitude less frequently online than 5...h5 and 5...0-0, has been the choice of many top players, and for good reason. By supporting the d5-pawn, Black takes the sting out of the typical exchange sacrifice. In order to force Black to move his h-pawn, we must deploy our bishop to the somewhat awkward e2-square. Still, it is a worthy price to pay, for inducing ...h5 is crucial if we are to poke holes at Black's ultra-solid setup.} 6. Be2 {Now, h5 obviously becomes a serious threat. Black's response is virtually forced.} (6. h5 $5 {This is an interesting move that both of us have played, and it offers decent practical compensation for the exchange. However, objectively, there is no question that Black is significantly better with only a few accurate moves.} Nxh5 7. Rxh5 gxh5 8. Qxh5 Nd7 {The exchange sacrifice is far less effective here, since Black does not need to spend an additional tempo guarding the c6-pawn (compared to the same position in the 4...a6 line). Black uses this tempo to maneuver the knight to f6, dousing the flames of White's initiative. There is no question that White has good practical chances here, but we decided against reccommending this line, since White's compensation is objectively insufficient.}) 6... h5 7. Nf3 {The presence of the bishop on e2 does not substantively change the fabric of the position when compared to the main line (5...h5). Our plan remains the same, to quickly shepherd the knight to e5 and ultimately to chisel away at Black's kingside with g2-g4.} Bg4 8. Ne5 Bxe2 9. Qxe2 Nbd7 10. O-O-O {Obviously, we are only willing to trade on our own terms.} Nxe5 (10... Qa5 11. Kb1 {It is almost always worth throwing in this prophylactic move, which ensures that ...Ne4 will never cause us headaches.} Nxe5 (11... Rc8 {The fate that awaits Black after this careless move shows that Black cannot keep his king in the center forever.} 12. Nxd7 Nxd7 13. e4 {The Black monarch's days of restful solitude are over. The moment he escapes to the kingside, we will pry open that side of the board with g2-g4.} Nb6 14. Bg5 {Threatening exd5, and virtually forcing Black to permanently weaken the dark squares in the center.} e6 {Having induced these weaknesses, we now switch gears and force the queens off the board.} (14... O-O 15. g4 $40) 15. exd5 cxd5 16. Qb5+ Qxb5 17. Nxb5 {[%cal Rb5d6,Rb5a7] It transpires that Black is facing a rather problematic double-attack: there is no way to guard d6 and a7 at the same time! Black has no choice but to part ways with the corner pawn.} Kd7 18. Nxa7 Rc4 19. Be3 $14 {Black's drawing chances are significant, but we are obviously playing for two results. Note that Black has no way to cut off the knight's evacuation route, since ...Rb4 will always run into a3.}) 12. Bxe5 O-O-O {Black's king is safer here than on the kingside, but his main problems are now in the center.} 13. e4 Rhe8 14. f3 $16 {[%cal Rg2g4] White is more active and better coordinated. Our likeliest follow-up is g4, which is rather problematic for Black even though the king is on the other side of the board.}) 11. Bxe5 O-O (11... b5 {Without any support, the pawn storm is anything but scary.} 12. f3 {You should not bat an eye at this move by now, which simultaneously prepares g2-g4 and e2-e4.} Qa5 13. a3 {Taking the sting out of ...b4, which will now create a complex of weak squares on the queenside that we are in perfect position to exploit.} b4 14. axb4 Qxb4 15. Qa6 {An instructive move, showing that our play is not only limited to the center and kingside in this line. We use the attack on c6 to force Black's queen back to his side of the board, which in turn allows our queen to find an ideal perch on a3.} Qb6 (15... O-O 16. Qxc6 Rfc8 17. Qa6 Rxc3 18. bxc3 Qxc3 19. Rd3 $18 {Black's "attack" has fizzled out before it began.}) 16. Qa3 O-O 17. Na4 Qa5 18. g4 $1 {As is so customary in the Jobava London, we combine play on both sides of the board. Having taken the c5-square under firm control, we now turn our attention to the kingside, carrying out a typical sacrifical operation aimed at opening the h-file.} hxg4 19. h5 Nxh5 20. fxg4 Nf6 21. Qxe7 {The queen joins the party, hitting Black where it hurts.} Qd8 (21... Rfe8 $2 22. Qxf6 $3 {Who needs the queen anyway?} Rxe5 23. Qxc6 Ree8 24. Rdf1 Rac8 25. Qd7 Rf8 26. Rxf7 $1 $18) 22. Qxd8 Raxd8 23. Bxf6 Bxf6 24. Kd2 $14 {[%cal Rd1a1,Ra4c5] We can finally see the importance of placing the knight on a4. Having done our job on the kingside, we will once again switch our attention to the other side of the board, deploying our rook to the a-file (it is especially good on a3, where it lends additional support to the e3-pawn) and entrenching the knight on c5 when the time is right.}) 12. Bxf6 Bxf6 13. g4 $1 $40 {Opening up a devastating kingside attack.} hxg4 14. Rdg1 $5 Kg7 15. Qxg4 Qc8 16. Qg2 {White will develop the attack with a well-timed h5, as well as the knight transfer to f4 via e2. Meanwhile, Black has absolutely nothing going on the other side of the board.}) (5... c5 {Yet again, this move is bad by default, since it allows White to establish a knight on b5 and follow up by securing the d4-pawn.} 6. Nb5 Na6 7. c3 O-O 8. Be2 {Developing the bishop and preparing h5.} Qb6 9. a4 Bd7 10. h5 $14 {Black's queenside counterplay has ground to a halt, while we are just getting started on the other flank. We will follow up by trading on g6 and maneuvering the knight to e5.}) 6. Nf3 O-O (6... c5 {Our response to this move is one and the same!} 7. Nb5 Na6 8. c3 O-O 9. Be2 {In contrast to 5...h5, we will have a harder time engineering a kingside attack, but from a strategic perspective, our advantage is indisputable.} Bg4 10. O-O Qb6 11. a4 $14 {Supporting the knight and taking the sting out of ...Bxf3. Black can barely move a muscle, e.g.} Rac8 $2 12. a5 Qd8 13. Nxa7 Ra8 14. Nb5 $16) (6... Bg4 7. Qd2 Bxf3 {This is the only way to "justify" the bishop's development to g4, but the resulting pawn structure works squarely in our favor.} 8. gxf3 {White's pawn structure may be visually unappealing, but the pawn on f3 will help prepare e4, and the semi-open g-file works in our favor as well.} O-O 9. O-O-O c5 {The only reasonable attempt to muddy the waters, though it falls woefully short.} (9... c6 10. e4 e6 11. Rg1 {Our last two moves underscore the strategic error inherent in the trade on f3.} Nbd7 12. e5 Nh7 13. Bd3 {Everything is now in place for the decisive breakthrough.} c5 14. Bxg6 $1 fxg6 15. Rxg6 Kh8 16. Rdg1 Rf7 17. Nb5 {White's knight joins in from the other side. Once it reaches d6, Black will be unable to deal with the countless threats coming from every side.} Qxh4 18. Bg3 {Calm, cool, and collected! It is important to shoo Black's queen away from the kingside before reopening the g-file.} Qe7 19. Nd6 Rxf3 20. Bf4 $1 {Imprisoning Black's rook and reopening the g-file.} Rg8 21. Bh6 $18) 10. dxc5 $1 {With the light-squared bishop gone, Black is unable to sustain his center without creating additional weaknesses.} e6 (10... Qa5 $2 11. Nxd5 {Intrepid and powerful!} Qxa2 {This move is almost never as scary as it looks; we disarm it by vacating an escape route for our king.} 12. Nxe7+ Kh7 13. Qb4 $18 {The check on a1 is innocuous, and we can always bring our bishop to d3 to clog up the d-file.}) 11. Bd6 {Hitting Black where it hurts!} Re8 12. e4 $18 {In the words of my coach, White has compensation for his extra pawn :)}) (6... c6 7. Ne5 {See 4...c6 5.h4 h5 6.Nf3 Bg7 7.Ne5.}) 7. Qd2 {Preparing to castle kingside.} c5 (7... Bg4 8. Ne5 Nbd7 9. f3 Bf5 10. O-O-O c5 11. g4 $1 hxg4 12. fxg4 Nxg4 13. Nxg4 Bxg4 14. Be2 Bxe2 15. Qxe2 $16 {[%cal Rh4h5]}) 8. Ne5 {This centralizing move is far more accurate than the immediate 8.0-0-0, which would enable Black to generate troublesome queenside counterplay with ...Qa5.} Nc6 {This position has occurred countless times in high-level tournament practice. Our first step is to control the e4-square and restrict the mobility of the f6-knight.} 9. f3 {A crucial follow-up, controlling the e4-square and preparing g2-g4.} cxd4 (9... Bf5 10. Bb5 Nb4 (10... cxd4 11. exd4 {See 9...cxd4 10.exd4 Bf5 11.Bb5.}) 11. Ba4 {We are used to this idea with colors reversed (i.e. ...Bb4-Ba5 in response to Nb5). Black's superficial queenside play has been fully disarmed, and we proceed to push the knight back to c6.} cxd4 12. exd4 Rc8 13. a3 Nc6 (13... Na6 14. O-O-O Qa5 15. Bb3 b5 16. Kb1 b4 17. Ne2 $18) 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. Na4 {As usual, we assert our dominance on both sides of the board. By controlling c5, we permanently halt Black's queenside play. The stage is now set for a lightning kingside offensive.} Ne8 16. g4 hxg4 17. h5 $40 {The computer assigns White a relatively modest plus, but from a human perspective, Black's position is nearly hopeless. Black has no good answer to the manifold threats on the kingside, e.g.} Nf6 18. h6 $1 Bh8 19. h7+ $1 Nxh7 20. fxg4 Be4 21. O-O-O $1 {A final flourish, connecting the rooks; we only need one of the towers to checkmate black on the h-file.} Bxh1 22. Rxh1 Bg7 23. Qh2 Nf6 24. Nc5 {with the unstoppable threat of Ned7, which will remove the only defender of the h7-square and lead to quick checkmate. This line is worth reviewing several times, for it clearly illustrates our ability to dominate on both sides of the board. It is worth thinking of the queenside as the "positional side," to be used primarily for the sake of optimizing the placement of the "Jobava knight." The h-file, on the other hand, is used to do the checkmating!}) 10. exd4 Bf5 (10... Qb6 {This tempting move plays right into our hands.} 11. O-O-O {Shepherding the king to safety and protecting b2 and d4. Black has no good answer to the typical plan of g2-g4 and h4-h5, which will tear open the kingside and quickly lead to a decisive attack.} Bf5 12. g4 (12. Nxc6 $5 {It is also possible for White to play it slow.} bxc6 (12... Qxc6 13. Re1 e6 14. Be5 $16) 13. Na4 $16 {Asserting dominance over the c5-square. Note that Black has absolutely no potential to develop a queenside attack, since the b8-square is permanently under our control and our last move renders ...c6-c5 impossible.}) 12... hxg4 13. h5 {While the engine finds a way to prolong the game, all human moves lead to Black's rapid demise.} Nxh5 (13... gxh5 14. fxg4 hxg4 15. Bd3 {Clinical precision! Trading off the light-squared bishop decisively loosens Black's control over h7.} Bxd3 16. Nxd3 {This recapture makes a lot of sense when you keep in mind that the queen's optimal square is on h2.} Nxd4 17. Ne5 Nf5 18. Qh2 $18 {We will follow up with Rdg1 and Nxg4. Black's days are numbered!}) (13... gxf3 14. h6 Bh8 15. h7+ {Prying open the h-file in thematic fashion. 15...Kg7 merely hastens Black's demise after 16.Bh6+, so he has no choice but to partake of the forbidden fruit.} Nxh7 16. Nxd5 Qd8 17. Qh2 g5 18. Bd3 $1 $18 {Just as in the 13...gxh5 line, this move is an integral part of White's attacking strategy. Eliminating the final defender of the h7-square spells immediate doom.}) (13... Nxe5 14. Bxe5 Nxh5 {This is Black's most resilient continuation from an objective point of view, but it leads to a position that we can only refer to as indefensible.} 15. Nxd5 Qd8 16. Rxh5 $1 {Pow! In contrast to the other lines within the 10...Qb6 sub-variation, the decisive invasion will occur along the g-file.} gxh5 17. Qg5 Bg6 18. Nxe7+ Kh7 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 {It may seem that White's attack has ground to a halt, but we are actually just one short step away from setting up a mating net.} 20. Bc4 {Calm developing moves like this one should no longer come as a surprise to you. This sets up the virtually unstoppable threat of Nf5+, when ...Kg8 is met with Qxg6+ (that is why we need the services of the bishop!).} Kh7 (20... Rh8 21. fxg4 hxg4 22. Rf1 $18 {[%cal Rf1f7]}) 21. Rh1 {Black is checkmated seven ways to sunday, e.g.} Qxd4 22. Nf5 $1 $18 {The computer's top suggestion here is 22...Qg1+, which tells us all that we need to know about the position!}) 14. fxg4 Nxe5 15. gxf5 Nf3 16. Qg2 Nxf4 17. Qxf3 g5 18. Rg1 Qf6 19. Qg4 $40 {Black has succeeded in staving off checkmate for the time being, but his position remains untenable from a practical standpoint. The following line demonstrates the truth of this statement:} Rac8 20. Kb1 {Given the total lack of counterplay on the queenside, we are well within our rights to optimize the safety of our king before rolling up our sleeves on the other side of the board.} a6 21. Qxg5 Qxg5 22. Rxg5 {It may seem counterintuitive to trade off the queens, but we have plenty of pieces with which to finish Black off on the kingside.} Kh7 23. Rg4 Nh5 24. Rh4 $18 {[%cal Rf1e2] Black loses the h5-knight, and with it, the game.}) 11. Bb5 $1 {A very interesting and unusual idea that poses serious practical problems. As is the custom all throughout the 3...g6 variation, White combines positional and tactical play on the two flanks: on the queenside, we wish to trade on c6 and control the c5-square with Na4. On the kingside, we are awaiting the perfect opportunity to blast open the landscape with g2-g4 and h4-h5. Black can maintain the balance with perfect computer play, but from a practical standpoint, the position is virtually unplayable. See 9...cxd4 10.exd4 Bf5 11.Bb5.} Nxe5 (11... Qb6 {This move is no better than it was on the previous move. In fact, it merely encourages us to do what we wanted to do anyway: assert our dominance over the c5-square.} 12. Bxc6 bxc6 13. Na4 Qb5 14. b3 {A familiar situation: Black has run out of active ideas on the queenside, and the stage is set for decisive kingside action.} Nd7 15. Nxd7 Bxd7 16. Nc5 Bf5 {The tempting 17.g4 is possible, but we have at our disposal a far more powerful - and much more beautiful - idea.} 17. Bc7 {A picturesque position that is worth examining for more than a few seconds! Yes, your suspicion is correct - Black's queen is trapped in the middle of the queenside, and he has absolutely no good answer to the threat of a2-a4.} a5 18. a4 Qb4 19. c3 $18 {It is rather amazing that we win the game without making a single attacking move on the kingside. Such is the unique power of the Jobava London!}) (11... Rc8 12. Bxc6 bxc6 13. Na4 {At this point, you should be ready to execute the last two moves automatically. With the c5-square under complete control, the stage is set for decisive action on the kingside.} Nd7 (13... Ne8 14. g4 hxg4 15. O-O-O f6 (15... Bxe5 16. Bxe5 f6 17. fxg4 Bxg4 18. Rdg1 Bh5 19. Nc5 fxe5 20. Ne6 Qd6 21. Nxf8 Kxf8 22. Qh6+ Kf7 $8 (22... Kg8 23. Qxh5 $18) 23. dxe5 Qxe5 24. Re1 $1 Qg7 25. Rhf1+ Kg8 26. Qxg7+ Nxg7 27. Rxe7 $36) 16. Nxg4 $40) 14. Nxd7 Qxd7 15. Nc5 Qe8 (15... Qd8 16. O-O-O Qb6 17. Rde1 Rfe8 18. g4 hxg4 19. fxg4 Bxg4 20. h5 $1 gxh5 21. Reg1 e5 22. Bh6 exd4 23. Qg5 $18) 16. O-O-O $16) 12. dxe5 Nd7 13. O-O-O {It may seem brazen to give up the e5-pawn, but our development is our top priority. Furthermore, once Black takes a bite of the forbidden apple, we swiftly transition our initiative to the center.} Nxe5 (13... Qb6 14. Bxd7 Bxd7 15. Nxd5 Qe6 {Here, do not walk into 16.Nc7?? Qxa2 17.Qxd7 Rad8 and White loses instantly!} 16. Nxe7+ Qxe7 17. Qxd7 $18) 14. Rhe1 Nc6 {A critical position has been reached. The natural move is, of course, 15.Nxd5, but that allows Black to force the queens off the board with 15...e6. Instead, we seize the opportunity to force the dark-squared bishops off the board, rendering Black's monarch even more susceptible to all sorts of shenanigans.} 15. Bh6 Bxh6 (15... e5 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 17. Nxd5 Nd4 (17... Qxh4 18. g4 Be6 (18... hxg4 19. Rh1 $18 {[%cal Rd2h6]}) 19. Rh1 Qd8 20. gxh5 Bxd5 21. hxg6 $1 fxg6 22. Qh6+ Kf7 23. Bd3 $1 Qf6 24. Bc4 Bxc4 25. Qh7+ Qg7 (25... Ke6 26. Qd7#) 26. Rd7+ Ne7 27. Rxe7+ Kxe7 28. Qxg7+ $18) 18. Rxe5 $1 {A scintillating piece sacrifice.} Nxb5 19. Qf4 Be6 $8 (19... Qa5 20. Rxf5 $1 gxf5 21. Qg5+ Kh8 22. Qh6+ Kg8 23. Nf6#) (19... Qc8 20. Rd2 Re8 21. g4 Be6 (21... hxg4 22. fxg4 Qb8 23. Rde2 Rxe5 24. Rxe5 Be6 25. Qf6+ Kh7 26. Rxe6 fxe6 27. Qf7+ Kh6 28. Nf4 $1 Qg8 29. g5#) 22. Qf6+ Kg8 23. Rxh5 $3 gxh5 24. Qg5+ Kf8 25. Qh6+ Kg8 26. Nf6#) 20. Nc3 Nxc3 21. Rxd8 Nxa2+ 22. Kb1 Raxd8 23. Rxe6 fxe6 24. Qe5+ Kh7 25. Kxa2 $14) (15... e6 {This fails to a lovely tactical sequence that is worth memorizing:} 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 17. Bxc6 bxc6 18. g4 $1 hxg4 19. fxg4 {The point of White's entire strategy is on full display in the event of 19...Bxg4, which runs into 20.Qd4+ - that is why it was so important to trade the bishops. Black has no choice but to accept a miserable endgame.} Be4 20. Qd4+ Qf6 21. Nxe4 dxe4 22. g5 {Clamping down on the kingside and forcing the queen trade on our terms.} Qxd4 23. Rxd4 $14 {We will recover the e4-pawn on the next move, when Black's deficient queenside pawn structure, coupled with the superior activity of our rooks, will grant us excellent winning chances.}) 16. Qxh6 e6 17. g4 $1 {Boom shaka-laka! The point of this move, believe it or not, is not to follow up with h5, but rather to open up the knight's access to e4 and g5.} hxg4 18. fxg4 Bxg4 19. Ne4 $1 {The point! The threat of Ng5 is absolutely devastating.} Qe7 (19... Re8 20. h5 Bxh5 (20... gxh5 21. Nf6+ $18) 21. Be2 Qh4 22. Nd6 Red8 23. Rh1 Qf6 24. Bxh5 Rxd6 25. Bxg6 $1 fxg6 26. Qh7+ Kf8 27. Rdf1 $18) (19... Bxd1 20. Ng5 $18) 20. Ng5 f6 21. Nxe6 Bxe6 22. Rxe6 Qxe6 23. Qxg6+ Kh8 24. Qh5+ Kg7 25. Rg1+ $18 {Jobava London-3...g6  2023}) (3... Nc6 $2 {Unfortunately for Black, this move virtually loses on the spot.} 4. Nb5 {[%cal Rb5c7,Rf4c7] Black has no way to defend c7 without giving up additional material. You will undoubtedly win more than a handful of games in this way!} e5 5. dxe5 ({Give to white advantage and} 5. Bxe5 Nxe5 6. dxe5 a6 7. Nc3 d4 8. exf6 dxc3 9. Qxd8+ Kxd8 10. bxc3 $16) 5... Ne4 $2 {This merely exacerbates an already critical situation.} (5... Nh5 6. e3 Nxf4 7. exf4 a6 8. Nd4 $16 {Black has absolutely no compensation for the pawn.}) 6. Qxd5 $1 {Bang!} Qxd5 7. Nxc7+ Kd8 8. Nxd5 $18 {Jobava London-Rare 3rd Moves  2023 The rest is clear without further analysis :)}) (3... Nbd7 $2 {This move loses for a similar reason.} 4. Nb5 e5 5. dxe5 a6 (5... Nh5 6. Bg5 Qxg5 7. Nxc7+ Kd8 8. Nf3 $1 $18) 6. Nc3 Nh5 7. Nxd5 c6 8. e6 $1 cxd5 9. exd7+ Bxd7 10. Be5 $16) (3... Bg4 $6 {A strange move that walks right into f3, enabling White to develop a quick and dangerous initiative on the kingside.} 4. f3 Bh5 5. g4 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. e3 e6 8. Bd3 Bxd3 9. Qxd3 Bd6 10. Nge2 c5 (10... Bxf4 11. Nxf4 Nbd7 12. O-O-O c5 13. g5 Ng8 14. g6 Ne7 15. gxf7+ Kxf7 16. Rhg1 $18) (10... Nc6 11. O-O-O a6 12. e4 dxe4 13. Nxe4 Nxe4 14. Qxe4 Qe7 15. Bxd6 cxd6 16. d5 exd5 17. Qxd5 $14) 11. Qb5+ Nc6 12. Qxb7 $18) (3... Bd7 {This ultra-rare move isn't a huge mistake, but it is rather awkward and leads to an inferior version of the 3...e6 line.} 4. e3 e6 5. Nb5 {Securing the bishop pair, or forcing the knight to a6.} Bxb5 6. Bxb5+ c6 7. Bd3 Bd6 8. Nf3 Bxf4 9. exf4 $14 {We have a nice space advantage, control over the light squares, and a massive clamp on the e5-square.}) (3... Bf5 {This move, which maintains developmental symmetry, obviously constitutes one of Black's most popular responses.} 4. e3 {We answer the symmetrical variation in outwardly modest fashion, opening up our light-squared bishop and setting up the future idea of g2-g4. A few years back, 4.f3 was virtually automatic, but this innocuous-looking move has overtaken 4.f3 in popularity and in hype -- for good reason. Against most of Black's setups, our knight heads directly for e5, as it does in almost every other variation.} e6 (4... c6 5. Nf3 e6 (5... Nbd7 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. cxd3 e6 8. e4 {Transposes to 4...e6 5.Nf3 Nbd7 6.Bd3 Bxd3 7.cxd3 c6 8.e4}) 6. Ne5 Nbd7 {The most popular response by far. Most players do not see the danger awaiting them on the kingside!} (6... h5 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. cxd3 Nbd7 9. Nf3 {[%csl Rh5]} Be7 10. e4 Qb6 11. Qe2 $14 {The pawn on h5 is a huge weakness which essentially precludes kingside castling. We can push e4-e5 at our leisure.}) (6... h6 7. g4 {This is still very strong, even though we are no longer trying to trap the bishop. Instead, we are storming the kingside with our pawns. Black has a very hard time stopping g4-g5, which has the potential to be devastating.} Bh7 8. h4 Nbd7 {This leads directly to a scintillating tactical confrontation.} (8... Nfd7 9. Bd3 {It is important to combine space-grabbing pawn moves on the kingside with simple developing moves. Once the knight leaves f6, g4-g5 no longer comes with tempo, so we hold off on that move for now.} Nxe5 10. Bxe5 Bxd3 11. Qxd3 Nd7 12. Bg3 {Do not be fooled by the symmetrical appearance of a position like this. White has a massive kingside space advantage, as well as better-placed pieces. It is not easy for Black to find a suitable home for their bishop, and must constantly reckon with a well-timed g4-g5.} Bb4 (12... Be7 13. O-O-O Qa5 (13... b5 14. g5 h5 15. g6 $16) 14. Kb1 O-O-O 15. e4 Nb6 (15... dxe4 16. Nxe4) 16. exd5 cxd5 17. b3 Bb4 18. Ne2 $16) 13. a3 Qa5 (13... Bxc3+ 14. Qxc3 Nf6 (14... Qe7 15. O-O-O $14) 15. f3) 14. Kd2 Bxc3+ (14... Be7 15. g5 hxg5 16. hxg5 Rxh1 17. Rxh1 Bxg5 18. Rh8+ Nf8 19. Qh7 $1 $18) 15. Qxc3 Qxc3+ 16. Kxc3 Nf6 17. f3 $14 {White can continue by expanding on both sides of the board: a4-a5 comes to mind, and a well-timed g4-g5 will open up the h-file and likely lead to immediate infiltration.}) (8... Ne4 9. Nxe4 Bxe4 10. f3 Bh7 11. Bd3 Bxd3 12. Qxd3 {Here, we recapture on d3 with the queen to preserve the integrity of the queenside. On the next move, we will castle queenside and our minor pieces will dominate in the center and on the kingside. White has a small, but very stable, advantage.} Nd7 13. O-O-O Nxe5 14. Bxe5 Qa5 15. Kb1 O-O-O 16. e4 $14) 9. g5 hxg5 (9... Ne4 10. gxh6 gxh6 11. Nxe4 Bxe4 12. f3 Bf5 13. Bd3 Bxd3 14. Qxd3 Qb6 (14... Be7 15. O-O-O Nxe5 16. Bxe5 Bf6 17. f4 $1 Bxe5 18. fxe5 $16 {[%cal Rd1f1,Rf1f6,Rh1f1]}) 15. O-O-O) 10. hxg5 Bxc2 $1 {A cute tactic, but it takes more than that to kill the Jobava London!} (10... Ne4 11. Nxe4 dxe4 12. g6 fxg6 13. Qg4 $18) 11. Rxh8 Bxd1 12. Kxd1 $3 {An incredibly deep recapture. The idea is to move the king away from the checking zone of the d7 knight: after ...Nxe5, ...Nf3 no longer comes with a check!} Nxe5 13. gxf6 gxf6 14. dxe5 {White has a kingdom and a horse for the queen! Black's attempt to open up the center leads absolutely nowhere.} d4 15. Ne4 dxe3+ 16. Ke2 exf2 17. Bh6 $18) (6... Bd6 7. g4 $1 Bxe5 (7... Bg6 8. h4 h6 9. Nxg6 fxg6 10. Bd3 Bxf4 11. Bxg6+ Kf8 12. exf4 $18 {Bortnyk-Fedoseev, Lichess (blitz) 2021.}) 8. dxe5 Nxg4 9. f3 Nh6 10. e4 Bg6 11. Qd2 dxe4 12. Qxd8+ Kxd8 13. fxe4 $14 {Black is entirely paralyzed, and White will have complete dominance over the d-file after the long castle. We can also stick a rook on d6 at our convenience.}) 7. g4 {See 4...c6 5.Nf3 e6 6.Ne5 Nbd7 7.g4.} Nxe5 (7... Bg6 8. h4 h6 (8... Qb6 9. Rb1 Nxe5 10. dxe5 Nxg4 11. Qxg4 Bxc2 12. Qe2 Bxb1 13. Nxb1 Bb4+ 14. Kd1 $14) 9. Nxg6 fxg6 10. Bd3 Kf7 11. Qf3 {[%cal Rg4g5]} Bb4 12. Ke2 $18) (7... Be4 8. f3 Bg6 9. h4 $18 {The presence of the pawn on f3 only helps White.}) 8. gxf5 Ned7 9. fxe6 fxe6 10. a3 Be7 11. Bh3 $16) (4... Nbd7 $4 {As usual, the premature development of the b8-knight loses material!} 5. Nb5 {[%csl Ra7,Rc7][%cal Rb5a7,Rb5c7]} Rc8 6. Nxa7 Ra8 7. Nb5 Rc8 8. Bd3 $16) 5. Nf3 {At this point, Black is at a major crossroads. There are countless ways for Black to develop, starting with the natural developing moves (5...Nbd7, 5...Bd6, and 5...Bb4) and ending with more extravagant moves such as 5...Ne4. We must consider all of them, since White's responses are a bit different in each case.} Bd6 {This is the most common move on Lichess, with 127,000 or so games at the time of analysis (and, hopefully, many more after the course enters the chess bloodstream :).} (5... Nbd7 {Believe it or not, this natural developing move is eighth (yes, eighth!) by popularity in the Lichess database. However, at the titled player level, it is the most common move, so it must be treated like a main line. Black immediately contests the e5-square, delaying the development of his dark-squared bishop until we have put our cards on the table. Now, 6.Ne5 makes no sense due to 6...Nxe5; the exchange does not yield any benefit. Instead, we aim for plan B, quickly completing our development and initiating queenside pressure.} 6. Bd3 Bxd3 (6... Bg6 7. Bxg6 {Generally, it is not a great idea to capture on g6 since it hands Black the h-file, but we follow it up with a concrete and powerful idea.} hxg6 8. Nb5 {The point! It transpires that Black has great difficulty defending the pawn, since ...Bd6 drops a pawn (though White must still respond accurately - see analysis below) and ...Rc8 drops the a7 pawn (after which the knight returns to b5). Indeed, there is only one way to defend c7, and it is not all that easy to find.} Bb4+ {Rushing the bishop to a5. This idea constitutes one of Black's main responses to 3...e6 4.Nb5. In this version, the placement of Black's knight on d7 granmts us the ability to engineer nasty queenside pressure.} (8... Bd6 $2 {This is actually the most common move, since ...Bb4-a5 is not the easiest maneuver to find for someone who is not experienced in this type of position.} 9. Bxd6 cxd6 10. Nxd6+ Ke7 {Now, it is crucial to refrain from 11.Nb5, which enables Black to seize the initiative with a check on a5 followed by ...Ne4. Instead, we help ourselves to another pawn!} 11. Nxb7 Qb6 12. Nc5 Nxc5 13. dxc5 Qxc5 (13... Qxb2 $2 14. O-O {We can follow up with Rb1-b7 or Qd4. Black is in serious trouble.}) 14. c3 Rab8 15. Rb1 $14 {Black has no compensation for the pawn. We will follow up by castling kingside; there is nothing to fear along the h-file, since the h2-pawn is very safely protected by the knight. Black will have a hard time finding shelter for their king. As a case in point, 15...Qc4 is met with the powerful 16.Ne5, angling for c6.}) 9. c3 Ba5 10. Qa4 {Deploying the queen with tempo, and preparing c3-c4. In the position after 3...e6 4.Nb5 Bb4+ 5.c3 Ba5, this move is ineffective due to ...Nc6, defending the bishop and cutting off the line of fire toward Black's king. Here, Black has no choice but to meekly withdraw the bishop, since 10...c6 runs into 11.Nd6+ followed by Nxb7.} Bb6 11. c4 a6 12. Nc3 c6 13. Qb3 {The queen has done its job on a4, and we now reposition it to the optimal square.} dxc4 (13... O-O $2 14. c5 $18) 14. Qxc4 Bc7 15. Bg5 $14 {[%cal Rh2h4,Re1g1,Re3e4,Ra1d1] White's advantage is indisputable. After buttressing our bishop with h2-h4 and castling short, we will turn our attention toward the center, expanding with e3-e4 and solidifying our center with R(a)d1.}) (6... Ne4 $6 {This is simply bad, as it enables us to ruin Black's pawn structure. Additionally, the bishop on f5 will end up totally out of commission.} 7. Bxe4 dxe4 8. Nd2 Bb4 9. Nb5 {Yet again, the knight sally to b5 is very effective.} Ba5 10. c3 {This is a lot better than 10.c4, which would justify the placement of Black's bishop on a5. Now, the bishop is taken out of the game and we follow up with a furious queenside pawn storm.} O-O 11. b4 Bb6 12. c4 $14 {[%cal Rc4c5,Rb5c3] Black is under massive pressure due to the threat of c5 as well as Nc3, when the e4-pawn, in the immortal words of GM Robert Hess, will not be long for this board. Note that ...Nf6 can be savagely met with Bg5.}) (6... Bb4 7. O-O {This is an improved version of the position we reach after 5...Bb4. The inclusion of the moves ...Nbd7 and 0-0 clearly favors White, since ...Ne4 is no longer a palatable option.} O-O (7... Ne4 8. Nb5 Ba5 (8... c6 $4 9. Nc7+ $18) 9. b4 {Let the queenside pawn storm begin!} Bb6 10. Bxe4 {Eliminating the knight grants our b5-knight a nice retreating square on c3.} Bxe4 11. a4 a6 12. Nc3 Bg6 13. a5 Ba7 14. Qe2 O-O 15. Na4 $14 {We have built up a massive clamp on the queenside, and things will go from bad to worse once we stick the knight on c5.}) (7... Bxc3 8. bxc3 {Obviously, this pawn structure transformation only favors White, since c3-c4 now comes with even greater effect.} O-O 9. c4 c6 10. cxd5 cxd5 11. Qb1 $1 {Hitting Black where it hurts!} b6 12. Rc1 {[%cal Rc2c4] The power of doubled pawns on full display. The first c4 break served to soften up Black's queenside, and the second instantiation of this pawn advance will cause even more problems.} Rc8 13. Ba6 $1 {Transitioning to tactics! It transpires that the rook runs out of squares!} Rc3 (13... Ra8 14. Bb7 $16) 14. Qb2 Rc6 15. Bb7 Rc4 16. Nd2 $18 {The rook has breathed its last breath.}) 8. Bxf5 exf5 9. Ne2 {Standard Sam! We prepare the typical plan of queenside expansion with b3 and c4, and make the bishop on b4 look rather dumb.} Ne4 10. c3 Be7 11. h3 {It is always worth including this move to take the sting out of ...g5, which is now duly met with Bh2.} c6 12. b3 $14 {We are ready to push c4, and we can follow up by smoking the knight out of e4 with Nd2.}) 7. cxd3 {This recapture should be automatic by now! We follow up in traditional fashion by deploying our queen to b3 and building up nasty queenside pressure along the c- and b-files.} Bd6 (7... c5 8. O-O {Keeping the tension. Of course, the impulsive 8.Nb5 would have run into 8...Qa5+, sending the knight right back to c3. Now, it appears that Black can cripple our pawn structure by capturing on d4, since Nxd4 runs into a fork. It turns out that Black is in for quite a surprise!} Be7 {The most sensible option, preparing to castle kingside. Still, by resolving the central tension, we will attain a stable and long-lasting edge.} (8... a6 {This prevents Nb5, but costs Black another tempo. We pile up on the queenside without any further delay.} 9. Rc1 Rc8 10. Qb3 b5 {This appears to call White's previous move into question: why provoke a move that grabs space and builds a rather imposing wall of pawns? In reality, the dutiful student of this course will know that ...b7-b5 is almost always a dreadfully weakening move, leaving behind massive holes on the light squares (particularly c6) and handing White the keys to a ravaging queenside initiative.} (10... Qb6 11. dxc5 Qxb3 12. axb3 {It may appear that Black is out of the woods after recapturing on c5, but his troubles are only beginning. Note that the rook on c8 lacks a defender - this tactical deficiency is about to become very important, regardless of which piece Black decides to recapture with.} Nxc5 (12... Bxc5 13. d4 {Sending the bishop back, and paving the way for a straightforward and devastating tactical blow.} Be7 (13... Bb4 14. Nxd5 $1 Rxc1 15. Rxc1 $1 $18 {[%cal Rc1c8] Even better than Nxf6+. The threat of Rc8, which will pick off Black's rook, forces instant resignation.}) 14. Nxd5 $1 {A thematic tactical blow, dealing Black the ultimate punishment for failing to castle in timely fashion.} Rxc1 15. Nxf6+ {We must remain careful until the end: the sloppy 15.Rxc1 would drop a piece after ...Nxd5, when the check on c8 is parried by ...Bd8 (in contrast to 13...Bb4).} Nxf6 16. Rxc1 $18 {Black is a pawn down, and cannot cover both infiltration squares (c7 and c8) at the same time.}) (12... Rxc5) 13. Na4 {[%cal Rd3d4] Crushing. We exploit the undefended rook on c8 to the maximum, setting up the deadly threat of d3-d4. Black can move the rook to a defended square, but it will be immediately chased away by the bloodthirsty knight on f3.} Rc6 (13... Kd8 14. Ne5 {Hitting f7. Black is woefully overextended.} Rc7 15. Nxc5 Bxc5 16. d4 {This is a lot better than 16.Nxf7+, which only wins a pawn.} Bd6 17. Rxc7 Kxc7 18. Rc1+ Kb8 19. Nxf7 Bxf4 20. Nxh8 $18) 14. Nxc5 Bxc5 (14... Rxc5 15. b4 Rxc1 16. Rxc1 $18 {We can basically premove Rc7, after which Black will not be able to move a single muscle. Of course, 16...Bxb4 is impossible on account of 17.Rc8+.}) 15. Ne5 Rc8 16. d4 $18 {The rightful culmination of our lightning queenside attack. White wins the bishop.}) 11. dxc5 Nxc5 12. Qc2 {Cold-blooded and ridiculously powerful. As in the 10...Qb6 line, Black suffers primarily because the rook on c8 is not sufficiently defended, so the tension down the c-file is always going to favor White. We now combine the threat of b2-b4 with sharp positional play, maneuvering our knight to the optimal d4-square.} Be7 13. Ne2 {[%cal Rb2b4]} O-O 14. Ned4 {Not fearing ghosts on the c-file: there is absolutely no risk of a discovered attack, since any move by the Black knight can be shut down with Nc6, followed by Nxe7+ and Qxe7 (not to mention that our queen can also simply retreat).} Qb6 15. b4 Nxd3 16. Qxc8 Rxc8 17. Rxc8+ Bf8 18. Rb8 Qa7 19. Nc6 Qd7 20. Nfe5 Nxe5 21. Nxe5 Qd6 22. Ng6 $18) (8... cxd4 9. Nb5 $1 {Tabasco! This knight sally is particularly problematic in ...Nbd7 lines. With our king safely tucked away on the kingside, Black doesn't even have the option of hurrying the bishop around to c7.} Rc8 10. Nxa7 {Before invading down the c-file, it makes sense to snap off a pawn and return to b5.} Ra8 11. Nb5 Rc8 12. Rc1 $18 {[%cal Rb5c7] Now, we get down to business. The devastating threat of Nc7+ is unstoppable.}) 9. dxc5 Nxc5 (9... Bxc5 10. d4 Bb6 (10... Be7 11. Nb5 O-O 12. Bc7 Qc8 13. Rc1 a6 14. Nd6 Bxd6 15. Bxd6 $18) 11. Bd6 $14) 10. b4 Ncd7 (10... Na6 11. Qa4+ Qd7 12. a3 Qxa4 13. Nxa4 $14) 11. Nb5 O-O 12. Bc7 Qc8 13. Rc1 $14 {We saw a similar position to this one (with the pawns on d4 and b2) in the 9...Bxc5 10.d4 Bb6 line - this cosmetic difference does not change all that much. Black is still in big trouble due to the threat of Nd6 and the pressure down the c-file.}) (7... Be7 $2 {This leads to immediate material loss:} 8. Nb5 Rc8 (8... Bb4+ 9. Ke2 Ba5 10. Qc1 $1 {10.Rc1 is also good, but this is even better, since it essentially leads to a forced queen trade.} O-O 11. Bxc7 Bxc7 12. Qxc7 $18) 9. Nxa7 Ra8 10. Nb5 $18) (7... c6 8. O-O Be7 9. e4 {Classic stuff, controlling the center. Black has a very hard time finding useful moves, because we have complete control over the center as well as more active pieces.} O-O 10. h3 Qb6 11. Qd2 c5 12. e5 Ne8 13. Na4 Qd8 14. Nxc5 Nxc5 15. dxc5 Bxc5 16. Rfc1 {The doubled d-pawns serve us well, since we can reinforce the center on demand with d4.} Be7 17. Rc2 $16) (7... a6 8. O-O) 8. Bxd6 cxd6 {Do not be fooled by the symmetrical appearance of the position. Our knight on c3 is superior to its counterpart on d7, and we are able to strike first on the queenside.} 9. Qb3 Qb6 (9... Rb8 10. Qa3 Qb6) 10. Qxb6 axb6 11. Rc1 $14 {White has a small but very stable advantage in the endgame.} Ke7 12. Rc2 Rhc8 13. Ke2 b5 14. a3 Rc6 15. Rhc1 Rb6 16. Na2 $1 $16 {[%cal Ra2b4]}) (5... Bb4 {This is the second most popular move online, with almost as many games as 5...Bd6. The pin against White's knight and the prospect of ...Ne4 appears bothersome, but we do not back down! Due to Black's lack of development, we are more than happy to give the c3-pawn away.} 6. Bd3 Ne4 {An inexperienced Jobava player would now be tempted to snap off the knight, but we are seldom willing to give up our light-squared bishop for the knight.} (6... Bxd3 {Given Black's last move, trading the bishop makes very little sense. After recapturing the bishop, Qb3 will come with tempo, leading to a nasty queenside attack and a clear advantage.} 7. cxd3 O-O 8. O-O {There is no big hurry to play Qb3; it makes sense to shuttle our king to safety first.} Nbd7 (8... Nc6 9. Qb3 {Even with the bishop on b4 defended, this move is quite powerful.} Qd7 10. Ne5 {A thematic follow-up, forcibly removing the knights and leaving both of Black's minor pieces hanging.} Nxe5 11. dxe5 Bxc3 $8 12. bxc3 Nh5 13. Bg5 {Much better than the greedy 13.Qxb7. The knight on h5 is entirely out of commission, and Black has no good answer to the twin threats of Qxb7 and Qd1,which induces a near-fatal weakening of the dark squares around Black's king.} b6 14. Qd1 $1 {Note that the tempting 14.g4 fails tactically to 14...h6 15.Bh4 g5, when Black retains material equality.} g6 15. Bh6 $16) 9. Qb3 {Time to hit the bishop!} Bxc3 (9... a5 10. a3 {This does not substantively change the nature of the position, since Black is forced to capture on c3 anyway.}) 10. bxc3 b6 11. a4 {Simple and strong: we threaten to accentuate the queenside pressure with a5, thereby creating even more holes on the queenside.} a5 (11... c5 12. a5 {[%cal Rf1b1,Rh2h3]} bxa5 13. Bd6 $1 {[%cal Rd6f8,Rd6c5] Fork time!} Re8 14. dxc5 $18) 12. c4 $14 {From a strategic perspective, Black's situation is quite dire. The knights have no active prospects, and we will turn up the dial on the queenside with Rac1.}) 7. O-O $1 {We do not back down! It transpires that accepting the pawn sacrifice is unwise, owing to Black's lack of queenside development and the vulnerability of whichever minor piece lands on c3.} Nxc3 (7... Bxc3 {Capturing with the bishop leads to much the same consequences as 7...Nxc3, further exacerbated by the fact that Black will have insufficient control over key dark squares in the center.} 8. bxc3 Nxc3 9. Qd2 Ne4 10. Qb4 {This is the ideal square for the queen, where it attacks b7 and prevents castling. Black has no choice but to spend a tempo defending b7, enabling us to bring our knight into the fold.} b6 (10... Qc8 11. Rab1 {It is crucial to induce ...b6, which further weakens the c6-square and makes it even harder for Black to properly develop his knight.} b6 12. c4 c5 13. dxc5 bxc5 14. Qxb8 $1 {An important tactical detail. White wins a piece.} Rxb8 15. Rxb8 $18) 11. Ne5 {We now threaten the deadly Bb5+, forcing Black to enter full retreat mode.} Nd6 {The most resilient defensive move, preparing to castle kingside by slicing off our control over the f8-square. In order to keep the initiative aflame, we must immediately find greener pastures for the lady.} (11... Nd7 $2 12. Bb5 Nef6 13. Nc6 $18 {A picturesque final position! Black drops the queen due to the threat of checkmate on e7.}) (11... c5 {Black's desire to prepare castling is understandable, but the queen saunters on over to the adjacent square, and Black's problems are multiplied hundredfold!} 12. Qa4+ Nd7 13. Bb5 $1 Nef6 14. Bg5 $18 {Black is tied hand and foot!}) (11... f6 $4 12. Bb5+ c6 13. Nxc6 $18) 12. Qa4+ {This move underscores the effect of inducing ...b6: if the pawn was still on b7, Black would effortlessly parry the check with ...c7-c6. Now, ...c6 is no longer possible, and 12...Nd7 runs into a gorgeous refutation that ties Black up completely. Black has no choice but to swallow the bitter pill and move the king.} Kf8 (12... Nd7 13. Ba6 $3 {A magnificent prophylactic move, taking away the c8-square from Black's queen (once again, Black pays a very steep price for weakening the light squares on the queenside). The point of this becomes clear if Black plays 13...f6:} f6 14. Nc6 $18 {The queen is trapped!}) 13. Bxf5 Nxf5 14. c4 {It is obviously not enough to force Black's king onto a compromised square. We now turn our attention back to the queenside, prying open the c-file and adding fuel to the fire.} dxc4 (14... f6 15. Nd3 g5 {The attempt to generate kingside counterplay (which resembles our own g4-h4 idea) falls short on account of a lovely counterblow.} 16. g4 $1 {Counter-attacking the knight and summarily halting Black's kingside pawn play. Needless to say, we are more than willing to part ways with the g4-pawn in order to win an additional tempo.} Nh6 17. cxd5 {An important insertion, using the weakness of the e6-pawn to open up the c-file. The bishop is poisoned, since Black will get massacred on the e6-square and in the center.} exd5 (17... gxf4 18. Nxf4 {[%cal Rf4e6]} Qd7 19. Qa3+ $1 {We are happy to trade queens, but only on our terms! Taking on d7 enables Black to develop the knight and open up escape squares for the a8-rook.} Qd6 (19... Kg8 20. dxe6 Qd6 21. Qc3 $18) 20. Qxd6+ {Now, the time has come!} cxd6 21. Nxe6+ Ke7 22. Nc7 Nd7 23. Nxa8 Rxa8 24. f3 $18 {White's central pawn phalanx is totally decisive. We will follow up by infiltrating down the c-file with Rac1, doubling rooks if necessary. It is instructive to observe how Black's knights are utterly dominated by our pawns - in particular, the d4 and g4-pawns take away the crucial e5 and f5 squares, condeming the horsies to a passive existence.}) 18. Bg3 Nxg4 19. Rac1 c6 20. Nb4 $18 {The pawn cannot be defended, and our bloodthirsty heavy pieces will tear Black's pitiful army to shreds.}) 15. Qxc4 {Resigning is not the worst option for Black here! He is totally and completely paralyzed, and we will follow up with Rac1. Black's extra pawn could not possibly matter less.}) (7... O-O 8. Bxe4 dxe4 9. Nd2 Bxc3 10. bxc3 Nd7 11. Qe2 $14) 8. bxc3 Bxc3 9. Rb1 {It has already become apparent that the pawn sacrifice is more than justified. Black is saddled with the twin threats of Rxb7 and Bxf5 followed by Qd3, recovering the pawn in both cases. More importantly, the incredible vulnerable placement of Black's bishop grants White a virtually decisive initiative.} b6 (9... Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Ba5 11. Qb5+ {Taking the b7-pawn with the queen is quite a bit more problematic for Black than 11.Rxb7, which would enable Black to ferry their king to safety.} Nc6 12. Qxb7 Ne7 13. c4 {Opening up the c-file is almost always a good idea in the Jobava London, and this position is no exception.} dxc4 14. Rfc1 O-O (14... c3 15. Qa6 {An important nuance, forcing the bishop away from its protection of the c3-pawn. In order to keep the bishop on a5, Black must seriously weaken seventh rank.} c6 16. Rb7 {Only now, after Black has opened up a direct line of fire to the knight, do we infiltrate with the rook! Black is under assault from all sides.} O-O 17. Rxe7 Qxe7 18. Qxa5 $18 {The two pieces are incomparably more powerful than the rook, and the c3-pawn is condemned. Victory is a matter of rather straightforward technique.}) 15. Qa6 {It is wise to evacuate the queen from b7 before recapturing on c4. The immediate 15.Rxc4 unnecessarily allows 15...Rb8, forcing trades and relieving the pressure.} Bb6 16. Rxc4 $16 {Black is devoid of any active counterplay. We, on the other hand, will turn up the heat on the queenside by doubling on the c-file. Also keep in mind the idea of a4-a5. Finally, it is worth noting that ...Nd5 is never dangerous on account of the prosaic Bg3, and we can kick the knight back at our leisure with e3-e4.}) 10. Bxf5 exf5 11. Qd3 {[%cal Rd3c3,Rd3f5] Fork city!} Ba5 12. Qxf5 {We have recovered the pawn, and Black's position is in shambles.} O-O 13. Ng5 {Just when it seems like Black has gotten his king to relative safety, we gang up on the monarch with our queen and minor pieces. The following sequence - 15 moves long - is hardly forced, but it clearly illuminates the extent of White's dominance, and culminates with one of the most beautiful mating combinations that we have ever come across!} g6 14. Qh3 h5 15. g4 {A thematic attacking idea, shattering Black's kingside.} Qd7 16. gxh5 {Transforming our advantage. With the g-file wide open, White's attack will not stop with the queens off the board; the rooks are more than happy to pick up the slack!} Qxh3 17. Nxh3 gxh5 18. Kh1 $1 {Much stronger than the penny-pinching 18.Bxc7. We have far greater fish to fry!} Kh7 19. Ng5+ Kg6 20. Rg1 {Setting up the threat of Ne6+, and driving Black's king further into the abyss.} Kf5 21. Nh7 {Clearing g5 for a more deserving piece.} Rh8 22. Rg5+ Ke6 23. Re5+ Kd7 24. Nf6+ Kc6 {Black's king has been rudely shuttled all the way from the kingside to the queenside. Now, 25.Rxd5 obviously keeps a decisive advantage, but we came across a far more beautiful crusher that we cannot help but include:} 25. c4 dxc4 26. Rc5+ $3 {Simply divine! The rook is sacrificed in order to establish control of the b-file. Note that the rook on b1, which thus far has patiently awaited its turn, finally has its moment in the sun! Together with the knight and bishop, it is the architect of the mating construction.} bxc5 (26... Kb7 27. Rxa5 $18 {A crucial detail!}) 27. d5# {Checkmate to his majesty!}) (5... c5 $2 {This incredibly common move is almost always a mistake (typical idea #5), since it allows White to entrench the knight on b5 and consign Black's knight to a miserable existence on the edge of the board.} 6. Nb5 Na6 {In contrast to the 3...e6 line, there is no other way to parry the threat of Nc7+.} 7. c3 Be7 8. dxc5 $1 {An important and profound positional idea (typical idea #6). Trading away our pawn on d4 seems anti-positional at first blush, but concrete considerations always transcend general reasoning! The point of this move is, of course, to clear the d4-square for our knight. Once it moves back to d4, Black will suddenly be confronted with a host of incredibly nasty positional threats, and his poor coordination will not permit him to address all of them at once.} Bxc5 {This is obviously forced, since the knight must remain on a6 to guard c7.} 9. Nbd4 {The point! White has no less than three serious positional threats: 1) Nxf5, gaining the bishop pair (although temporarily) and leaving the d-pawn isolated and weak; 2) Bxa6, crippling the queenside pawn structure; 3) Qa4+, forcing the king to f8.} O-O {This is unquestionably the lesser evil, but it leaves Black with a pawn structure that is worse than George Washington's teeth!} (9... Bg6 10. Qa4+ Nd7 11. Bxa6 bxa6 12. Ne5 $18 {White has a raging initiative, and Black has no way to relieve the pressure whatsoever. The knight from e5 cannot be kicked out (...f6 runs into Nxe6 and ...Bd6 drops a minor piece to Nxg6), and we can add even more heat with h2-h4 and/or c2-c4. In the worst case scenario, White can always snatch the pawn on a6 and castle kingside, when Black will have no compensation whatsoever. But there will likely be no need to cash in your chips in this way.}) 10. Nxf5 exf5 11. Bxa6 bxa6 12. O-O $16 {Our next move will be Qd3, hitting two pawns at the same time. Following that, we can bring one of our rooks to d1 and tickle Black's knight with either Be5 or Bg5. Black will not be able to hang on to all of his pawns for very long!}) (5... a6 {Securing the b5-square is strategically sensible, but contains a serious concrete drawback: Black leaves the e5-square unattended. Consequently, we adopt the typical plan of kingside expansion, sticking a knight on e5 and following up either with g2-g4 and h2-h4, or (if Black makes a luft square for the bishop) with Bd3, shifting our focus to the queenside.} 6. Ne5 Bd6 (6... h6 {This is probably Black's best move from an objective standpoint, but it leads to a familiar type of position in which White is definitely having all the fun.} 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. cxd3 {As always, we recapture with the pawn to increase our central control, open the c-file, and carve out a path to b3 for our queen. This position is quite similar to the one that arises after 5...Bd6 6.Ne5 h6 7.Bd3 Bxd3 8.cxd3; the addition of the move ...h6 does not substantively alter the nature of the ensuing struggle. If anything, ...Bd6 is a significantly more useful move, so we would argue that this is a somewhat improved version of an already-pleasant position.} Nbd7 9. O-O Nxe5 {This is the only way for Black to justify delaying the development of their DSB; remember that this move is not possible when the bishop is on d6 due to dxe5, winning a piece. Now, recapturing with the pawn makes little positional sense.} 10. Bxe5 $14 {Black has gotten rid of the knight, but the other pieces are more than happy to pick up the slack.} Bd6 11. h3 {Given the relatively docile nature of the position, we have the luxury to spend a tempo creating luft for our king and preventing a knight sortie on g4, which will become important once our queen leaves its initial square.} O-O (11... Bxe5 12. dxe5 Nd7 13. d4 O-O 14. f4 $36 {Black has no good answer to the threat of f4-f5, with tremendous play on the kingside.}) 12. Qb3 Rb8 {As usual, the impulsive 12...b5 would be met with 13.Ne2 followed by Rac1, accentuating the weakness of the c6-square.} 13. Rac1 {There is no question that Black can keep the balance with computer-like precision, but our vast experience in such positions convinces us that Black has a very unenviable task ahead of him.} Nd7 14. Bxd6 cxd6 15. Rc2 {Preparing to double rooks and assert our dominance on the queenside.} Qb6 16. Qa4 {Obviously, the queen trade would play right into Black's hands.} Nf6 17. Rfc1 $14 {Our next step is to involve the pawns with b2-b4, increasing our control over key squares on the queenside. Furthermore, Black has quite a few tactical minefields to sidestep; the most natural move walks right into a vicious tactical shot.} Rfc8 $2 18. Nxd5 $1 {Bang! This is why we went through the trouble of doubling rooks!} Rxc2 19. Nxf6+ $1 {Capturing the queen is a lot less clear after ...Rxc1+.} gxf6 20. Qxc2 $18 {Black is down a pawn, and his kingside is in shambles. We will follow up with Qc7, when our opponent's position will collapse like a house of cards.}) (6... Nbd7 7. g4 Nxe5 {As usual, this is clearly superior to 7...Bg6, although it occurs quite a bit more rarely in online play.} (7... Bg6 8. h4 {A situation that you should be intimately familiar with by now! To prevent the loss of a minor piece, Black has to accept a decisive weakening of his kingside.} h5 9. Nxg6 fxg6 10. g5 Ne4 11. Nxe4 dxe4 12. Bg2 $18 {Bortnyk-Ajay, Chess.com (blitz) 2021. This position hardly needs elaboration!}) 8. gxf5 {Amazingly, we have transposed to 3...a6 4.e3 Nc6 5.Nf3 Bf5 6.Ne5 e6 7.g4 Nxe5 8.gxf5, so we advise you to head on over to the 3...a6 file and refresh your memory on the subsequent moves.}) 7. g4 {Taking up the gauntlet!} Bxe5 (7... Bg6 8. h4 h5 9. Nxg6 fxg6 10. Qd3 {As is the custom, we immediately drag Black's king into the open.} Kf7 11. gxh5 gxh5 12. Rg1 $18 {There is no good answer to the devastating threat of Qg6+, since 12...Ng4 runs into 13.Bg5 followed by either f2-f3 or Bh3.}) 8. dxe5 Nxg4 {An important position that has occurred frequently in the blitz practice of the authors of this course. It is vital to avoid the tempting 9.Rg1, due to 9...g5. We have both lost countless games in precisely this fashion, especially to Minh Le and Firouzja. Instead, we must target the knight on g4 straightaway.} 9. f3 $1 {Chasing the knight away. We saw a very similar idea (and position) in the 5...Bd6 6.Ne5 0-0 line; here, Black's king is still in the center, but that does not change any of White's ideas. As always, ...Qh4+ is coolly rebuffed by Bg3.} (9. Rg1 $2 {Incredibly tempting, but wrong!} g5 $1 10. Bg3 Nc6 $19 {White loses the e5-pawn and the game.}) 9... g5 {A very difficult move to find, since this involves a minor piece sacrifice (note that this is Black's best move in the 5...Bd6 6.Ne5 0-0 version as well). The alternative, however, leads to a position that can only be described as hellish.} (9... Qh4+ $4 10. Bg3 $18) (9... Nh6 10. e4 {In contrast to the ...0-0 line, here this move is incredibly effective, since the capture on e4 enables us to favorably trade queens and draw Black's king onto a vulnerable square.} dxe4 (10... Bh3 11. Qd2 {Ignoring the forbidden fruit! Capturing on h3 would have led nowhere good after ...Qh4+.} Bxf1 12. Bg5 $1 {Surgical chess. With this intermediate move, we put a permanent stop to ...Qh4-related shenanigans and exert our complete dominance over the kingside.} Qd7 13. Rxf1 d4 14. Ne2 Nc6 15. Nf4 Nxe5 16. Nh5 $40 {Black has no good way to defend against the twin threats of Nxg7+ and Bxh6 followed by Nf6.}) (10... Bg6 11. h4 {[%cal Rh4h5]} Bh5 12. exd5 $16 {Black is in deep trouble, since 12...exd5 loses immediately to 13.Bg5 Qd7 14.Bh3, with total devastation to follow.}) 11. Qxd8+ Kxd8 12. fxe4 Bg6 13. h4 {The customary move, threatening h4-h5 and forcing Black's bishop to leave the g-file. In turn, this will enable us to gang up on the tender g7-pawn.} Bh5 14. Rg1 Bg6 {Obviously, ...Rg8 would have dropped the knight on h6. Black's objectively best option was to simply abandon the g7 pawn, but we trust that you can handle the resulting position on your own!} 15. Be2 $18 {Quiet and deadly. There is no defense to h5, which will pick up the bishop.}) 10. fxg4 gxf4 11. h4 $3 {Delaying the capture of the bishop to take away the h4-square from Black's queen. The immediate 11.gxf5 leads to a total mess after 11...Qh4+.} Bg6 (11... Be4 12. Nxe4 dxe4 13. Qxd8+ Kxd8 14. exf4 $18) 12. exf4 {We decide to refrain from going after the bishop altogether (12.h5 is certainly possible, but we concluded that the position after 12...fxe3 13.hxg6 fxg6 is extremely messy and hard to handle). Instead, we reestablish material equality and prepare for a devastating kingside pawn storm.} d4 (12... h5 13. f5 $1 {Hitting where it hurts! Taking on f5 results in immediate disaster after Nxd5, so Black has no choice but to allow the partial dissolution of his center and the creation of a powerful protected passer on the kingside.} Bh7 14. fxe6 fxe6 15. g5 Bf5 16. Bd3 $16 {The monster pawn on g5 affords us the clearly better game. Note that the pawn on e5, though isolated, is quite easy to defend. More precisely, Black does not have the time to attack it, since we threaten Bxf5 and the trade on d3 enables us to stick our queen on g6. Even if it were to be captured, the open e-file would probably favor us anyway.}) 13. h5 {Needless to say, this is vastly superior to the listless 13.Ne2. The resulting endgame is utterly miserable for Black.} dxc3 14. Qxd8+ Kxd8 15. hxg6 fxg6 16. bxc3 Nc6 17. Rb1 {Black is under fire from all sides, e.g.} b5 18. Bg2 Kd7 19. Rd1+ $18) (5... Nc6 6. Bd3 {Do not be fooled by the appearance of total symmetry: the extra tempo gives us a very pleasant game. It is worth observing that 6.Ne5 - which is our recommended response to 3...a6 4.e3 Nc6 5.Nf3 Bf5 - is less attractive here since Black has already established a pawn on e6.} Bxd3 (6... Bg6 7. O-O Bd6 8. Ne2 $1 {Trading on our own terms! This clever positional move has a lot more meat on the bone than the meek 8.Bg3. White prepares c2-c4, which underscores the awkwardness of the knight on c6. White's extra tempo means that playing copycat with 8...Ne7 will not end well.} O-O (8... Ne7 9. c4 c5 10. cxd5 exd5 11. Qa4+ Nc6 {Mr. Copycat is in for a rather nasty surprise in this position:} 12. Ba6 $3 {Stunning and immediately decisive. Black's queenside collapses faster than the Stock Market in 1929.} Qa5 {This is the only way to avoid losing a minor piece, though it leads to a dead lost endgame nonetheless.} (12... Qc7 13. Bxd6 Qxd6 14. Bxb7 $18) (12... Bxf4 13. Bxb7 $18) 13. Qxa5 Nxa5 14. Bxd6 bxa6 15. Bxc5 $18) 9. c4 dxc4 10. Bxc4 Bh5 11. Bg3 {We certainly do not fear ...Bxf3, which damages our pawn structure on a superficial level but grants us exciting prospects along the g-file, as well as the possibility of asserting dominance in the center with e3-e4.} Bxf3 (11... Bxg3 12. hxg3 {Next, the knight will hop into f4, and our bishop will retreat to e2. After carrying out this mini-regrouping, we will commence queenside operations with Rc1, Qb3, etc.}) 12. gxf3 e5 13. f4 $1 {Fighting fire with fire! This forces Black to give up the e-pawn on our terms.} exd4 (13... e4 $2 14. Bh4 Be7 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Ng3 $18 {Black loses the e-pawn, and with it the game.}) 14. Nxd4 Nxd4 15. Qxd4 $14 {We have a great position, with prospects on both sides of the board. The stock plan in such positions is Kh1 followed by Rg1, but we shouldn't forget about Russian schoolboy-type moves such as Rad1 and (eventually) e3-e4.}) 7. cxd3 Bd6 8. Qb3 Rb8 9. Be5 Bxe5 (9... O-O 10. Rc1 $14) 10. dxe5 Nd7 11. d4 $14) (5... c6 6. Ne5 Nbd7 7. g4 {See 4...c6 5.Nf3 e6 6.Ne5 Nbd7 7.g4.}) (5... Be7 {Developing the bishop to e7 is far less common than the two alternatives, but it has been the choice of countless high-level players, including Armenian GM and opening expert Hrant Melkumyan. Our strategy does not change: we plant the knight on e5 and go either for g2-g4 or for Bd3. In both cases, the type of position reached will be very similar to the one we have already observed in several variations, including 5...Bd6.} 6. Ne5 Nbd7 (6... O-O {A masochistic move! Now, White's kingside pawn storm will be even more devastating.} 7. g4 Bg6 8. h4 h5 9. Nxg6 fxg6 10. g5 Nfd7 11. Bh3 Kf7 12. Qf3 $18 {[%cal Rf3f7] Black cannot keep e6 protected and answer the threat of a discovered check at the same time.}) 7. g4 {At this point in your Jobava journey, this move should be second nature (with the exception of those who have decided to start with this line). Black is already in deep yogurt.} Bg6 (7... Nxe5 8. gxf5 Nc6 9. Rg1 $16) 8. h4 Nxe5 9. dxe5 Ne4 10. h5 Nxc3 (10... Bh4 11. Rh2 Nxc3 12. bxc3 Be4 13. Rb1 Rb8 14. Kd2 $36 {Despite the chaotic appearance of the position, Black is in big trouble. Both of his bishops are vulnerable, and f2-f3 is threatened.}) 11. bxc3 Be4 12. f3 {Black's bishop has run out of squares!} Bh4+ {An empty check.} 13. Ke2 g5 14. Bh2 $18 {Black has run out of ways to stave off the inevitable. He will have some compensation for the pawn due to the somewhat flimsy placement of our monarch, but a few careful consolidating moves, such as Bg2 and Rf1, should render this compensation meaningless.}) (5... h6 {This rare move contains relatively little independent value. After the trade on d3, we will reach a structure and a type of position that we have already seen countless times.} 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. cxd3 Bd6 {Now, 8.Ne5 transposes into a position that we reach via the move order 5...Bd6 6.Ne5 h6 7.Bd3 Bxd3 8.cxd3. However, it turns out that we actually have an even better option: we can go straight to work on the queenside.} (7... Nh5 8. Be5 {We are happy to give up the bishop, but only on our terms!} Nd7 (8... f6 $4 {This horrific move loses on the spot.} 9. Nh4 $18 {No more analysis is required! Black will drop h5 with check, with further material losses to follow.}) 9. Nb5 $1 {Forcing Black's hand.} Nxe5 10. dxe5 $14 {The threat of g2-g4 forces Black to introduce serious kingside weaknesses with 10...g6. We can follow up by castling and reinforcing our central control with d3-d4. Once the knight drops back to c3, it can prepare further central expansion with e3-e4.}) 8. Qb3 {Standard fare! As usual, this forces Black to permanently weaken the queenside, granting us enduring and dangerous play on that side of the board.} b6 (8... Bxf4 $2 9. Qxb7 Nbd7 10. exf4 $16) 9. Bxd6 cxd6 (9... Qxd6 10. e4 Qd8 (10... Qd7 11. Ne5 $16) 11. O-O O-O 12. Rac1 c6 13. Rfe1 $14) 10. Nb5 {Now, Black is best advised to castle, though his position remains quite thankless after 11.Rc1 Ne8 12.e4. it is far more natural to try to chase the knight away from b5, but that leads to immediate calamity:} a6 11. Qa3 $1 {Pow! Indirectly defending the knight and hitting d6, which Black can only defend with his king.} Ke7 12. Rc1 $16 {Moving to the open file and shutting down the b8-knight's development prospects. Black is in terrible shape.}) (5... Ne4 {A rare and rather dubious move, though it has been the consistent choice of several good players online. The simplest response is to trade knights and quickly push c2-c4, reaching a favorable QGD structure that we have already seen in several other lines.} 6. Nxe4 Bxe4 {Interestingly, we have transposed to a known theoretical position that occurs in the Trompowsky Opening (1.d4 Nf6 2.Bg5). There is only one difference: here, it is White to move! We put our extra tempo to immediate use, knocking the bishop off of e4.} 7. Nd2 Bg6 8. c4 c6 (8... Bd6 9. Bxd6 Qxd6 10. Qb3 $14 {with a pleasant edge.}) 9. h4 {It is worth flicking in this move to grab some space on the kingside and soften up the bishop on g6.} h6 10. Qb3 Qb6 11. c5 $1 {A classic idea in the Semi-Slav and many other openings. Black has no choice but to acquiesce to the queen trade, opening up the a-file and handing us a queenside attack on a golden platter.} Qxb3 12. axb3 Nd7 13. b4 $16 {We are playing squarely for two results. We will follow up either with b4-b5, shattering Black's pawn chain and opening up avenues of infiltration, or with Nb3-a5, when Black will have a very hard time defending the b7-pawn, since we (permanently) control the b8-square.}) 6. Ne5 {The customary response (see basic idea #2). White sets up the powerful threat of g2-g4.} h6 {Most knowledgeable players make this move automatically. By opening up an escape square on h7, Black removes the danger from the advance of the g-pawn. As in the case of 6...Nbd7, this is a sign that we should switch our approach to methodical positional play.} (6... O-O {As usual, ignoring the brewing storm on the kingside leads to trouble.} 7. g4 Bxe5 {This is unquestionably the lesser evil, but Black's position will remain utterly miserable.} (7... Bg6 8. h4 h6 (8... h5 {The normal response is to capture the bishop on g6, crippling Black's pawn structure. However, since Black has already castled, it is worth starting with gxh5, in order to pop open the g-file and facilitate the ensuing attack.} 9. gxh5 Nxh5 (9... Bxh5 10. Be2 Bxe2 11. Qxe2 {Black is utterly screwed on the kingside: we will castle queenside, stick a rook on g1, and rush the h-pawn up the board.}) 10. Nxg6 fxg6 11. Bxd6 cxd6 {The smoke has cleared, and the next step is to target Black's incredibly weak kingside pawns.} 12. Qg4 Qf6 13. O-O-O {Cold-blooded and powerful, taking the sting out of ...Qxf2 (which will now be met by Qxe6+).} Nc6 14. Bh3 {Turning up the pressure dial on the e6-pawn.} Rae8 15. f4 {A vital positional resource, ending Black's counterplay along the f-file.} Kh7 16. Nb5 Qd8 17. Rdg1 Rf6 {Black's defenses are stretched to the limit, and a gorgeous maneuver seals the deal.} 18. Bf1 $1 a6 19. Bd3 $18 {The pressure on g6, coupled with the deadly threat of Qxh5+, is decisive.}) (8... Nfd7 $4 9. h5 Be4 10. f3 $18) 9. g5 {This is far more effective than the stereotypical Nxg6.} Nh5 10. Nxg6 fxg6 11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. Qd3 {Attacking g6 and paving the way for a timely queenside castle.} Kf7 13. Be2 $18 {Further analysis is hardly needed! Black's position is in shambles. We can follow up by castling queenside, or by taking direct action on the kingside with gxh6, Bxh5, and Qh7+. In either case, Black's position is virtually resignable.}) (7... Be4 {As usual, this move changes nothing, since the bishop is quickly sent back to g6.} 8. f3 Bg6 9. h4 h5 10. Qd2 c5 11. Nb5 $16) 8. dxe5 Nxg4 9. f3 {Not fearing the check on h4, which is parried with Bg3. Black's knight is sent into exile on h6, when our kingside attack, coupled with our superior piece placement and the total safety of our own king (after we castle), ensures overwhelming compensation for the pawn.} Nh6 (9... g5 {An ingenious defensive move, attempting to pry the bishop away from its protection of the e3-pawn.} 10. fxg4 gxf4 11. gxf5 Qh4+ {This check appears intimidating, but White's king calmly sidesteps the trouble by remaining in the center.} 12. Ke2 fxe3 13. Qe1 {Protecting the f2-square. We are obviously in no rush to capture the e3 pawn.} Qg4+ 14. Kd3 $1 {An important nuance: the immediate 14.Kxe3 runs into ...d4+, so we have to wait for Black's queen to leave the fourth rank to capture the pawn.} Qxf5+ (14... Nc6 15. Qxe3 Qxf5+ 16. Kd2 d4 17. Qg3+ Kh8 18. Ne2 Nxe5 19. Rd1 $14) 15. Kxe3 d4+ 16. Kxd4 {To our knowledge, this is the only position in the entire course in which White's king sits on d4 - and the queens are still on the board! Upon closer inspection, it becomes clear that the king is unassailable: it will quickly evacuate back to e3, and Black's initiative fizzles out within a few moves.} Nc6+ 17. Ke3 Qxe5+ 18. Kd2 Rad8+ 19. Bd3 Qg5+ 20. Qe3 $18 {Phew! Our king has finally reached a safe square, where it is surrounded by friendly forces. Black has nothing better than to acquiesce to the queen trade, when victory will be a matter of straightforward technique.}) (9... Nxe3 10. Bxe3 Nc6 {Black threatens ...d4.} 11. Qe2 $1 {A superb defensive move that nips Black's initiative in the bud. We prepare to meet ...d4 with Rd1, when Black's play in the center will be instantly disarmed.} d4 (11... Nxe5 12. O-O-O Nc6 13. Rg1 $18) 12. Rd1 {It may seem more intuitive to castle queenside, but 12.0-0-0 would be a grave error on account of 12...Qh4, when White has no way to save both minor pieces. The issue is that 13.Bf2 runs into Qf4+. In this version, after 12...Qh4+ 13.Bf2 Black's queen is unable to move away with check. One of the few situations in which remaining in the center is more desirable!} Qe7 13. Bxd4 $16 {Black's compensation for the piece is virtually nonexistant.}) 10. h4 {Grabbing space and preventing the check on h4 once and for all. Note that it is important for us to complete our queenside development and shuttle our king to safety before initiating the kingside attack in earnest.} Nc6 (10... f6 11. e4 {Meeting fire with fire! Opening up the center will enable us to more effectively take advantage of Black's incredibly weak pawns.} dxe4 12. Qxd8 Rxd8 13. fxe4 {Just because the queens are off the board doesn't mean that White's attack is over! Au contraire, we are just getting started!} Bg6 (13... fxe5 14. Bg5 $18 {[%cal Rg5d8,Re4f5]}) 14. h5 Be8 15. Rg1 $18 {The twin threats of exf6 and Bxh6 spell immediate doom.}) (10... c5 11. e4 dxe4 12. Qxd8 Rxd8 13. fxe4 Bg4 14. Rg1 $18) 11. Qe2 {The queen is better placed on e2 than on d2, since ...d4 can now be met with 0-0-0. Furthermore, after we castle, e3-e4 will come with far greater effect due to the x-ray that our rook will exert against Black's queen.} f6 12. O-O-O {Setting up the devastating threat of e3-e4.} fxe5 13. Bxh6 gxh6 14. e4 Bg6 15. exd5 exd5 16. Nxd5 Kh8 17. Qe3 $18 {Black's center has been fully shattered, and we now turn our attention to the kingside. Among other things, White threatens h4-h5 and Qxh6, as well as the prosaic Nb6.}) (6... Nbd7 {Unfortunately, 7.g4 is no longer effective on account of 7...Bxe5 8.dxe5 Nxg4, when Black will follow up by capturing another pawn on e5. Therefore, we transition to straightforward positional play, completing our kingside development.} 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. cxd3 {As usual, we recapture with the pawn, leading to the classical central pawn formation. Our powerful knight on e5, coupled with our total control over key central squares, promises White a very pleasant game.} O-O 9. O-O a6 {Preventing Nb5.} (9... Qe7 10. Rc1 c6 11. Qb3 Rab8 12. h3 $14 {Now that our queen has left d1, it is important to take the sting out of the move ...Nh5, which can now be met by the prosaic Bh2. In fact, our next step is to drop the bishop down to h2 (or g3) on our volition, in order to reinforce the knight with f2-f4. The computer assigns White a very modest plus, but our experience leads us to believe that Black's cramped position and lack of a clear plan affords White excellent prospects in the middlegame.}) (9... c6 $4 {A thematic blunder, leaving the bishop on d6 undefended.} 10. Nxc6 $18) (9... c5 $2 {This is somewhat better than 9...c6, but succumbs to a similar tactic:} 10. Nc6 $3 {Amazingly, this lovely tactical shot is even more effective than 10.Nxf7.} bxc6 (10... Qc7 11. Ne7+ Kh8 12. Nb5 $18) 11. Bxd6 {Amazingly, this position has occurred once over the board - in a game played by none other than Shakhriyar Mamedyarov, in the year 2000! Black suffers decisive material losses.} cxd4 (11... Re8 12. dxc5 e5 13. b4 Re6 14. d4 $18) 12. Bxf8 dxc3 13. Ba3 $18 {Black does not even manage to gain a pawn for the exchange.}) 10. Rc1 {Bringing our rook into position.} Qe7 (10... b5 $2 11. Ne2 $16 {Black's last move was a terrible positional mistake, and we exploit it immediately by reinforcing our control over the newly-weakened c6-square. Before planting our knight on c6, it is worth piling up on the c-file with Qc2.}) 11. Na4 {A typical positional move, aiming to situate the knight on c5 when the moment is right. Notice, once again, the indispensable role played by White's central pawn mass: all of the crucial squares in the center, particularly e5 and e4, are under White's control. It is incredibly difficult for Black to find helpful improving moves.} h6 12. Rc2 {White's campaign is one of slow, but steady, queenside expansion. The queen will make way for the other rook, and once we are doubled on the c-file, the queenside pressure will reach a peak.} c6 13. a3 Rac8 14. b4 $14 {We continue to improve our position in countless ways. The knight will hop into c5, and Black will ultimately be forced into a nasty positional concession.}) 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. cxd3 Nbd7 9. Rc1 O-O {In contrast to the position that arises after 6...Nbd7, the presence of the pawn on h6 affords us an exciting additional possibility.} 10. h4 {The experienced Jobava player will not bat an eye at this move, which sets up the potentially devastating advance of the g-pawn to g5. Black is best advised to immediately remove the knight from the pawn's line of fire.} Ne8 (10... Qe7 11. g4 {The threat of g4-g5 is instantly crushing, e.g.} c5 12. g5 Nh7 {Obviously, opening up the h-file with ...hxg5 merely hastens Black's defeat!} 13. gxh6 g6 14. Qg4 $40 {White has opened up a raging attack on Black's king. We threaten both the prosaic h4-h5 and the lovely Nxg6, and Black's impotent army is powerless to stop the avalanche.}) (10... Bxe5 {As usual, removing the knight from the board does not promise Black a single measure of solace. The knight will be capably replaced by an even nastier pawn.} 11. dxe5 Ne8 12. d4 $16 {Reinforcing our total central dominance. We will follow up by rushing the g-pawn up the board, posing decisive threats against Black's king.}) 11. Qb3 {Playing on both sides of the board! We deploy our queen to an optimal square and increase the queenside pressure.} Rb8 12. Ne2 {An important positional detail, buttressing the bishop on f4 and taking the venom out of ...f7-f6.} c6 13. a3 {Black's incredibly passive situation means that we are in absolutely no hurry to make any committal moves. Defending the b4-square is always a good idea.} f6 14. Ng6 Rf7 15. h5 $14 {Jobava London-3...Bf5  2023 We will follow up by gradually intensifying the kingside pressure with f3 and g4, while the king can be tucked away to safety on d2. White's robust central pawn phalanx means that our king will almost always remain completely safe in the center.}) 4. e3 Qb6 (4... Bf5 {See 3...Bf5 4.Nf3 c6.}) 5. Rb1 {Not the prettiest looking move, but it gets the job done. Furthermore, we never castle queenside in this line, so there is no chance that we will regret this move down the line. Finally, the rook on b1 can be a major asset in the ensuing queenside battle, helping to prepare the typical b4-b5 minority attack.} (5. a3 $5 {This is not our main recommendation, but it is a cute trap nonetheless:} Qxb2 $4 6. Na4 $18) 5... Bf5 6. Bd3 {We develop the bishop and immediately confront Black with an important decision.} Bxd3 (6... e6 7. Bxf5 exf5 {This exchange may seem to favor Black due to his increased control over the e4-square, but in reality, the f5-pawn is a serious target that we now use as fuel for a furious kingside expansion.} 8. h3 {White's intentions are far more violent than simply making luft for the bishop on h2. We are preparing to tear Black apart with g2-g4!} Be7 (8... h5 {This stops g2-g4, but creates more holes on the kingside. We now transition to straightforward development, and follow up with pawn play on the queenside.} 9. Nf3 Nbd7 10. O-O Ne4 (10... Be7 11. Ne2 {A versatile move, with two main ideas. The knight can be repurposed to d3 (via f4, once the bishop moves), and by un-blocking the c-pawn, we are preparing rapid queenside expansion with an eventual c2-c4.} Ne4 (11... O-O 12. b3 {Everything is ready for the queenside attack!} Qa6 13. c4 $1 {Anyway!} dxc4 (13... Qxa2 14. Ra1 Qb2 15. Qd3 dxc4 16. bxc4 {In order to un-trap the queen, Black has to give up f5:} Qb6 17. Rfb1 Qd8 18. Qxf5 $18 {Note that we avoided 18.Rxb7 because of Nc5! White's massive queenside pressure, coupled with our pawn superiority in the center, grants us a decisive advantage.}) 14. bxc4 Qxc4 15. Rxb7 $14 {Black's queenside is in tatters, and he suffers from weaknesses on the other side of the board as well.}) 12. Nd2 {Getting rid of Black's strongest piece.} g5 {An attempt to jump-start a kingside attack, but it falls rather short.} (12... Ndf6 13. Nxe4 fxe4 (13... Nxe4 14. f3 {Pushing away the knight.} Nf6 15. Qd3 {Attacking f5 and inducing yet another weakness.} g6 16. Be5 $16 {[%cal Re3e4] White's next step is to blast open the center with e3-e4.}) 14. c4 {Time for the queenside pawn storm! This positional sacrifice shatters Black's central pawn chain.} dxc4 (14... O-O 15. Be5 {[%cal Re2f4] The knight is coming to f4, and Black is forced to capture on c4 anyway.} dxc4 {See 14..dxc4 15.Be5 0-0.}) 15. Be5 {First, we centralize the bishop. The ability to take on f6 will serve us well in the long run, especially if our knight moves on to c3 or g3.} O-O 16. b3 $1 {Prying open the b-file.} cxb3 17. Rxb3 Qa6 18. Ng3 $16 {[%cal Rg3h5,Rg3f5] White's compensation for the pawn is overwhelming: we are already threatening to win the pawn back with Nxh5 and Bxf6-Nxe4, but the prospect of Nf5 is even scarier.}) 13. Bh2 g4 14. Kh1 {[%cal Rc2c4] Calmly withdrawing the king to a safer spot, underscoring the ineffectiveness of Black's pawn invasion. Black's monarch, on the other hand, has no safe haven. Our next move is c2-c4, prying open the queenside and leading to the collapse of Black's pawn structure. A sample line:} O-O-O 15. c4 Nxd2 16. Qxd2 dxc4 17. b3 {The same idea that we saw in the 12...Ndf6 13.Nxe4 fxe4 14.c4 line, forcing open the b-file and leading to a long-lasting attack against Black's king.} cxb3 18. Rxb3 Qa6 19. Qc2 $18 {[%cal Rf1b1,Rc2f5] Targeting f5 and x-raying Black's king. Rfb1 is coming next.}) (10... g6 11. Ne2 Bg7 12. b3 O-O 13. c4 $16) 11. Nxe4 fxe4 12. Nd2 Be7 13. c4 {Undermining Black's pawn chain, and starting a furious queenside pawn storm.} Nf6 14. b4 $1 {Simple and powerful. We threaten to blast open the queenside with b4-b5, and the pawn is untouchable due to the pin against the queen.} a6 15. a4 $14 {[%cal Rb4b5,Rf4e5] White is having all the fun: apart from the imminent threat of b4-b5, there are many ways to improve the position and accentuate the pressure, such as Qb3 and Be5. If Black is forced to take on c4, the e4-pawn will become a long-term weakness. Also note the awkwardness of the h5-pawn: anytime Black castles, Be5 will carry the additional threat of Bxf6 and Qxh5.}) (8... Bb4 9. g4 {Ignoring the possibility of ...Ne4, which doesn't bother us!} Ne4 (9... fxg4 10. hxg4 Ne4 11. a3 Nxc3 (11... Ba5 12. Nge2 $16) 12. bxc3 Bxc3+ {Now, time for some Jobava London magic: the intuitive move is 13.Kf1, but we use the king as an agent of attack:} 13. Ke2 $1 Qa6+ 14. Qd3 $1 Qxd3+ 15. Kxd3 {The idea is revealed: the bishop on c3 is under attack, and Black has no time to defend b7. His position collapses.} Ba5 16. Rxb7 $18) 10. f3 $1 {The genius of the rook's placement on b1 is revealed in this line: by sacrificing the c3-pawn, we open up the rook and reveal the hidden vulnerability of Black's queen. Black cannot hang on to the extra pawn, since f5 hangs as well.} Nxc3 11. bxc3 Bxc3+ 12. Kf2 Qa6 13. gxf5 Nd7 14. Qe2 Qxe2+ (14... Qxa2 15. Rxb7 $18) 15. Nxe2 $18 {[%cal Re2c3,Rb1b7]}) 9. g4 fxg4 10. hxg4 Nbd7 11. g5 Ne4 12. Nxe4 dxe4 13. c3 O-O-O 14. b4 $16 {[%cal Gc3c4,Gb4b5] White's queenside attack is virtually unstoppable.}) (6... Nbd7 $4 {An incredibly common blunder!} 7. Bxf5 $18) (6... Bg6 {With this move, Black keeps the tension and tosses the ball back into White's court.} 7. h4 {A typical Jobava move: we aim to force Black to trade bishops on our terms. By forcing Black to take on d3, we will create the desirable d3-d4-e3 pawn mass, enabling us to assert our dominance in the center and on the queenside.} e6 (7... h5 $2 {As usual, this move loosens Black's control over the g6-square, making the trade far more desirable!} 8. Bxg6 fxg6 9. Qd3 Kf7 10. Nf3 $18 {[%cal Rf3e5,Rf3g5]}) 8. h5 Bxd3 9. cxd3 {The desirable structure has been obtained. Now, it is time to develop our kingside and expand in the center.} h6 10. Nf3 Be7 (10... Nbd7 11. e4 Bb4 12. e5 {The knight has nowhere good to go, since d7 is taken up by the other knight!} Ng4 13. a3 {Note the power of the rook on b1, which shines through in the event that Black trades on c3. The awkwardness of Black's queen is on full display here.} Be7 14. Na4 Qa6 15. Ng1 $1 f5 (15... f6 16. Qxg4 Qxa4 17. Qxg7 Rf8 18. Bxh6 $18) 16. f3 $18 {It turns out that the knight on g4 is trapped!}) 11. e4 O-O 12. e5 {Driving the pawn down and gaining more space.} Nfd7 13. Rh3 $16 {[%cal Rh3g3,Re1f1,Rf1g1] A sexy move indeed! We lift the rook up to g3, creating massive threats against Black's king, which has just lost its last loyal defender. White's pawn structure is visually unappealing, but our central pawn mass does a fantastic job of holding Black's counterplay at bay. For a more thorough explanation, see the note to Black's 11th move in the main line (6...Bxd3 7.cxd3).} c5 14. dxc5 Nxc5 {Now, it is important to avoid 15.d4, which grants Black the e4-square. Instead, we use our other pawn to push Black's knight back.} 15. b4 Ncd7 16. Kf1 {A little prophylaxis before launching the kingside attack. We've taken the sting out of ...Bxb4, since the knight will no longer hang with check.} Bxb4 (16... Nc6 17. b5 Na5 18. Qd2 Kh7 19. g4 $40 {The attack commences! Black has nothing going on the Q-side, while we are fully ready to tear Black apart on the other side of the board.}) 17. a3 $18) 7. cxd3 {As usual, we recapture with the pawn (see typical ideas #1 for a more thorough explanation of the potency of White's central pawn mass). We now carry out a typical set of steps: first, we complete our kingside development. Following that, we initiate central expansion with e3-e4, giving us a pleasant and easy game.} e6 8. Nf3 Be7 9. O-O {Step 1 has been achieved.} O-O 10. e4 {Time for phase 2, expanding in the center and preparing to shove the pawn down to e5, cramping Black even further.} (10. b4 $5 {A worthwhile alternative, initiating immediate queenside play along typical lines. Of course, the pawn is untouchable due to a3.} a6 11. a4 Bxb4 (11... Nbd7 12. a5 Qd8 13. h4 $14) 12. Bd6 $1 $16 {A bolt from the blue, winning at least an exchange.}) 10... Nbd7 11. e5 Ne8 {It is worth pausing and appreciating the unique potency of White's central pawn structure. The d4-e5/e6-d5 pawn chains resemble an Advanced French, with the chief difference being the additional presence of White's pawn on d3. Visually, White's pawn mass appears rather ugly, but the pawn on d3 is actually a great asset. Any time Black pushes the c-pawn - and this is Black's only active idea - White can capture on c5 and rebuild the pawn chain with d3-d4, highlighting the hidden value inherent in having doubled pawns!} 12. h4 {Expanding on the kingside. Just because we are castled kingside certainly does not mean that we can't attack on that side of the board! Note that this move does not commit us to anything in particular: the pawn on h4 is not a serious weakness, and we are not obliged to follow up with h4-h5. We are in charge on all three sides of the board: on ther queenside, we are ready to act with b2-b4. In the center, Black has nothing going and 12...c5 fails to break up our center (see main line). On the kingside, we can expand gradually with h5, g2-g3, Kg2, Rh1, etc.} c5 {Black aims to generate a semblance of activity.} (12... h6 13. Ne2 {White aims to regroup the minor pieces by retreating the bishop to e3 (this also discourages ...c5), and repositioning the knight on f4, from where it can jump to h5.} c5 14. dxc5 Nxc5 15. Be3 Qa6 16. b4 $5 {Using tactics to force the knight out of c5. Once again, it is important to refrain from 16.d4, which would grant Black a powerful post on e4.} Nxd3 (16... Nd7 17. a4 Rc8 18. Qb3 $16 {Black is pushed to the edge of the board, and we will wrest the c-file from his control with Rfc1 on the next move.}) 17. b5 Qa3 18. Rb3 $18) (12... f6 {This idea is typical for French Advance-type positions, but in this case it falls short.} 13. g3 {Reinforcing the bishop and taking the sting out of ...fxe5.} fxe5 14. dxe5 c5 15. d4 {Meeting Black at the top! This move grants Black a protected passer in the center, but enables us to establish complete dominance over that pawn by gaining the d4-square for our knight.} cxd4 16. Nxd4 {Black lacks the piece activity and coordination to kick the knight out of d4. Furthermore, the e6-pawn is a long-term weakness that Black must babysit at all times.} Nc7 (16... Bxh4 17. b4 $1 {Ignoring the situation on the kingside! In reality, we are preparing a devious maneuver that will force Black to lose contact with the e6-pawn.} Be7 18. Na4 Qa6 19. b5 Qa5 20. Nxe6 $18) 17. Rc1 {Simple chess! Of course, we could not care less about the b2-pawn, for many reasons (such as 17...Qxb2 18.Na4, picking off the c7-knight).} Rac8 18. h5 {Playing on both flanks! We expand on the kingside, preparing a timely h5-h6.} Qxb2 19. Qg4 {Now, the queen jumps into the game!} Nc5 20. Ncb5 $1 {Deflecting the c7-knight from its control over e6.} Nxb5 21. Nxe6 Rf7 (21... Nxe6 22. Qxe6+ Kh8 23. Rxc8 $18) 22. Nxg7 $1 {Classic Jobava London action! The rook on c8 hangs!} Rc6 23. Nf5+ Kh8 24. Rb1 Qc2 25. Nxe7 Rxe7 26. Rxb5 $18 {Gaining back the piece, with a clearly overwhelming position.}) 13. Na4 {An important move, which ensures that we will remove the knight from d7 as a result of the trade on c5. This will leave Black with a far more passive knight on e8, and grant us the ability to pressure the queenside without obstruction.} Qb5 14. Nxc5 Nxc5 15. dxc5 Bxc5 16. Rc1 Be7 17. Qd2 $16 {Defending b2 and coordinating the rooks. We have countless ways to improve our position, such as d3-d4, Rc2-Rfc1, as well as h4-h5. Black has no easy way to improve the placement of the knight, and suffers from a general lack of coordination and lack of space.} *

