1. d4 Nf6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Bf4 a6 {A solid continuation, covering the b5-square once and for all.} 4. e3 e6 {This is, without question, one of Black's best lines from an objective standpoint. There is no doubt that Black equalizes, but we stress that the position remains complex and difficult to handle from a practical standpoint. From this position, Black has two main setups: the first involves striking at the center right away with ...c7-c5. In response, we will trade on c5 and quickly prepare the typical central breakthrough e3-e4, granting us a nice space advantage and easy piece play. The second plan, more seldom seen at the club player level, involves grabbing as much queenside space as possible with ...b7-b5, and following up with ...Bb7 to prevent e4. We are quite proud of our recommendation against this second plan, which involves quickly closing up the queenside and transitioning our focus to a lightning kingside attack.} (4... Nc6 {This is extremely popular online, with over a hundred thousand games! Although developing the knight to c6 is natural, it blocks the c-pawn, handing White immediate supremacy in the center. Of course, we have also committed the same transgression, but we have the benefit of the extra tempo! We harness our development advantage by quickly shepharding the knight to e5, and following up with g2-g4 in the event that Black develops the bishop to f5.} 5. Nf3 Bg4 (5... Bf5 6. Ne5 Nxe5 (6... e6 7. g4 $1 {The typical shot works to perfection here.} Nxe5 {This is necessary in order to maintain an objectively acceptable position, but from a practical standpoint, White has a major initiative.} (7... Bg6 {This is far more common than 7...Nxe5, but sends Black even further into deep trouble. The only way to extricate the bishop is to introduce near-fatal weaknesses on the kingside.} 8. h4 {As usual, the threat of h5 induces a decisive kingside weakness.} Nxe5 (8... h6 9. Nxg6 fxg6 10. Bd3 Kf7 11. Qf3 $16 {Adding insult to injury! After we castle, the threat of g4-g5 will spell immediate doom to the Black king.}) 9. dxe5 Ne4 10. Nxe4 Bxe4 11. f3 Bg6 12. h5 $18 {A staggering number of games concluded in this fashion. Black loses the bishop.}) 8. gxf5 Nc6 (8... Nc4 9. Bxc4 dxc4 10. fxe6 fxe6 11. Qe2 b5 12. Rg1 $16 {This move prevents the easy development of the bishop. Black's king has nowhere safe to go, and his structure is in shambles.}) (8... Ned7 9. fxe6 fxe6 10. Bh3 {Hitting where it hurts! Note that 10...Qe7 drops the c7-pawn, so Black has no choice but to part ways with e6.} Bb4 (10... Qe7 11. Bxc7 $16) 11. Bxe6 c6 12. Rg1 $16 {Black has zero compensation for the pawn, and ...Ne4 always runs into the devastating Qh5+.}) 9. fxe6 fxe6 10. Bh3 Bd6 {Here, it is important to preserve the bishop pair rather than to greedily capture on e6, which leads to the complete dissolution of White's center after the trade on f4.} (10... Qd7 11. a3 Bd6 12. Bg3 O-O-O 13. Qd3 $14 {White has the bishop pair (for now) and a pleasant bishop. Next, we will castle queenside and can play either in the center (e3-e4) or expand on the kingside with f2-f4.}) 11. Bg5 Qe7 12. e4 {This is the best practical try. The computer gives equality (as usual), but Black's position is remarkably hard to play and traps exist by the basinful.} dxe4 13. Nxe4 O-O-O 14. c3 {Only 14...Kb8 preserves a double-edged position; the natural moves all fail to the same trap, which wins immediately:} Kb8 (14... Qf7 $2 15. Bxf6 gxf6 16. d5 $16 {[%cal Rh3e6,Rd5c6]}) 15. Qe2 h6 16. Bxf6 gxf6 17. O-O-O f5 18. Nxd6 Rxd6 19. Rhg1 $11 {Black struggles with the weak pawn on e6, and the bishop has great potential once it returns to g2. Black can maintain the balance with computer-like play (starting with 19...Qh4), but there is no question that any normal human would prefer White.}) (6... Nb4 {In contrast to Nb5, this move is nothing more than an empty threat. We easily protect the pawn and send the knight back where it belongs.} 7. Rc1 e6 8. a3 Nc6 9. g4 {The rook on c1 and pawn on a3 change nothing: this shot is as strong as ever.} Nxe5 10. gxf5 Nc6 11. fxe6 fxe6 12. Ne2 $14 {[%cal Rc2c4]}) 7. dxe5 Ne4 {Here, it is important not to capture on d5, as that enables Black to develop quickly and dangerously with 8...e6.} 8. Nxe4 dxe4 (8... Bxe4 9. e6 $1 {Shattering Black's center and carving out a dominant outpost on e5. We see the exact same idea in the 3...c5 line.} fxe6 10. Be5 Qd7 11. f3 Bg6 12. h4 {Grabbing space and inducing even more kingside weaknesses.} h5 13. Qd2 O-O-O 14. a4 $40 {Total domination in the center and an unstoppable attack on the queenside. Our next move is to b2-b4, and after the pawn appears on b5, Black will probably be best advised to resign in order to avoid a total massacre. Also do not forget about the possibility of a rook lift to c3, particularly if Black's queen appears on c6.}) 9. Qd4 $1 {Trading on our own terms!} Qxd4 10. exd4 e6 11. c3 {Solidifying the center.} c5 12. Be3 {An astute positional move, preparing to recapture on d4 with the bishop.} cxd4 13. Bxd4 {It may seem more intuitive to recapture with the pawn, but that would grant Black active prospects for his dark-squared bishop, and subject us to long-term pressure down the d-file. On d4, the bishop lords over the entire position, preventing the active development of Black's DSB and keeping the d-file closed for business. Our next step is to complete our development and get our king to e3, when Black experiences long-term problems with the e4-pawn.} Rc8 14. Be2 Bc5 15. Kd2 Ke7 16. Rhc1 Rhd8 17. Ke3 $14 {White will combine kingside and queenside expansion. Next up is g4 followed by h4, and we can expand on the queenside with a4-a5 as well - that is why we left the rook on a1! Finally, after g4 is played, remember that you can transfer your light-squared bishop via f1 to g2, when Black will likely lose the e4-pawn.}) 6. h3 {Asking the bishop a polite question!} Bxf3 (6... Bh5 7. g4 Bg6 8. Ne5 {This series of moves should be automatic by now! With Black's pawn not yet on e6, the power of the knight on e5 is increased even further.} e6 (8... Nxe5 9. dxe5 Ne4 10. Nxe4 Bxe4 11. f3 Bg6 12. e6 $1 {This move should be automatic as well!} fxe6 13. Be5 $16) 9. h4 h6 (9... Nxe5 10. dxe5 Ne4 (10... d4 {This is the only way to muddy the waters, although White still emerges from the complications with a nearly winning position.} 11. exd4 Nd5 {Now, the intuitive 12.Nxd5 gives Black a nagging initiative, so the silicon fiend unearths a delightful idea that defies belief:} 12. Bg2 $1 {Simply leaving the f4-bishop en prise! Upon closer investigation, the idea of this move should become clear: after Black takes the bishop, we will capture the pawn on f7, when the threat of h4-h5, coupled with the prospect of a nasty check on c6, spells immediate doom for a totally uncoordinated and underdeveloped Black army. Ideas like these are why we love the Jobava.} Nxf4 13. Bxb7 Be7 (13... Bb4 14. Bc6+ Kf8 15. Qf3 (15. h5 {As tempting as it is, this move meets with an incredible refutation that gives Black attacking chances.} Bd3 $3 16. cxd3 Rb8 $13 {[%csl Rd4] The weakness of the d4-pawn grants Black sufficient counterplay.}) 15... Qxd4 16. Bxa8 Qxe5+ 17. Qe3 $18 {Black's attack fizzles out, and White's material advantage will be decisive. Note that the g2-square is controlled by our bishop!}) (13... Rb8 14. Bc6+ Ke7 15. Qd2 Rb6 16. Ba4 Qa8 17. O-O-O Nd5 18. h5 Nxc3 19. Qxc3 $18 {[%cal Rc3c7]}) 14. Bc6+ {An important intermediate move, to prevent castling.} Kf8 15. h5 {Black loses a decisive amount of material. There is one more detail to keep in mind, though:} Bd3 {As in the 13...Bb4 line, taking the bishop leads to unnecessary complications. Instead, we sidestep the bishop and act with surgical precision.} 16. Qf3 $1 {Who cares about the bishop? Black is unable to save the rook and the knight at the same time.} Qxd4 17. Bxa8 Qxe5+ 18. Qe3 $18 {The queen trade is inevitable, after which Black loses even more material.}) 11. Nxe4 Bxe4 12. f3 Bg6 13. h5 $18) 10. Nxg6 fxg6 11. Qd3 Kf7 12. O-O-O $18 {Black's position is in ruins. Note that ...Nb4 is not scary, as our queen simply drops down to e2.}) 7. Qxf3 e6 {Although we have the bishop pair, Black has the twin ideas of ...Bb4 and ...Bd6, both of which threaten to neutralize our initiative. In order to maintain the edge, it is crucial for us to act decisively.} 8. O-O-O Bb4 (8... Bd6 9. g4 {Initiating kingside pressure. It is rather painful to part ways with the bishop pair, but there was nothing we could have done: 9.Bg5 would be futile on account of 9...h6 followed by ...g5.} Bxf4 10. Qxf4 Qd6 (10... Qe7 11. g5 Nh5 (11... Nd7 $2 12. Qxc7 $16) 12. Qg4 g6 {With the knight out of commission, we switch the center of action from the kingside to the queenside.} 13. Na4 b6 (13... O-O 14. Be2 Ng7 15. h4 $40 {Black has no good answer to the straightforward idea of dropping the queen to h3 or g2 and following up with h5, blasting open the h-file.}) 14. c4 $1 {Having induced the dreadfully weakening ...b6, we initiate queenside operations without further delay. It may seem rather brazen to push the c-pawn once we have castled queenside, but Black's pieces are too passive and uncoordinated to present a serious dangerous to our king. Worst comes to worse, the king can always find shelter on b1 or a1. Black's king, on the other hand, has nowhere safe to run.} dxc4 {This natural move loses quickly:} (14... Qb4 15. Nc3 {Black has no obvious continuation: the pawn on d5 is hanging, and his position is on the verge of collapse.} Ne7 16. cxd5 exd5 17. Nxd5 $1 Nxd5 18. Qe4+ $18 {Picking up the knight with interest.}) 15. Bg2 Qd7 16. Qe4 Qd5 17. Qxd5 exd5 18. Bxd5 Kd7 19. Bxc4 $18) 11. Qxd6 cxd6 {Even with the queens off the board, we are able to generate a massive initiative in the center with a series of accurate moves:} 12. g5 Nd7 13. e4 {Breaking through in the center.} dxe4 {Now, the meek 14.Nxe4 would enable Black to repair his center with 14...d5. Instead, our intentions are far more violent.} 14. d5 exd5 15. Rxd5 $16 {Black is in trouble: he will lose either d6 or e4, or both. Whichever pawn we capture first, we will bring out the rest of our pieces and surround the other one, likely with Bg2 and Re1 or Rhd1.}) 9. Ne2 {Simple and powerful! Of course, we cannot allow ...Bxc3. Now, the bishop looks dumb. Later down the line, we also possess the idea of pushing c2-c4, Queen's Gambit-style.} Ne4 (9... Qe7 10. Bg5 {An annoying little move, aiming to fully destroy Black's kingside pawn structure with Bxf6. The bishop pair is meant to be given away!} h6 11. Bxf6 gxf6 (11... Qxf6 12. Qxf6 gxf6 13. Nf4 Ke7 14. g4 $14 {White has a stable plus in the endgame. We will plant the knight on h5, tying Black down to f6, and follow by preparing c2-c4 or even (after Bg2 and Rhf1) f4-f5, accentuating the weakness of the f6-pawn.}) 12. Nf4 {Bringing the knight into position. We now threaten to freeze the f6 pawn with g2-g4, when Black will have no good answer to the threat of Nh5.} f5 13. g4 {This move is just as powerful here as with the pawn on f6. By opening the h-file, we shift our focus from the f-pawn to the h-pawn.} fxg4 14. hxg4 O-O-O 15. Rh5 $16 {Anatoly Karpov would kill for such a position with White! Our next step is to develop the bishop and stack the rooks. Black's ability to defend h6 is limited by the poor coordination of his pieces and by the fact that the knight on c6 has no access to any central squares.}) (9... O-O 10. g4 {Commencing the attack without delay.} Ne4 11. Ng3 (11. h4 {This is also good, and perhaps even more straightforward than 11.Ng3.} Qe7 12. Ng3 Nc3 $1 {A scary-looking tactic, but it is easy to parry.} 13. Rd3 $1 Nxa2+ 14. Kb1 Bc3 15. e4 $1 Nxd4 16. Rxd4 Bxd4 17. Kxa2 $16) 11... g5 {It appears that we have blundered a piece, but this is all part of the plan. In return for the minor piece, we will receive two pawns and, most importantly, fantastic chances to checkmate Black on the kingside.} 12. Bxc7 $1 Qxc7 13. Nxe4 dxe4 14. Qxe4 Rae8 15. h4 $16 {From a practical perspective, Black's position is untenable. The h-file is about to open up, and Black will have no answer to the massive amount of material that we will pile up on the h7-pawn, which will include the bishop and the other rook.}) (9... Bd6 10. g4 {See 8...Bd6 9.g4. Here, the only difference is in the placement of the knight; we believe that it is even better on e2, since ...Bxf4 will always be met with Nxf4.} Ne4 11. Ng3 Nxg3 12. fxg3 $5 $14 {An ingenious positional move, opening up the f-file. Our long-term pressure along the f-file will guarantee White a stable advantage. Also, do not forget about c2-c4, which would come at the least convenient moment for Black.}) 10. Ng3 g5 11. Be5 (11. Nxe4 $5 {This sacrifice deserves serious merit, although it leads to a rather chaotic tactical battle that requires intense study.} dxe4 (11... gxf4 12. Nd2 fxe3 13. fxe3 $14) 12. Qxe4 gxf4 13. d5 f5 $8 14. Qxe6+ Ne7 15. d6 Bxd6 16. Bc4 Rf8 17. e4 fxe4 18. Qxe4 $40 {[%cal Re4h7,Re4b7]}) 11... Nxe5 (11... f6 12. Nxe4 fxe5 13. Qh5+ $18 {Black's king is driven out and massacred.}) 12. dxe5 Nxg3 13. Qxg3 {Visually, Black's position appears more than tenable, but the incredibly weak pawn on g5 tells a different story. We will use that pawn in order to develop enduring pressure down the h-file. Black, on the other hand, has very little going on the other side of the board: our king is perfectly safe on c1.} Qe7 14. h4 {Using the g-pawn as a "hook" to open the h-file, a typical motif worth remembering!} gxh4 15. Rxh4 O-O-O 16. Bd3 Rdg8 17. Qf4 Bc5 18. g3 $14 {Black has a permanent weakness on h7. We will accumulate the pressure with Rdh1, when the pawn's days are likely numbered.}) (4... c5 5. dxc5 {Typically, Black does not have any difficulty winning back the pawn, but this position is a notable exception. If Black plays ...e7-e5 (after 5...Nc6), the d5-pawn becomes very soft, and we maintain our material advantage. If, on the other hand, Black goes after the c-pawn immediately with 5...e6, we cling to it with 6.Na4, forcing Black to give up the valuable dark-squared bishop in order to recover the pawn. In either case, White has a long-lasting advantage, but the lines in the 5...Nc6 line are very sharp and it is worth spending an hour or two to understand the zen of the position.} Nc6 (5... e6 6. Na4 {We ain't gonna give that pawn up easy! It turns out that Black has no way to win back the pawn without yielding the bishop pair, which turns out to be incredibly valuable in this particular structure.} (6. b4 $2 {It is essential to avoid this move, after which our queenside will collapse like a house of cards.} b6 $1 7. cxb6 (7. Rb1 bxc5 8. bxc5 Qa5 $15) 7... Bxb4 8. Nge2 Qxb6 $19) 6... Bxc5 (6... Nbd7 7. b4 {In human terms, Black is already in massive trouble.} b6 (7... a5 8. a3 $16) 8. c6 {This move signals the key difference between 7.b4 and 6.b4: the knight in d7 is now under fire, and after 8...Bxb4+ 9.c3 we attack two minor pieces at the same time. Amazingly, Black can make this line work using an insane sequence of computer moves, but White still maintains an objective advantage at the end. Most people who stumble into this line will have done so unintentionally!} Bxb4+ 9. c3 Ba5 10. cxd7+ Bxd7 {It transpires that Black has serious compensation for the pawn, in the form of pressure on the c3-pawn and the constant possibility of ...Bxa4. We have to proceed with the utmost precision.} 11. Be5 Ne4 12. f3 $1 {Not fearing the check on h4, which is cooly rebuffed by g2-g3. Practically speaking, Black is already in very serious trouble. The engine finds ways to keep Black's initiative going, but it is safe to say that a human will not be able to demonstrate anything resembling sufficient compensation for the piece.} f6 13. Bd4 Nd6 14. Rb1 {Poking and prodding at the b6-pawn. Now, ...b5 yields the c5-square, so Black must defend obediently.} Rb8 15. f4 {Preventing ...e5, and preparing to develop our knight.} O-O 16. Nf3 $14 {We aim to complete our development with Bd3 and 0-0, when Black's compensation will simply evaporate. The engine finds a way to keep the spark of Black's initiative going, but we will bet a silver dollar that this position will never occur in your tournament or online practice :)}) 7. Nxc5 Qa5+ 8. c3 Qxc5 9. Nf3 Nc6 10. Bd3 O-O (10... Qe7 {A rather devious move, threatening to win a piece with ...e5 followed by ...e4.} 11. Ne5 {As in most Jobava positions, this knight sally is incredibly effective. In particular, it underscores the extent to which Black misses his dark-squared bishop in these sorts of positions; it is remarkably difficult to kick our minor pieces away from e5; Black can only do so at the cost of seriously compromising the activity of his own minor pieces.} Nd7 (11... O-O 12. O-O Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Nd7 14. Bg3 e5 15. Bc2 Nf6 16. Bh4 $14 {Turning up the pressure dial on d5. We have a litany of improving moves that we can follow up with, such as a4-a5 (to grab space on the queenside) and Qd3 (to threaten the h7 pawn and coordinate the rooks). If we can induce ...e5-e4, then we will use the d4-square as an outpost for the queen.}) 12. Nxc6 {Taking this knight permanently damages Black's queenside pawn structure.} bxc6 13. Bg3 $1 {Taking the sting out of ...e5.} O-O 14. Qc2 h5 15. Qe2 {[%cal Re2h5]} g6 (15... h4 $2 16. Qh5 $16) 16. h3 Nc5 17. O-O $14 {We are more than happy to enter an opposite-colored bishop middlegame, where our vise-like grip on the dark squares, coupled with the prospect of opening the center at our leisure with e3-e4, guarantees White a pleasant edge. Black, on the other hand, will have a very hard time deploying his light-squared bishop to a reasonably active square.}) 11. O-O b5 12. b4 {Using tactics to halt the progress of Black's pawn. Of course, c3 is untouchable due to Rc1.} Qe7 13. a4 $16 {Prying open the queenside, and creating a serious long-term weakness on a6. Note that in response to 13...bxa4, we first have to withdraw the dark-squared bishop to g3 in order to take the sting out of ...e5. Then, we will recapture on a4 and turn up the queenside pressure.}) (5... Qa5 {A sensible way to recapture the c5-pawn, but it comes at the steep cost of Black's development.} 6. Qd2 Qxc5 (6... e6 7. Nf3 Bxc5 8. Bd3 O-O 9. O-O Nc6 10. a3 Be7 11. b4 Qd8 12. h3 $14 {[%cal Re3e4]}) 7. O-O-O $1 {Capitalizing on our development advantage and applying immediate pressure on the d5-pawn.} e6 (7... Bf5 {Indirectly defending the d5-pawn.} 8. Nf3 Nc6 (8... e6 9. Nh4 {Securing the bishop pair and reopening the possibility of e3-e4.} Bg6 10. Nxg6 hxg6 11. e4 $16) 9. Ne5 {We now transition to a plan of kingside expansion, preparing to underscore the vulnerability of the f5-bishop with f2-f3 followed by g2-g4. Even without the pawn on d4, our knight is perfectly safe on e5, and the bishop that will take its place in the event of ...Nxe5 will pose the serious threat of Bxf6, crippling Black's kingside pawn structure.} e6 10. f3 Bd6 (10... Be7 11. g4 Bg6 12. h4 Nxe5 13. Bxe5 h6 14. Kb1 $16 {Black's king has nowhere to run!}) 11. Nxc6 Bxf4 12. exf4 bxc6 13. g4 {Transitioning to a furious kingside attack! Even without a knight on e5 to guide them forward, the kingside pawns constitute a formidable force. As a result of Black's compromised queenside pawn structure, the Black king has no safe place to hide.} Bg6 14. h4 h6 (14... h5 {This move halts the progress of White's pawns on the kingside...or so it seems. We take the opportunity before Black has castled to smash open the e-file, giving us a dangerous and enduring initiative.} 15. f5 exf5 16. Na4 {Forcing the queen away from c5 and preparing to entrench the knight on b6.} Qd6 17. Nb6 {It may seem that the knight is anything but stable here, but we will use some fascinating tactics to justify its placement!} Rb8 18. g5 {The point is revealed: the knight is untouchable due to the fork on e3!} Nd7 19. Re1+ Kf8 {Just when it seems that our attack has fizzled out, a lovely tactical shot comes to the rescue.} 20. Qh2 $1 {Gorgeous! The queen is taboo due to Nxd7+, and Black is forced to part ways with his extra pawn.} f4 21. Nxd7+ Qxd7 22. Qxf4 $14 {We have recovered our material investment, and stand clearly better. Our pawn structure is superior, and we can continue stoking the coals of attack by bringing our bishop out to h3. Of course, ...Qb7 is not dangerous on account of b3, and Black's "attack" comes to an end.}) 15. Re1 {Threatening f5 and forcing Black to seek shelter on the kingside...where a far scarier enemy awaits him!} O-O 16. h5 Bh7 17. g5 $40 {The attack is already quite devastating.}) 8. e4 {Straightforward and extremely powerful. The pawn is obviously untouchable due to checkmate on d8.} Nc6 9. exd5 exd5 10. Na4 {We have seen this typical move many times in these variations (such as in the 7...Bf5 line, 14...h5 sub-variation). The queen is chased away to a far less desirable square, making it easier for us to exploit the countless weaknesses in Black's position. In particular, we put our knight in position to infiltrate the dreadfully weak b6-square, which spells immediate doom for Black.} Qa7 11. Nf3 Be7 12. Be3 {The beginning of the end.} Qb8 13. Nb6 $18 {Black loses a ton of material.}) 6. a3 {Preparing to reinforce the pawn with b4, and essentially forcing Black's hand.} e5 (6... g6 {I have faced this move several times in blitz (DN), and it makes a good deal of sense: 7.b4 is now quite a bit less desirable, since the bishop on g7 will exert rather nasty pressure on our knight. Instead, we defend the pawn in an altogether different way:} 7. Na4 $1 {This removes the knight from the line of fire of Black's bishop, and threatens Nb6. Note that ...Qa5+ is never dangerous in such positions due to c3 (followed by b4, chasing the queen right back to its initial square).} Nd7 8. b4 {Refraining from the greedy Qxd5, which causes unnecessary complications. With the knight on a safe square, there is nothing to fear from the deployment of Black's bishop to the long diagonal.} Bg7 9. Rc1 {An astute positional move. The rook will support the c2-c4 breakthrough.} e5 (9... b5 10. cxb6 Nxb6 11. c4 $1 $16 {This is why we put the rook on c1!}) 10. Bg3 d4 11. Nf3 O-O 12. c3 {Forcing Black to get rid of the d4-pawn.} dxe3 13. fxe3 e4 14. Nd4 $16 {Black has no compensation for the pawn. Moreover, he can barely move, since ...Nde5 will always run into Nb6. We will follow up by completing our kingside development, when our extra pawn and superior piece placement gives us a near-decisive advantage.}) 7. Bg5 {Here, and only here! The lackluster 7.Bg3 enables Black to recover the pawn without consequences.} Be6 (7... d4 {This combative move does not solve Black's problems, but it leads to a good deal of tactical complexity, so it is worth looking over the following moves very carefully.} 8. Na4 {We refrain from capturing on d4, which enables Black to entrench his knight on a powerful central square.} Be6 (8... Qa5+ $2 $18 {As always, this move is innocuous and merely hastens Black's demise.} 9. c3 Ne4 (9... dxc3 10. b4 {A crucial detail that justifies the entire line! We shut down Black's queenside counterplay, and prepare to recapture the pawn on c3 with a dominant position. The piece sac on b4 leads nowhere:} Nxb4 11. axb4 Qxb4 12. Qc2 {Simple and cold-blooded, preventing Black's only idea.} Bxc5 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Bd3 $18 {The c3-pawn is not going anywhere! First, we will complete our kingside development, and then capture the pawn at our leisure. Black's position is resignable.}) 10. exd4 $1 {Fearless and incredibly powerful. The piece sacrifice is temporary; we will use our control over the b6-square and the dreadful placement of Black's queen to gain the material back with interest.} Nxg5 11. Nb6 {First, the knight is placed on b6 to slice off the queen's only evacuation route. We now threaten b2-b4, which will trap Black's queen.} Ne4 (11... Rb8 12. b4 $18) 12. b4 {Anyway! This is a lot better than the greedy 12.Nxa8, which allows Black to seriously complicate affairs with a nasty capture on c3. In order to save the queen, Black must part ways with his extra piece.} Nxc3 13. Qb3 Nxb4 14. Qxb4 Qxb4 15. axb4 Rb8 16. d5 $18 {The pawn phalanx is totally overwhelming. We can continue by smoking out Black's knight with Ne2, and we can respond to ...Be7 with d5-d6.}) (8... Bf5 {This nondescript developing move accomplishes nothing, and enables us to attain an ideal setup on the queenside.} 9. Nb6 Rb8 10. b4 h6 11. Bxf6 Qxf6 12. Nf3 Rd8 13. e4 $1 {Shutting down Black's counterplay on the d-file.} Bxe4 14. Bd3 Bxf3 15. Qxf3 Qxf3 16. gxf3 {Black is borderline lost due to our immense control over key light squares, including e4 and c4. Black also struggles with a total lack of coordination and an inability to find a suitable square for the dark-squared bishop.} Be7 17. Nc4 O-O 18. Be4 f6 19. O-O-O $18 {[%cal Rf3f4] f3-f4 is coming sooner rather than later.}) 9. exd4 Nxd4 (9... Qxd4 10. Qxd4 Nxd4 11. Bd3 Bd5 12. f3 Nd7 13. b4 a5 14. Nc3 Be6 15. Rb1 b6 16. cxb6 Nxb6 17. Nge2 $14) (9... exd4 $2 10. Nb6 Ra7 11. b4 h6 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. Bd3 $18) 10. Nb6 Bxc5 11. Nxa8 Qxa8 12. Bxf6 gxf6 13. Nf3 Qc8 14. Nd2 {[%cal Rd2e4]} Bf5 15. Bd3 Rg8 16. Ne4 Bxe4 (16... Qc6 17. Nxc5 Qxg2 18. Kd2 b6 19. Bxf5 Qg5+ 20. Kc3 Qxf5 21. Qd3 $1 Qc8 22. Rag1 $18) (16... Rxg2 17. c3 $1 Bxe4 (17... Nb5 18. Nxc5 Qxc5 19. Qf3 {[%cal Rf3f5,Rf3g2]}) 18. Bxe4 Rg4 19. f3 Rg2 20. cxd4 Bxd4 21. Rc1 $18) 17. Bxe4 f5 18. Bd5 $14) (7... Bxc5 8. Bxf6 {Of course, this is the point of deploying the bishop to g5. Black loses the d5-pawn without compensation.} Qxf6 9. Qxd5 Bb6 10. Qf3 $16 {Withdrawing the queen from the center and offering a queen trade. Black has absolutely nothing in return for the pawn.}) 8. b4 Be7 (8... h6 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Na4 {Standard fare, deploying the knight to the beautiful b6-square.} Qc7 11. Nb6 Rd8 12. c3 {Halting the progress of Black's d-pawn. Despite the somewhat chaotic apperance of the position, Black has no active plan and no compensation for the pawn.} f5 (12... d4 {is futile on account of} 13. Qc2 $16) 13. Bd3 f4 14. Bc2 {Taking the sting out of ...e4, which will no longer come with tempo.} fxe3 15. fxe3 Rg8 16. g3 {Calmly defending g2.} Rg4 17. Ne2 d4 18. e4 $1 {Keeping things tight in the center!} dxc3 19. Nd5 $18 {A devastating final resource, underscoring the potency of the knight. Trading on e5 hands over a decisive pawn phalanx in the center, while 19...Qc8 enables us to recapture the c3-pawn with the other knight, when its colleague on d5 will be unassailable. Black is objectively lost.}) (8... d4 9. exd4 Nxd4 (9... exd4 10. Na4 a5 11. Bb5 axb4 12. axb4 Be7 13. Nf3 O-O 14. O-O Nxb4 15. Nxd4 $16) 10. Nf3 $16) 9. Nf3 O-O 10. Be2 {White is now up a pawn for no compensation, but there is one more detail worth mentioning.} Qe8 $5 {[%cal Rf6e4] A devious resource that was employed against DN in a recent speedrun game. Now, 11.0-0 (as played in the game) runs into 11...Ne4, and Black wins material. As usual, our top priority is to lead our c3-knight to the promised land.} 11. Na4 Rd8 12. O-O d4 13. Qc1 $14 {Cold-blooded and powerful. Black is out of active ideas, and we can improve our position in a litany of ways, ranging from Nb6 to c2-c3 to Rf1-d1.}) (4... b5 {This has been the choice of many strong GMs; in particular, Belarussian GM Sergei Zhigalko has played this line regularly against the authors of this course. Black aims to rapidly deploy the bishop to b7, making it virtually impossible for White to break in the center with e3-e4. We spent a long time on this move, and are proud to recommend a rare idea that confronts Black with serious practical problems.} 5. a3 {Preventing the further progress of Black's b-pawn, and setting up the main idea of the line.} Bb7 6. Nf3 e6 7. b4 {This is a rare, but extremely dangerous idea. We freeze Black's queenside play entirely, preventing ...c7-c5. Without this idea, Black is essentially stalemated. We, on the other, have clear ways to improve our position: the knight will be centralized on e5, the bishop developed to d3, and once Black castles kingside, our queen is immediately transported to h3, creating long-lasting and potentially fatal threats on the kingside. Do not be fooled by the engine's assessment: our practice and analysis reveals that Black's position is very hard to play due to the conspicuous absence of a clear active plan.} Nbd7 (7... Bd6 8. Ne5 O-O 9. Bd3 Nbd7 {See 7...Nbd7 8.Ne5 0-0 9.Bd3 Bd6.}) 8. Bd3 Bd6 (8... Ne4 {This rare move presents a serious challenge to White's setup. There is no question that Black equalizes, but we can definitely pose some serious problems with accurate play.} 9. Bxe4 dxe4 10. Nd2 Nf6 (10... f5 11. Nb3 Bd6 12. Na5 Bd5 13. Qh5+ g6 14. Qh6 Bf8 15. Qh3 Bd6 16. Qg3 Nb6) 11. O-O Be7 12. Bg5 $16) 9. Ne5 {The knight on e5 is completely unassailable: to get rid of it, Black must either part ways with a minor piece or must hand over the bishop pair to White. We see this exact type of setup in many lines. Note the power of the pawn on b4: without the possibility of ...c7-c5, Black has no good way to assail our dominance in the center.} O-O (9... Ne4 {An inventive way to prevent Qf3, but this enables us to change the pawn structure in our favor.} 10. Bxe4 dxe4 11. Qg4 {Asking Black a rather nasty question. Castling fails tactically, while ...g6 creates long-term weaknesses on the kingside. Black can maintain objective equality with a host of computer moves, but no one in their right mind would prefer Black's position here!} Qf6 {Trying to force pieces off the board fails tactically.} (11... O-O $2 12. Bh6 $16 {Black loses the exchange, since 12...Qf6 drops the knight (and then the rook).}) (11... Kf8 12. h4 $36 {A great example of a position where the computer evaluation is utterly useless. The computer gives 0.00, but we strongly believe that White's position is incomparably preferable. We will expand on the kingside with h4-h5, and can follow up by dropping the queen to h3 and advancing the g-pawn. Black has no active plan.}) (11... g6 $5 12. h4 Nf6 13. Qe2 {[%cal Rc3b5]} Nd7 (13... h6 14. Nxb5 $1 $16) 14. Bg5 Be7 (14... f6 15. Nxd7 fxg5 (15... Kxd7 16. Bf4 Bxf4 17. exf4 f5 18. a4 $36) 16. Nc5 Bxc5 17. bxc5 gxh4 18. Qg4 $16) 15. Nxd7 Qxd7 16. Bxe7 Qxe7 17. Qg4 f5 18. Qg5 $14) 12. Nxd7 Kxd7 13. Bxd6 cxd6 14. Nxe4 Qg6 15. Qxg6 hxg6 16. f3 {A crucial resource, supporting the knight and keeping our extra pawn.} Bxe4 17. fxe4 Rh4 {Just when it appears that Black is out of the woods, we open a second front.} 18. a4 $1 {Perfect timing; this move works to perfection with the rook on a8 now undefended.} Rxe4 19. axb5 Rxe3+ 20. Kd2 Re4 21. Kd3 $18 {Defending the d4-pawn with tempo. We will capture on a6 on the next move, when the connected passers should swiftly decide the outcome of the game.}) 10. Qf3 {A classic maneuver: the queen is ferried over to h3, where it exerts long-term pressure against the h7-pawn. Without the possibility of ...c7-c5, Black is consigned to total passivity and must defend very precisely merely to avoid getting checkmated in the next few moves.} Nb6 11. Qh3 {The ideal setup is now complete. We are exerting massive pressure on h7, and intend to follow up with the prosaic g4-g5.} Ne4 (11... g6 {This creates massive holes on the kingside, which we can exploit by repositioning our bishop to a more active square.} 12. Bg5 Be7 13. Qh4 $16) (11... h6 {Of course, this merely throws more oil into the fire.} 12. g4 Ne4 13. g5 $1 {Ignoring the hanging knight on c3, which is non-essential to our attack. Unsurprisingly, given the superiority of our pieces, the tactics work out perfectly for White.} Nxg5 (13... Nxc3 14. Rg1 {It is important to appreciate the value of these types of moves. Even though White is down a piece, we are in no big hurry, and it is worth bringing in the big boys to ensure that the attack is decisive.} Ne4 15. gxh6 g6 16. Bxe4 {Eliminating Black's only active defender.} dxe4 17. h7+ Kh8 18. Rxg6 $1 {Smashing through Black's pitiful pawn shell.} fxg6 19. Nxg6+ Kg7 20. Qh6+ Kf7 21. Qh5 $1 {Another important quiet move, setting up the threat of a discovered check. The simplest threat is Nxf8+ followed by h8=Q. Black's king is forced back into the ring of fire.} Kg7 22. Nxf8 Qxf8 23. Bh6+ {And now the coup d'grace, skewering the queen!} Kh8 24. Bxf8 Rxf8 25. O-O-O $18 {[%cal Rd1g1,Rg1g8] Nominally, Black has a lot of material for the queen, but the minor pieces can only spectate as we get our rook to g1 and follow up with Qg4/g5/g6, setting up unstoppable threats along the g-file.}) 14. Bxg5 Qxg5 15. f4 {Forcing the queen off of the g-file and lending further support to the knight, taking the sting out of ...Bxe5.} Qe7 16. Rg1 Kh8 17. O-O-O {The point of this move is not to shuttle the king to safety; it was perfectly safe in the center. Rather, by connecting the rooks, we are setting up the unstoppable threat of a sacrifice on g7!} a5 (17... Rg8 18. Rg6 $1 Qf8 {Now, a beautiful sequence decides the game:} 19. Nxf7+ $1 Qxf7 20. Rxh6+ $1 gxh6 21. Qxh6+ Qh7 22. Qxh7#) 18. Rxg7 $3 Kxg7 19. Rg1+ Kh8 20. Qxh6# {This line demonstrates the incredibly potency of White's construction.}) 12. f3 $1 {Get out!} Nf6 (12... Nxc3 13. Qxh7#) 13. Bg5 g6 14. Qh6 Re8 15. O-O Be7 16. f4 $18 {[%cal Rf1f3,Rf3h3] The rook lift to h3 will lead to immediate checkmate. The extent of Black's queenside paralysis is rather picturesque.}) 5. a3 {An important inclusion. By preventing ...Bb4, we are making it difficult for Black to find active prospects for the dark-squared bishop. More importantly, we are preparing to take control of the queenside with a well-timed b2-b4, which will make it exceedingly difficult for Black to attain much-needed activity on the queenside.} c5 (5... b5 6. b4 {This very likely transposes to 4...b5 5.a3 Bb7 6.b4 e6. Of course, Black can also play this line without fianchettoing the light-squared bishop, but that hardly changes the general tenor of the game. White's plan will remain the same: to entrench a knight on e5, develop the light-squared bishop to d3, and light the kingside on fire by maneuvering the queen to h3.} Bd6 (6... Bb7 7. Nf3 {See 4...b5 5.a3 Bb7 6.b4 e6 7.Nf3.}) 7. Nf3 O-O 8. Bd3 Nbd7 9. Ne5 Nb6 10. Qf3 Bb7 (10... Nfd7 11. Qg3 Nxe5 12. dxe5 Be7 13. Bh6 g6 14. h4 $40 {In these types of positions, you almost never want to capture the rook on f8 because the bishop is such a valuable attacker. White is dominating on the kingside, but we do not even have to rush with h4-h5: an even more powerful continuation in response to 14...Kh8 would be to shuttle the knight around to d4 via e2.}) 11. Qh3 {Amazingly, we have transposed to a position that arises in the 4...b5 line! White's attack is virtually unstoppable.}) 6. Nf3 {For now, we keep the tension, awaiting a more opportune moment to trade on c5.} b5 {Black continues to expand on the queenside.} (6... Nc6 7. h3 {It is important to throw this move in to discourage ...Nh5.} Bd6 (7... b5 8. dxc5 {We cannot hold off on this move any longer, since Black was ready to play ...c4 or ...b4. We now proceed to quickly prepare the e4 pawn break.} Bxc5 9. Bd3 {Importantly, the knight on c6 hinders the development of Black's bishop, and makes it impossible for him to stop e4.} Bb7 10. O-O {Before pushing the e-pawn, we complete our deverlopment.} O-O 11. e4 {The time has come! Our pieces are perfectly placed to deal with the consequences of this breakthrough.} dxe4 {The most common response, resolving the central tension straightaway.} (11... d4 12. Nb1 {A very important idea; this is almost always the desirable response to ...d4. The knight is re-routed to d2, and it can later find a home on b3.} Rc8 13. Nbd2 h6 14. Nb3 Bb6 15. e5 {Staking a claim in the center and popping open the b1-h7 diagonal.} Nd5 16. Bh2 $14 {White is better for several reasons: 1) The pawn on d4 is a long-term weakness that cannot be easily eradicated; 2) We will proceed with Qe2-e4, a nasty idea that will induce a serious kingside weakness; 3) We have many auxiliary ways to improve our position, such as Rfd1 (after Qe2), Nb3-d2-e4-d6, and a timely a3-a4 can also be employed to underscore the hidden vulnerability of Black's queenside structure. In contrast, Black has no clear way to improve his position or cause problems on either side of the board.}) (11... h6 12. exd5 {This is quite a bit better than the tempting 12.e5, when Black drops the knight back to d7 and reopens the kingside with the nasty ...f7-f6.} Nxd5 13. Nxd5 Qxd5 14. Re1 {Moving the rook to a better spot and setting up the positional threat of Be4, which will dislodge Black's queen from its active post.} Rad8 (14... Nd4 $4 {This move looks scary, but it simply loses a piece:} 15. Be4 Nxf3+ 16. Qxf3 $18) 15. Be4 Qh5 (15... Qxd1 16. Raxd1 Rxd1 17. Rxd1 Rd8 18. Rxd8+ Nxd8 19. Bd3 {White has definite chances for an advantage: the idea is to expand on the queenside with b2-b4 and c2-c4, ultimately creating an outside passer. Of course, we do not fear ...Bxf3, which hands over the bishop pair and creates a long-term weakness on the light squares.}) 16. Qe2 Rfe8 {This position occurred in Bortnyk-Baryshpolets, Nabokov Memorial 2015.} 17. c3 {This is best, leading to a small edge. White controls the crucial d4-square.} e5 18. Be3 Bxe3 19. Qxe3 $14 {Our dark-square control, coupled with the nasty threat of Qb6, secures a very pleasant game for White. Black's queen on h5 is also somewhat out of commission.})) (7... cxd4 $6 8. exd4 {The position resembles a favorable version of an exchange Caro, with Black's light-squared bishop locked behind his pawns.}) 8. dxc5 {This forces Black to make another move with his dark-squared bishop, thereby losing a tempo.} Bxc5 (8... Bxf4 $6 {This is a very common response, but Black will be unable to easily recover the pawn.} 9. exf4 Qc7 (9... Qe7 10. b4 e5 11. Nxd5 Nxd5 12. Qxd5 exf4+ 13. Be2 O-O 14. Qd6 $1 Qe4 15. Qd3 Qe7 16. O-O Rd8 17. Qc4 $16) 10. Qd2 $14 {There is no way to win the c5-pawn, which can be supported with b2-b4 as necessary. Our queen can also park itself on e3.}) 9. Bd3 O-O (9... Bd6 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. O-O e5 12. e4 d4 13. Ne2 (13. Nd5 $5 {An innovative alternative, although this is quite unnecessary, since 13.Ne2 transposes into a line that leads to a very dangerous position for Black.} Nxd5 14. exd5 Qxd5 15. Re1 O-O (15... f6 16. c3 O-O 17. Be4 Qd6 18. Qd3 $14) 16. Be4 Qb5 17. Bxc6 bxc6 18. Rxe5 Qxb2 19. Qxd4 Qxd4 20. Nxd4 $40) 13... O-O 14. Ng3 {Transposes to 10...Bd6 11.Bxd6 Qxd6 12.e4 etc.}) 10. O-O {We see a virtually identical position in the 7...b5 line; the placement of the pawn on b7 does not change the nature of the position. We are ready to push e3-e4, posing Black with serious positional problems.} h6 (10... Bd6 11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. e4 d4 (12... dxe4 13. Nxe4 Nxe4 14. Bxe4 Qc7 (14... Rd8 $2 15. Qxd6 Rxd6 16. Rfd1 Rxd1+ 17. Rxd1 Kf8 18. Bxc6 bxc6 19. Ne5 c5 20. Rd8+ Ke7 21. Rg8 g6 22. Rh8 h5 23. f4 $18) 15. Qe2 $14) 13. Ne2 e5 14. Ng3 Be6 15. Qd2 {This position epitomizes the power of the Jobava London, especially with respect to its ability to puzzle even the strongest engines. Stockfish 15, upon reflection, gives equality with a very small preference to Black. But if you look at White's online results, they are staggering (I myself have won almost all my games, including a crushing win over Nepomniatchtchi - O.B.). This is because White has a very clear plan: to maneuver the f3-knight to h4 (and later to f5) and to blast open the f-file with f2-f4. Black, on the other hand, does not have any clear plan of action on the queenside, since the b4-square is guarded and the c2-pawn is well-defended. If this explanation doesn't convince you, perhaps the subsequent analysis will!} h6 16. Nh4 Ne7 17. f4 $14 exf4 18. Rxf4 Nd7 19. Raf1 g5 (19... Ne5 20. Nh5 Rac8 21. Rf6 N5g6 22. Nf5 $18 {Bortnyk-Nepomniatchtchi, Titled Tuesday (blitz) 2022.}) 20. Nhf5 $16 Nxf5 21. Nxf5 Bxf5 22. Rxf5 $18) 11. e4 d4 (11... Re8 12. e5 Nd7 13. Qd2 Bf8 14. Rfe1 $14) (11... dxe4 12. Nxe4 Nxe4 13. Bxe4 Qf6 (13... Qxd1 14. Rfxd1 $16 {[%cal Rf4d6]}) 14. Qc1 e5 15. Bg3 Re8 16. Re1 $14 {[%csl Re5][%cal Re4c6,Rf3e5]}) 12. Ne2 Qe7 13. e5 Nd5 14. Re1 $16 {Black is in a vise-like grip. The bishop can be maneuvered to h4, and White can follow up by amputating the d5-knight with Qd2 and Nf4.}) (6... cxd4 7. exd4 Bd6 8. Bxd6 Qxd6 9. Bd3 O-O 10. O-O Nc6 11. Re1 {White has a small but stable advantage, much akin to the Exchange Caro where Black has closed in his bishop with a premature ...e6. We will inevitably ensconce on our knight on e5 and follow up by building up the kingside pressure.}) (6... Nh5 $6 {This is somewhat premature, since the bishop has a suitable escape square on g5.} 7. Bg5 Be7 8. Bxe7 Qxe7 {With the dark-squared bishop gone, it is a perfect opportunity to resolve the tension in the center and launch a furious kingside offensive.} 9. dxc5 Qxc5 10. g4 $1 Nf6 11. g5 $1 Nfd7 12. e4 $16 {Blasting open the center, after which our knight will sink its teeth into the juicy d6-square!}) 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. Bd3 {We follow up with the typical developing move to d3, preparing e3-e4.} Bb7 {Without question, Black's setup constitues one of the more problematic lines of play against the Jobava London. Black makes it very difficult for us to open up the center with e3-e4 (in contrast to lines with an early ...Nc6, when it is quite a bit easier to carry out this break) while developing his minor pieces to very active posts. In order to avoid getting squeezed, we must insist on pushing e4 as quickly as possible.} 9. Qe2 Nbd7 (9... Nc6 {This move somewhat contradicts the purpose of Black's overall setup, which is to discourage e3-e4. Still, it is a natural developing move and must be taken seriously.} 10. O-O O-O 11. e4 d4 12. Nb1 (12. e5 $2 {This is tempting, and it works on certain occasions (usually with the queen on d1), but here it fails due to the unfortunate placement of White's queen:} dxc3 13. exf6 Qxf6 14. Bg5 Nd4 $1 $17 {White's queen trap falls apart.}) 12... Nh5 13. Bg5 f6 14. Bc1 {It appears that our pieces are all moving backwards, but we have in fact provoked serious, potentially decisive, weaknesses on both sides of the board. We currently threaten to trap the knight with g2-g4, forcing Black to overextend himself even further.} e5 15. a4 {Poking at the weak b5-pawn, and opening up the c4-square for our bishop.} b4 16. Ng5 {Let the party begin!} fxg5 (16... Nf4 17. Bxf4 exf4 18. Bc4+ Kh8 19. Nxh7 $18) 17. Qxh5 h6 {It turns out that this ultra-natural move is a mistake, but find us a human player who will refrain from this move!} 18. h4 $1 gxh4 19. Bxh6 $1 gxh6 20. Qg6+ Kh8 21. Qxh6+ Kg8 22. Bc4+ Rf7 23. Qg6+ $18) (9... O-O 10. e4 {Now, the top computer move is 10...e5, which we refuse to take seriously because no one in their right mind would ever chance upon such a move. Barring that, the position is equal objectively, but definitely easier to play for White.} dxe4 (10... d4 11. Nb1 Nc6 {Now, 12.0-0 transposes into a position that we reach via 9...Nc6 10.0-0 0-0 11.e4 d4 12.Nb1, but we have a superior move:} 12. e5 Nd5 13. Qe4 g6 14. Bh6 Re8 15. Nbd2 $14 {with excellent kingside prospects connected to the idea of opening the h-file with h2-h4.}) (10... e5 11. Bxe5 Nbd7 12. Bxf6 Nxf6 13. e5 Re8 14. O-O {Black doesn't recover the pawn easily, and must continue to make computer moves.} Ng4 $2 15. h3 $1 Nxe5 16. Nxe5 Qc7 17. Qh5 g6 18. Nxd5 $3 Qxe5 (18... Bxd5 19. Nxg6 hxg6 20. Qxd5 $16) 19. Qxe5 Rxe5 20. Nf6+ Kg7 21. Nd7 Rd5 22. Nxc5 Rxc5 23. Rfd1 $16) 11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Bxe4 Bxe4 13. Qxe4 Nd7 14. O-O {Transposes to 9...Nbd7 10.e4, etc.}) 10. e4 {Despite Black's best efforts, we have succeeded in pushing e4, leading virtually by force to a series of piece exchanges in the center. The resulting position may appear rather lifeless, but upon closer inspection, it turns out that White certainly has the easier practical game.} dxe4 11. Nxe4 Nxe4 12. Bxe4 Bxe4 13. Qxe4 {The position has simplified considerably, and the symmetrical material may lead you to believe that the position is devoid of serious winning chances. That could not be further from the truth! Black's queenside is rather weak, and the pawn on b5 is more of a liability than an asset. White's play is ultra straightforward: we will castle and quickly position our rooks on d1 and e1, when White's superior pieces grant us easy practical play.} O-O 14. O-O Nf6 15. Qe2 Qd5 16. Rfe1 Rad8 17. h3 h6 18. Be5 Nd7 19. Rad1 {White has an indisputable initiative, with control over the center and a better-placed bishop. Black must continue to make precise moves to keep equality.} *

